\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  appears divided after hearing arguments on emergency  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,196 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 21:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 24\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal Emtala law has become focal point in heated debate over emergency abortions in states that ban procedure\par \par \uc2 For the second time in a month, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  on Wednesday heard arguments in a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights case, the second to reach the justices since they overturned Roe v Wade two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s case involves a 1986 federal law called the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, or Emtala, which requires hospitals that receive federal dollars to stabilize the health of patients who show up at their emergency rooms with medical emergencies. The Biden administration has sued the state of Idaho, arguing that its ban clashes with Emtala because Idaho only permits abortions in medical emergencies if a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk \u8211\'20\'13 a higher threshold than Emtala.\par \par \uc2 Tensions flared at multiple moments during the two-hour hearing. The court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices \u8211\'20\'13 all of whom are women \u8211\'20\'13 spent several minutes emphasizing the medical and legal dilemmas unleashed by bans like Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s, while conservative justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  raised an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  theory that would ultimately lead to the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  writ large. By the end of the hearing, conservative justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  appeared to be the most skeptical of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s arguments, but the court seemed largely divided along ideological lines.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , the US solicitor general, emphasized the danger now facing women who show up in crisis at Idaho emergency rooms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf a woman comes to an emergency room facing a grave threat to her health, but she isn\u8217\'20\'19t yet facing death, doctors either have to delay treatment and allow her condition to materially deteriorate, or they\u8217\'20\'19re airlifting her out of the state so she can get the emergency care that she needs,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar said. \u8220\'20\'1COne hospital system in Idaho says that, right now, it\u8217\'20\'19s having to transfer pregnant woman in medical crisis out of the state about once every other week. That\u8217\'20\'19s untenable and Emtala does not countenance it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The case is a test of one of the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s chief efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Shortly after the supreme court overturned Roe and allowed states to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the administration issued guidance clarifying that Emtala requires hospitals everywhere to perform abortions if patients need them in emergencies.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge initially sided with the Biden administration, stopping Idaho from enforcing the parts of the ban that conflicted with Emtala. But the supreme court in January issued an order allowing Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s full \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Idaho, which is also represented in the case by the powerful Christian law firm the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , has argued in court papers that its ban does not conflict with Emtala, in part because Emtala does not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cannot compel doctors to offer care that is illegal under state law.\par \par \uc2 Liberal justices \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  hammered Joshua N Turner, a lawyer for Idaho, on when and if doctors could intervene in cases where a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health is at risk but her life is not yet threatened. At one point, Sotomayor read off a list of cases of women whose healthcare was delayed due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including the story of a woman whose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was delayed to the point that she lost her pregnancy and then had to get a hysterectomy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re telling me the doctor there couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have done the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier?\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor asked Turner.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt goes back to whether a doctor can in good faith medical judgment \u8211\'20\'13 \u8221\'20\'1D Turner started to say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a lot for the doctor to risk,\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor interrupted. A provider who violates Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban could face up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Coney Barrett, the sole woman among the conservative majority, seemed taken aback by Turner\u8217\'20\'19s claim that doctor could be prosecuted for performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health. While Turner insisted that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s state legislature did not mean for its ban to apply to doctors acting in good faith to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life, Coney Barrett asked: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if a prosecutor thought differently?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat, Your Honor, is the nature of prosecutorial discretion,\u8221\'20\'1D Turner replied. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it may result in a case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Idaho is one of seven states with laws on the books that require a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life to be at risk before an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be performed.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has also argued that Emtala requires providers to treat an \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn child\u8221\'20\'1D. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , a diehard conservative, brought up that phrase, indicating that Emtala is meant to imply that doctors have two equal patients when a pregnant woman shows up at an ER: the woman and her fetus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems that the plain meaning is that the hospital must try to eliminate any immediate threat to the child, but performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is antithetical to that duty,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito said.\par \par \uc2 Prelogar insisted that phrase, added to Emtala in 1989, was meant to ensure that hospitals treated women whose fetuses were in crisis while they were not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe statute did nothing to displace the woman herself as an individual with an emergency medical condition,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar replied. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd in many of the cases you\u8217\'20\'19re thinking about, there is no possible way to stabilize the unborn child because the fetus is sufficiently before viability that it\u8217\'20\'19s inevitable that the pregnancy is going to be lost.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The pair went back and forth for several minutes before Alito added: \u8220\'20\'1CNobody is suggesting that a woman isn\u8217\'20\'19t an individual, that she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t deserve stabilization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the exchange evoked the specter of fetal personhood, a priority of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that aims to endow fetuses with full legal rights and protections \u8211\'20\'13 even if those rights conflict with those of the woman carrying the fetus. Enshrining fetal personhood across the US \u8211\'20\'13 which is also a goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement \u8211\'20\'13 would dramatically rewrite vast swaths of US law and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely.\par \par \uc2 The consequences for abortions in medical emergencies, like those at issue in the Wednesday arguments, are unclear; although anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have spent years pursuing fetal personhood, the end of Roe has forced them \u8211\'20\'13 and the rest of the nation \u8211\'20\'13 to reckon for the first time with its real-world consequences.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing comes just weeks after the court heard arguments in another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case, which involved the availability of a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill and the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ability to regulate it. Lawyers for the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  also argued that case on behalf of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists. Both arguments have significant implications for the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s power to regulate healthcare.\par \par \uc2 If anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states prevail, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou\u8217\'20\'19re basically deconstructing the entire way that we manage health nationally in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gerson Smoger, chair of the \uc2 Physicians for Human Rights\uc2  board of directors anda co-author of an amicus brief in the Idaho case. \u8220\'20\'1CThere has to be some baseline that we have, nationally.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rulings in both the Emtala and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill cases are expected in June.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240424ek4o0018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
