\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-led house repeals strict  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein \par \uc2 6,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 20:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This live blog is now closed. For the latest on Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s harsh \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, you can read our full report:Arizona house votes to repeal near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 The supreme court heard from both sides in a dispute between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66291f198f08e53db06877c5#block-66291f198f08e53db06877c5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Biden administration}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-662912468f08e53db06876fb#block-662912468f08e53db06876fb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho}}}\uc2  over whether federal law requires doctors in the state, where a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is in effect, to perform the procedure on women in emergency circumstances. The court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-6629243f8f087a8ea2b790ce#block-6629243f8f087a8ea2b790ce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were skeptical}}}\uc2  towards Republican-governed Idaho, as were some members of the conservative majority, but it is unclear if they will ultimately find against the state. A decision is expected in June, which could again raise the public\u8217\'20\'19s awareness of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66290fb28f08415dae0dde4f#block-66290fb28f08415dae0dde4f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the impacts}}}\uc2  of Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19s downfall, just months before the November presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s house{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66295d1e8f0881afefd55a7f#block-66295d1e8f0881afefd55a7f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed legislation}}}\uc2  to repeal its near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, after three Republicans joined with all Democrats in the chamber. It now heads to the state senate, where it is set to be voted on next week.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-662937fc8f08b7ccbb88c205#block-662937fc8f08b7ccbb88c205"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  the $95b foreign aid bill passed by the Senate yesterday into law. It approves further military assistance for Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, and threatens social media app TikTok with a nationwide ban unless its Chinese owner sells it in a year.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The White House\u8217\'20\'19s campaign against junk fees continued, with the transportation department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66294a5c8f087a8ea2b792d5#block-66294a5c8f087a8ea2b792d5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debuting}}}\uc2  new regulations against hidden charges, and requirements that passengers receive automatic refunds in certain cases.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The defense department{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66293c1e8f0881afefd55949#block-66293c1e8f0881afefd55949"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  a $1b weapons shipment to Ukraine shortly after the aid bill was signed that president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said would help them defend against Russia\u8217\'20\'19s attacks.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 TikTok{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66293f248f087a8ea2b79249#block-66293f248f087a8ea2b79249"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is in trouble}}}\uc2  in the US. The just-signed foreign aid bill requires its owner, Chinese firm ByteDance, to sell the popular social media app in a year, or be banned nationwide.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/arizona-abortion-1864-ban-repeal-lawmakers-84b0cd9a8c44ddaefed2c45e9eff1c79"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  that the Arizona house finally passed a bill repealing its near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after three Republicans voted to advance the measure alongside all 29 Democrats in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Previous attempts to pass the bill had failed for a lack of votes by Republicans, who hold a narrow majority in the house.\par \par \uc2 The divide in the Senate is similar small, with Republicans in the majority with 16 seats, and Democrats with 14.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s state senate Democrats say the bill repealing the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban will be voted on 1 May:\par \par \uc2 enltrUPDATE: Senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AnnaHernandezAZ?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AnnaHernandezAZ}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s SB1734, the bill to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/repealtheban?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #repealtheban}}}\uc2  went through a second read in the AZ Senate and is ready to be voted on the next legislative day (May 1)!\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Arizona Senate Democrats (@AZSenateDems) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AZSenateDems/status/1783209898500895201?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 24, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Arizona house repeals strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, state senate must still approve\par \par \uc2 Arizona lawmakers took a step towards repealing the strict, century-and-a-half old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that was set to go into effect due to a court decision earlier this month, Reuters reports, with the state house just now voting to repeal the law. However, the state senate must approve the repeal before it comes into effect.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates who are hoping to win one of the swing state\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat in November and its electoral votes for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had denounced the revived near-total ban and asked the state legislature to repeal it, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/17/arizona-republicans-block-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous efforts}}}\uc2  fell short in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on the saga:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-against-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona Republicans denounce revived 1864 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban in sudden reversal}}}\par \par \uc2 This post has been corrected to say the ban was set to go into effect, and was not currently in effect.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court appears split as arguments over emergency abortions conclude\par \par \uc2 When it heard oral arguments earlier today over whether a federal law known as Emtala requires Idaho, a state with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, to allow the procedure in emergencies, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s justices appeared split into three camps.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s three-justice liberal minority was sympathetic to the Biden administration, which has championed reproductive health access and sued Idaho over its law. Of the court\u8217\'20\'19s six-justice conservative supermajority, three \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 seemed partial to Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s insistence that it had the authority to dramatically narrow when doctors may perform abortions, even during emergencies.\par \par \uc2 The other three conservative justices \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 asked questions that indicated they may at least be willing to consider the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s positions. The latter two justices had voted in 2022 to overturn Roe v Wade, setting the stage for Republican-led states to ban or restrict the procedure.\par \par \uc2 From the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/carter-sherman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Carter Sherman}}}\uc2 , here\u8217\'20\'19s a recap of arguments in the case, which will likely be decided in June \u8211\'20\'13 less than five months before a presidential election where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is expected to be a key issue for voters:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court appears divided after hearing arguments on emergency \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  care}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Progressive group Indivisible hit out at the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives after they earlier today heard oral arguments in a dispute between the White House and Idaho over whether the conservative state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban can prevent doctors from performing the procedure in an emergency.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration argued that federal law requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be performed in emergencies, an argument that the court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices appeared to agree with. But the six justices who form its conservative supermajority appeared either skeptical, or unsure of how they may rule.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Indivisible\u8217\'20\'19s managing director Mari Urbina linked the court\u8217\'20\'19s stance on the case to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s appointment of three of the justices who make up its conservative bloc:\par \par \uc2 We must stress unequivocally: this case has no business being decided by these MAGA Supreme Court justices. Their decision to hear it and put the law into effect is a calculated move, aligned with their deeply misogynistic, twisted, anti-women MAGA agenda.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a known fact, yet it remains profoundly disturbing that our fundamental rights\u8212\'20\'14our very bodies and lives\u8212\'20\'14are controlled by these MAGA, Trump-selected extremists.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito\u8217\'20\'19s remark that a woman is \u8216\'20\'18an individual\u8217\'20\'19 painfully exposes the core of their ideology. The fact that Republicans need to assert that women are indeed human beings blatantly reveals the depravity of their values and political agenda.\par \par \uc2 That last part is a dig justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , a conservative stalwart who was appointed by \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 A billionaire TikTok investor has been linked to $16m in donations to anti-Muslim and pro-Israel groups, a new investigation has found.\par \par \uc2 Eli Clifton reports for the Guardian:\par \par \uc2 Top Republican donor and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/tiktok"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TikTok}}}\uc2  investor Jeff Yass is connected to over $16m in funding to anti-Muslim and pro-Israel groups that have advocated for a US war with Iran and other militaristic policies in the Middle East, according to an investigation by the Guardian and Responsible Statecraft.\par \par \uc2 Media reports on Yass, the billionaire co-founder of \uc2 Susquehanna International Group\uc2 , a trading and technology firm, have focused on his outsized role in the Republican party, to which he is now the largest political donor in the 2024 election cycle, contributing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2024/04/09/jeff-yass-millions-to-influence-schools-courts-and-markets.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $46m}}}\uc2  thus far.\par \par \uc2 Yass has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/jeffrey-yass-summer-lee-bhavini-patel_n_66185af2e4b00830e916d6fb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emerged}}}\uc2  as the biggest funder of a group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/17/pro-israel-money-progressives-congress-challenges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  targeting progressive}}}\uc2  representative Summer Lee in her primary race, suggesting an interest in influencing Democratic primary outcomes, not just in boosting Republicans.\par \par \uc2 For the full story, click here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/24/jeff-yass-anti-muslim-pro-israel-donations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Billionaire Jeff Yass linked to $16m in donations to anti-Muslim and pro-Israel groups}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 The transportation department has issued a new set of rules to protect passengers from hidden airline fees.\par \par \uc2 In a video address on Wednesday, transportation secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Biden-Harris administration is now going to require airlines to give passengers an automatic cash refund if your flight is cancelled or significantly delayed, and you\u8217\'20\'19ll get your fee refunded if your bag doesn\u8217\'20\'19t arrive in time or if you don\u8217\'20\'19t get a service that you paid for like wifi.\par \par \uc2 No more refund runarounds and no defaulting to a travel credit that expires. We\u8217\'20\'19re also eliminating hidden fees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrOur department just issued rules to protect people from hidden airline fees and to require airlines to give passengers automatic cash refunds when owed. No more having to fend for yourself and jump through hoops to get your money back\u8212\'20\'14airlines will have to automatically do this. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jv7dFmnNkI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Jv7dFmnNkI}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  (@SecretaryPete) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SecretaryPete/status/1783097342792438232?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 24, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , the former Democrat who ran for president in 2020, has repeated a false claim about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \u8220\'20\'1Cgrooming\u8221\'20\'1D in a new book.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Martin Pengelly reports:\par \par \uc2 Accusing Democrats of making up \u8220\'20\'1Ca conspiracy theory that [Trump] was \u8216\'20\'18colluding\u8217\'20\'19 with the Russians to win the election\u8221\'20\'1D in 2016, Gabbard claims: \u8220\'20\'1CHillary Clinton used a similar tactic against me when I ran for president in 2020, accusing me of being \u8216\'20\'18groomed by the Russians\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gabbard ran for the Democratic nomination. Clinton did not accuse her of being \u8220\'20\'1Cgroomed by the Russians\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What Clinton {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2019/10/18/hillary-clinton-tulsi-gabbard-russia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , in October 2019 and on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/campaign-hq-with-david-plouffe/id1479487160"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 podcast}}}\uc2  hosted by the former \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  adviser David Plouffe, was that she thought Republicans would encourage a third-party bid in 2020, aiming to syphon votes from the Democratic candidate in key states as Jill Stein, the Green candidate, and the Libertarian, Gary Johnson, did four years before.\par \par \uc2 For the full story, click here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2024/apr/23/tulsi-gabbard-repeats-false-hillary-clinton-grooming-claim"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard repeats false Hillary Clinton \u8216\'20\'18grooming\u8217\'20\'19 claim in new book}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 The supreme court heard from both sides in a dispute between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66291f198f08e53db06877c5#block-66291f198f08e53db06877c5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Biden administration}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-662912468f08e53db06876fb#block-662912468f08e53db06876fb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho}}}\uc2  over whether federal law requires doctors in the state, where a strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is in effect, to perform the procedure on women in emergency circumstances. The court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-6629243f8f087a8ea2b790ce#block-6629243f8f087a8ea2b790ce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were skeptical}}}\uc2  towards Republican-governed Idaho, as were some members of the conservative majority, but it is unclear if they will ultimately find against the state. A decision is expected in June, which could again raise the public\u8217\'20\'19s awareness of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66290fb28f08415dae0dde4f#block-66290fb28f08415dae0dde4f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the impacts}}}\uc2  of Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19s downfall, just months before the November presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-662937fc8f08b7ccbb88c205#block-662937fc8f08b7ccbb88c205"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  the $95b foreign aid bill passed by the Senate yesterday into law. It approves further military assistance for Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, and threatens social media app TikTok with a nationwide ban unless its Chinese owner sells it in a year.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic lawmakers{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-662933568f087a8ea2b791a5#block-662933568f087a8ea2b791a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will try}}}\uc2  to get the state legislature to repeal its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, after two previous attempts failed.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The defense department{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/apr/24/supreme-court-abortion-emergency-biden-trump-latest-updates?page=with:block-66293c1e8f0881afefd55949#block-66293c1e8f0881afefd55949"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  a $1b weapons shipment to Ukraine shortly after the aid bill was signed that president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said would help them defend against Russia\u8217\'20\'19s attacks.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 The legislation just signed by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will also force ByteDance, the Chinese company that owns TikTok, to sell the popular social media app within a year or face a ban in the United States. Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on what happens next, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/danmilmo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dan Milmo}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has signed into law a bill that requires TikTok\u8217\'20\'19s Chinese owner to sell the social media app\u8217\'20\'19s US operations or face a ban, after the Senate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/23/senate-passes-bill-banning-tiktok-will-parent-company-sell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the legislation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law, part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/21/house-approves-61bn-aid-for-ukraine-what-we-know-so-far-and-what-happens-next"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 foreign aid package for Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan}}}\uc2 , sets the clock ticking on a potential ban for a platform that is hugely popular in the US.\par \par \uc2 Here is a guide to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/tiktok"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TikTok}}}\uc2  legislation and what might happen next.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/apr/24/why-is-us-threatening-to-ban-tiktok-and-could-other-countries-follow-suit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why is US threatening to ban TikTok and will other countries follow suit?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signed the foreign aid bill this morning, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.defense.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/3754238/biden-administration-announces-significant-new-security-assistance-for-ukraine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the defense department announced}}}\uc2  that it would ship $1b of new weapons and ammunition to Ukraine amid fears Russia\u8217\'20\'19s military will soon overrun more territory.\par \par \uc2 Some of what the defense department says it sent:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 RIM-7 and AIM-9M missiles for air defense;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stinger anti-aircraft missiles;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Small arms and additional rounds of small arms ammunition, including.50 caliber rounds to counter Unmanned Aerial Systems (UAS);\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Additional ammunition for High Mobility Artillery Rocket Systems (HIMARS);\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 155mm artillery rounds, including High Explosive and Dual Purpose Improved Conventional Munitions rounds;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 105mm artillery rounds;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 60mm mortar rounds;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bradley Infantry Fighting Vehicles;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mine Resistant Ambush Protected Vehicles (MRAPs);\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 High Mobility Multipurpose Wheeled Vehicles (HMMWVs);\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Logistics support vehicles;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tactical vehicles to tow and haul equipment;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tube-Launched, Optically-Tracked, Wire-Guided (TOW) missiles\par \par \uc2 In response, Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said his country was \u8220\'20\'1Cgaining the support we need to continue protecting lives from Russian attacks\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden signed into law today the support package approved by Congress, which includes Ukraine aid. We completed this half-year path. Regardless of what anyone says, we are gaining the support we need to continue protecting lives from Russian attacks.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  / ????????? ?????????? (@ZelenskyyUa) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ZelenskyyUa/status/1783168697307177273?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 24, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden signs $95b bill that aids Ukraine, Israel, threatens TikTok ban\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  this morning signed the $95b bill Congress passed yesterday that authorized further military aid for Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, and will see social media app TikTok banned nationwide in a year unless its owner ByteDance divests.\par \par \uc2 The White House released a video showing the moment Biden transformed the bill into a law:\par \par \uc2 enltrToday, we answered history\u8217\'20\'19s call at this critical inflection point.\par \par \uc2 With the signing of our national security package, we tell the world that America stands resolutely for democracy and freedom, and against tyranny and oppression. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/APhOSWWJLN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/APhOSWWJLN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1783173478213922974?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 24, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The Senate\u8217\'20\'19s overwhelming passage of the legislation yesterday capped months of congressional wrangling that at one point saw Democrats ready to swallow strict immigration regulations demanded by the GOP, until \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/feb/06/republicans-border-bill-mayorkas-impeachment-ukraine-israel-aid-latest#top-of-blog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emerged to torpedo the deal}}}\uc2 . Here\u8217\'20\'19s a recap of how we got here, and what the bill may mean for Washington\u8217\'20\'19s allies:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/24/biden-signs-foreign-aid-ukraine-israel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden signs $95bn foreign aid package for Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Beyond the supreme court, state legislatures have become important arenas for determining where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be accessed, and where it cannot. Today in Arizona, Democrats will once again try to repeal the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, after Republicans narrowly blocked previous efforts:\par \par \uc2 Democrats at the Arizona legislature are attempting to repeal the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total ban on abortions for a third straight week, again spotlighting an issue that has put {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  on the defensive in a battleground state for the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier this month}}}\uc2  concluded the state can enforce a long-dormant law that permits abortions only to save the pregnant patient\u8217\'20\'19s life. The ruling suggested doctors could be prosecuted under the law first approved in 1864, and anyone who assists in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could face two to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 The ruling put enormous pressure on Republicans in the state, who on the one hand are under intense pressure from some conservatives in their base who firmly support the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and from swing voters who strongly oppose the measure and will decide crucial races including the presidency, the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s control of the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Some prominent Republicans, including the GOP candidate for Senate, Kari Lake, have come out against the ban. But Republicans in the statehouse so far have blocked efforts by Democratic lawmakers to repeal the law.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/24/arizona-repeal-abortion-ban-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona Democrats in new effort to repeal 1864 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban law}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has wrapped up hearing the arguments in the case pitting Idaho against the Biden administration over whether states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans must allow the procedures in emergencies under the federal law Emtala.\par \par \uc2 But before arguments concluded, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s deputy solicitor Josh Turner rose to rebut arguments made by \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , the US solicitor general.\par \par \uc2 He warned that the conflict between state and federal law at issue in this case will return to the court because other states have strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws like Idaho, and their definitions of emergency situations where abortions can be performed are narrower than Emtala:\par \par \uc2 The administration\u8217\'20\'19s position ultimately is untethered from any limiting principle. There\u8217\'20\'19s just no way to limit this to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and there\u8217\'20\'19s no way to limit it to Idaho. There are 22 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books. This isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to end with Idaho, it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to end with the six states that general Prelogar mentioned because all of the states that have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations define the health and the emergency exception narrower than Emtala does. So this question is going to come up in state after state after state.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 US solicitor general \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2  pushed back against Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s claim that their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban does not conflict with the requirements of Emtala.\par \par \uc2 Under Emtala, hospitals are required to stabilize the health of patients who show up in the midst of medical emergencies. But under Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban, abortions are only permitted if a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk \u8211\'20\'13 a much higher threshold.\par \par \uc2 Prelogar called the argument that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and Emtala can coexist \u8220\'20\'1Cgravely mistaken\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s inconsistent with the actual text of the Idaho law, it\u8217\'20\'19s inconsistent with medical reality and it\u8217\'20\'19s inconsistent with what\u8217\'20\'19s happening on the ground,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar said.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban, she continued, \u8220\'20\'1CDoctors have to shut their eyes to everything except death. But under Emtala, you\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to be thinking about: is she about to lose her fertility?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Moreover, she said, doctors and pregnant patients in Idaho \u8211\'20\'13 whose full \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is currently in effect \u8211\'20\'13 are in reality not getting the care they are entitled to under Emtala.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen in Idaho today are not getting treatment,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are getting airlifted out of the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Three liberal justices express skepticism toward Idaho's arguments for strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 The three liberal justices have all expressed skepticism towards Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s argument against Emtala, the federal law that the Biden administration believes preempts strict state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Idaho was also in for some skeptical questions from \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a conservative who was among the justices that overturned Roe v Wade in 2022. Oral arguments only provide a hint of how the justices may ultimately rule, and in order for Idaho to lose, it would need five votes against it. Should Barrett band together with the liberals, who might be the fifth?\par \par \uc2 A candidate for that place could be \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , the chief justice and a conservative, but one who has occasionally acted as the court\u8217\'20\'19s swing vote in recent years. In an exchange with US solicitor general Elizabeth Pelogar, he asked her to address the scenario of how Emtala would handle a hospital where all the doctors cited conscience objections to abstain from performing an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the question is, could you force an individual doctor to step in, then, over a conscience objection, the answer is no \u8230\'20\'26 the question is whether or not they must have available someone who can comply with the procedures required by Emtala and what would be the consequence if they didn\u8217\'20\'19t? Would it be eventual termination of their participation in Medicare?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Roberts, referring to the federal health insurance program that many hospitals participate in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s right,\u8221\'20\'1D replied Prelogar. \u8220\'20\'1CIf a hospital was continually disobeying the requirement to have in place sufficient personnel to run their emergency room, then I imagine that (the \uc2 US department of health\uc2  and human services) enforcement action would work with that hospital to try to bring it into compliance, and if the hospital ultimately is just leaving itself in a position where it can never provide care, then it would terminate the Medicare funding agreement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the court rules, we may find out if that answer was enough to assuage Roberts\u8217\'20\'19s concerns.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Solicitor general pushes back against Idaho claim linking mental health emergencies and abortions\par \par \uc2 US solicitor general \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2  pushed back against Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s claim that a mental health emergency could necessitate an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Emtala, a federal law that requires hospitals to stabilize patients in medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere can be grave mental health emergencies, but Emtala could never require pregnancy termination as the stabilizing care,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy?\u8221\'20\'1D justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , a conservative, interrupted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere\u8217\'20\'19s why,\u8221\'20\'1D Prelogar responded. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s because that wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t do anything to address the underlying brain chemistry issue that\u8217\'20\'19s causing the mental health emergency in the first place. This is not about mental health. Generally, this is about treatment by ER doctors in the emergency room. And when a woman comes in with some great mental health emergency, if she happens to be pregnant, it would be incredibly unethical to terminate her pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 she might not be in a position to give any informed consent. Instead, the way you treat mental health emergency is to address what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in the brain. If you\u8217\'20\'19re having a psychotic episode, you administer antipsychotics.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Prelogar later emphasized that in the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s view, Emtala\u8217\'20\'19s protections would never extend to abortions for people in mental health crises.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the arguments, some of the conservative justices expressed confusion over whether Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban conflicts with the federal law Emtala, which requires hospitals to stabilize patients facing medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIs there any condition you\u8217\'20\'19re aware of where the solicitor general says Emtala requires an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be available under an emergency circumstance where Idaho law says there is not?\u8221\'20\'1D asked justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , a conservative.\par \par \uc2 Yes, if someone wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a mental health condition, replied Idaho deputy solicitor Josh Turner.\par \par \uc2 Kavanaugh seemed to move past Turner\u8217\'20\'19s claim, indicating that the justices had not been briefed enough on that particular topic. But it struck his fellow conservative justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 . If that\u8217\'20\'19s the only daylight between Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban and Emtala, she asked Turner: \u8220\'20\'1CWhy are you here?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Although Turner seemed to waver during oral arguments, Idaho has argued in court papers that its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban does align with Emtala. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists across the country have been making a similar argument: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans do not preclude people from getting medically necessary abortions, and so they don\u8217\'20\'19t need to be amended to further protect people in emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Doctors and patients say that, in reality, the exceptions written into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are so vague that they are unworkable. Dozens of women have launched lawsuits, including in a separate case in Idaho, saying that they were denied medically necessary abortions. Roughly 50 Idaho doctors have also left the state rather than try to navigate an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that carries a hefty criminal penalty for violations.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden administration argues Idaho law puts doctors, pregnant women in 'impossible position'\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is now hearing from US solicitor general \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , who is asking the justices to allow federal law Emtala to require Idaho and other states with strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions to perform the procedure in cases of emergencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday, doctors in Idaho and the woman in Idaho are in an impossible position. If a woman comes to an emergency room facing a grave threat to her health, but she isn\u8217\'20\'19t yet facing death, doctors either have to delay treatment and allow her condition to materially deteriorate, or they\u8217\'20\'19re airlifting her out of the state so she can get the emergency care that she needs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Prelogar, who represents the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 She continued:\par \par \uc2 One hospital system in Idaho says that right now, it\u8217\'20\'19s having to transfer pregnant women in medical crisis out of the state about once every other week. That\u8217\'20\'19s untenable, and Emtala does not countenance it. None of petitioners\u8217\'20\'19 interpretations fit with the text, and so they try to make this case be about the broader debate for access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of unwanted pregnancy. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not what this case is about at all. Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is enforceable in virtually all of its applications. But in the narrow circumstances involving grave medical emergencies, Idaho cannot criminalize the essential care that Emtala requires.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 If the justices side with Idaho, Americans could be left with a federal emergency medicine law that is open to exempting disfavored people, conditions and treatments \u8211\'20\'13 for example emergencies involving people addicted to opioids, Aids -related emergencies or treatment for gender dysphoria.\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s quest to exert total control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also, in the long term, establish legal theory that undermines the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s broader ability to regulate healthcare, the same way exempting the so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, mifepristone, from \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  analysis, would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/25/abortion-pill-case-fda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 undermine drug regulation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both cases are about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on their face, but the legal theories underpinning them could have extraordinarily broad implications for the health of all Americans.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/21/emtala-supreme-court-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  states are targeting an emergency healthcare law. Will the supreme court side with them?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Justices hammer Idaho over whether abortions should only be allowed to save lives rather than fertility\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s four female justices, including conservative \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , seemed taken aback by the claim by Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s deputy solicitor general, Josh Turner, that a doctor could be prosecuted for performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health, rather than her life.\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban only permits abortions in medical emergencies if a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is at stake. Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , a liberal, read off a list of real-life cases where women had their abortions delayed in medical emergencies. The problem with bans like Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s, doctors say, is that doctors are forced to wait and watch until patients get so sick that their life is at risk \u8211\'20\'13 rather than intervening as soon as a health issue emerges.\par \par \uc2 After the story of one woman, who Sotomayor said lost her baby and was forced into a hysterectomy, the justice asked if, under Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re telling me the doctor there couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have done the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt goes back to whether a doctor can in good-faith medical judgment \u8211\'20\'13 \u8221\'20\'1D Turner started to say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a lot for a doctor to risk,\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor interrupted. If a doctor violates Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, they can face up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Turner tried to insist that a doctor operating in good faith would not be prosecuted under the Idaho state legislature\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Coney Barrett asked: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if the prosecutor thought differently?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the nature of prosecutorial discretion,\u8221\'20\'1D Turner replied.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Emtala case turns on difference between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that protects woman's health and one that saves her life\par \par \uc2 The case that the justices are hearing right now, which deals with whether federal law protects hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 ability to offer medically necessary abortions, turns on an oft-overlooked nuance in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law: the difference between an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that may protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health versus one that may save her life.\par \par \uc2 All US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans technically allow abortions in medical emergencies. But seven states, including Idaho, have bans on the books that say that the patient\u8217\'20\'19s life has to be at risk \u8212\'20\'14 a higher standard than bans that allow for abortions when a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health is in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration sued Idaho over its ban, arguing that it conflicts with the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, or Emtala. Emtala requires hospitals to stabilize patients\u8217\'20\'19 health when they are facing medical emergencies, including \u8212\'20\'14 in the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s view \u8212\'20\'14 in cases where patients need abortions. If an Idaho doctor performs a \u8220\'20\'1Chealth-saving\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they could run afoul of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban \u8212\'20\'14 but if they don\u8217\'20\'19t, they could violate Emtala.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHer life is not in peril but she\u8217\'20\'19s going to lose her reproductive organs \u8230\'20\'26 unless an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes place,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , a liberal. \u8220\'20\'1CNow, that\u8217\'20\'19s the category of cases where Emtala says: \u8216\'20\'18My gosh, of course an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd yet Idaho says: \u8216\'20\'18Sorry, no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  here.\u8217\'20\'19 And the result is these patients are now helicoptered out of state,\u8221\'20\'1D Kagan continued.\par \par \uc2 The problem with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, doctors say, is that it is not always clear when a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk. And if doctors know that a pregnant patient\u8217\'20\'19s condition may worsen, they want to help her \u8211\'20\'13 not wait until she\u8217\'20\'19s on the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 Fellow liberal justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  proceeded to read off a list of real-life cases where women\u8217\'20\'19s abortions were delayed due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including the case of a woman who was ultimately forced to have a hysterectomy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s attorney Josh Turner argued that Emtala cannot override the state\u8217\'20\'19s law to require medical treatment in contravention of its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 He immediately received pushback from the court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem we\u8217\'20\'19re having right now is that you\u8217\'20\'19re sort of putting preemption on its head. The whole purpose of preemption is to say that if the state passes a that violates federal law, the state law is no longer effective,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sotomayor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf objective medical care requires you to treat women who are, who present the potential of serious medical complications, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only thing that can print them and that you have to do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, better known to hospitals administrators nationally as Emtala, requires emergency rooms to stabilize patients regardless of their ability to pay.\par \par \uc2 The act is among Americans\u8217\'20\'19 only universal federal rights to healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 one propelled into law by stories of private hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cdumping\u8221\'20\'1D patients who could not pay, including women in active labor, at public hospitals.\par \par \uc2 This law could be left in tatters if justices side with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states, represented by Idaho. Emtala requires doctors to treat patients if they face a serious risk to health, treatment that includes exceedingly rare emergency room abortions, the Biden administration argues.\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s state law is in direct conflict with this federal right to health, because it only allows doctors to intervene with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a woman is about to die. On the one hand, Emtala can levy serious penalties against providers and hospitals if doctors do not treat women. On the other, doctors could serve jail time or lose their license if they violate Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/21/emtala-supreme-court-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  states are targeting an emergency healthcare law. Will the supreme court side with them?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 The arguments begin with opening statements from Josh Turner, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional litigation and policy chief.\par \par \uc2 He argued that the Biden administration is interpreting Emtala \u8211\'20\'13 the federal law that the White House argue requires hospitals to perform \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emergencies, even when that runs afoul of state law \u8211\'20\'13 too broadly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone understands that licensing laws limit medical practice. That\u8217\'20\'19s why a nurse isn\u8217\'20\'19t available to perform open heart surgery, no matter the need, no matter her knowledge,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe answer doesn\u8217\'20\'19t change just because we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The court should reject the administration\u8217\'20\'19s unlimited reading of Emtala and reverse the district court\u8217\'20\'19s judgment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court begins hearing emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has convened to hear a case from Idaho challenging the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s requirement that federally funded hospitals carry out abortions in emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Follow along here as we cover it live.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 What's at stake as the supreme court hears emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case?\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is about to hear arguments over whether federal law can require hospitals in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to perform medically necessary abortions.\par \par \uc2 The case originated in Idaho, which only permits abortions in medical emergencies if a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk. The Biden administration has sued Idaho, claiming that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s stringent ban violates the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act \u8211\'20\'13 known as Emtala.\par \par \uc2 Idaho is expected to argue that its ban doesn\u8217\'20\'19t conflict with Emtala, because Emtala does not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cannot compel doctors to perform procedures that are illegal under Idaho law.\par \par \uc2 The case marks the second time this year that the supreme court, where the six conservative justices outnumber the three liberals, and which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  less than two years ago, has dealt with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Last month, the justices also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heard arguments in a case}}}\uc2  that deals with the availability of a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 A decision in the case is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Protesters converge on supreme court ahead of hearing on emergency abortions\par \par \uc2 As the supreme court prepares to weigh whether Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban conflicts with the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s insistence that federally funded hospitals carry out emergency abortions, protesters representing both sides of the debate are gathering outside:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court to hear conservative challenge to Biden policy requiring hospitals to provide emergency abortions\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issue of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  will once again be before the supreme court this morning, and the conservative justices who two years ago upended the country\u8217\'20\'19s politics by voting to overturn Roe v Wade and allow states to ban abortions. In response to that decision, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that even states where the procedure is banned must still allow hospitals that receive federal funds to perform the procedure in an emergency, citing a 1986 law called the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, or Emtala. Today\u8217\'20\'19s legal tussle involves the deeply conservative state of Idaho, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited unless a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk in an emergency. The Biden administration sued the state, arguing that its law clashed with Emtala, and the justices now have to sort out the dispute.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is dominated by a supermajority of six conservative justices against three liberals, but since dismantling Roe, they haven\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily shown the same zeal for other efforts to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . About a month ago, the justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared skeptical}}}\uc2  of attempts by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors to roll back the availability of mifepristone, a drug used nationwide in medication abortions. Today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing begins at 10am ET, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll be listening to the arguments for a sense of how they are leaning on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else we are watching today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Biden is expected to sign {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/23/senate-aid-bill-ukraine-israel-taiwan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a bill approved by the Senate last night}}}\uc2  that authorizes $95b in aid to Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, capping months of congressional wrangling over the contentious issue.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Republican speaker of the House, Mike Johnson, is heading to \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , which has been rocked by pro-Palestinian protests in recent days and police arrests of demonstrators. He will hold a press conference at 3:45pm this afternoon, and you can follow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/apr/24/columbia-student-gaza-protests-israel-biden-latest-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our live blog}}}\uc2  for more.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Summer Lee, a progressive Democratic congresswoman who faced a primary challenge over her support for a ceasefire in Gaza, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/23/summer-lee-wins-pennsylvania-primary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 easily won}}}\uc2  the party\u8217\'20\'19s nomination again to represent her Pennsylvania district.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240424ek4o001xj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
