\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Have you signed yet?\u8217\'20\'19: Arizona activists battle to overturn near-total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Leingang in Phoenix \par \uc2 1,953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 16:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Citizen\u8217\'20\'19s initiative\u8217\'20\'19 aims to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution and repeal a long-dormant outright ban\par \par \uc2 As people streamed into the empanada restaurant, Susan Anthony made eye contact, pointing to her sign that asked whether they were pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHave you signed yet?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked patrons at the establishment in Mesa, Arizona. She carried a clipboard with petition sheets for a citizen\u8217\'20\'19s initiative, a ballot measure that would put the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the swing state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Since the state supreme court ruled on 9 April that lawmakers in Arizona intended to fully ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the signatures have come in more quickly, Anthony said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo, but I\u8217\'20\'19d like to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI drove here to sign this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been wanting to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve signed it, probably multiple times.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to tell my friend to come here and sign it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starting the day the ruling came down, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arizonaforabortionaccess.org/what-is-the-aaa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona for \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Access}}}\uc2  measure has seen its volunteers grow from about 3,000 to more than 5,000, spokeswoman Dawn Penich said. More than 5,300 small-dollar donors gave money for the first time since the ruling. The group is not giving out a new total update for the number of signatures, but Penich said that volunteers brought in 2,200 signatures to get notarized in one hour at a Phoenix coffee shop.\par \par \uc2 A handful of legislative Republicans have been scrambling since the ruling to try to peel back the outright ban, first passed there in 1864, before Arizona was a state, and instead reaffirm the more recent law, a 15-week ban. In the House, Democrats and a couple of Republicans could again try to force the repeal to a vote this Wednesday, the third attempt in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in this swing state will ultimately be decided on November\u8217\'20\'19s ballot, where voters will probably face multiple questions. Democrats also hope the issue will turn out enough voters to flip the statehouse blue, and some Republican officials are now worried about how the ruling, which most of them wanted, will affect their political prospects. Those in swing districts and close races, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  candidate Kari Lake, have spoken against the ruling despite previously supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 In the house, efforts to move toward repealing the ban have so far failed, while the senate limped forward. A document detailing plans to derail the citizen\u8217\'20\'19s ballot measure, accidentally sent to all lawmakers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/legislature/2024/04/15/arizona-abortion-republicans-seek-to-undercut-ballot-measure/73332934007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 floated the idea}}}\uc2  of sending three separate questions from the legislature directly to voters, bypassing the Democratic governor and confusing the issue at the ballot.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the 1864 ban could go into effect as early as 8 June. The Democratic attorney general, Kris Mayes, has said her office will not prosecute providers over abortions at any point. And neighboring California is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2024/04/21/gavin-newsom-arizona-abortion-california-00153509"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 working}}}\uc2  to allow Arizona \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers a way to get licensed quickly there to assist their patients, anticipating more people will cross state lines for care.\par \par \uc2 Legislature in limbo\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access measure would allow abortions without any limitations until the point of fetal viability, and access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability if a healthcare provider determines it is needed to protect the patient\u8217\'20\'19s life or physical and mental health.\par \par \uc2 The legislature has several routes it could take: doing nothing, and upholding the 1864 ban; repealing the ban, which would set a 15-week limit as the prevailing law; sending one or more questions to voters to set limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access measure needs about 384,000 valid signatures from Arizona voters by 3 July to make the ballot and has reported collecting more than 500,000 so far. But the state applies strict scrutiny to citizens\u8217\'20\'19 initiatives, with intense requirements for each signature and the people collecting them. In recent years, groups have sued, at times successfully, to remove signatures for various reasons in attempts to keep measures from reaching the ballot.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know the Republicans in the next three months are going to do everything in their power to try to take that initiative off the ballot,\u8221\'20\'1D the former Democratic lawmaker and congressional candidate Raquel Ter\u225\'00\'E1n said at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rally last Friday. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we should not count on just half a million \u8211\'20\'13 we need to turn in a million signatures or more. Do not stop. We cannot stop, nor take any signature for granted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers do not have to collect any signatures to put their questions to voters, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t need the governor\u8217\'20\'19s approval. Instead, they can vote to send any number of questions to the ballot directly.\par \par \uc2 But to get the repeal up for a vote, some Republican lawmakers would need to vote against their party\u8217\'20\'19s leaders to override normal procedures \u8211\'20\'13 and they have so far been unwilling. Republican representative David Cook predicted that could happen this week, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kjzz.org/content/1877601/republican-says-abortion-ban-repeal-will-advance-state-house-wednesday"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 telling Phoenix public radio outlet KJZZ}}}\uc2  that there would be enough votes to alter rules and allow the repeal up for a vote.\par \par \uc2 While a few Republican lawmakers have said they think the ban goes too far, others have held fast to their support for it. The Center for Arizona Policy, an influential state organization responsible for lobbying for strict anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws for decades, called on lawmakers to oppose any efforts to repeal the ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPolitical posturing for the sake of votes and back-pedalling when faced with hostility only feeds voter cynicism at the cost of human life,\u8221\'20\'1D the group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/azpolicy/status/1780283321370673274"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in a statement}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The house speaker, Ben Toma, a Republican, has defended the ban and is not in favor of repealing it, despite the potential political consequences. Toma is now running for an open congressional seat in a crowded GOP primary where Trump has already endorsed one of his opponents. Toma is not currently available for interviews.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt comes down to: what do I think is right? What is just? What is ethical? And I have made my decision. And I am not going to change my mind,\u8221\'20\'1D he recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/20/us/arizona-abortion-trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the New York Times}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Beyond the repeal machinations, Republicans are trying to figure out what, if anything, they should send to the ballot. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/24548816-legislative-strategies-on-abortion-powerpoint"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presentation of options}}}\uc2 , written by the Arizona house Republican general counsel, Linley Wilson, floated three potential ballot referrals:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplementary (not conflicting)\u8221\'20\'1D measure that would include policies like only physicians being allowed to perform abortions and parental consent for minors seeking them. This would require the courts to consider it alongside the constitutional access measure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A 14-week ban \u8220\'20\'1Cdisguised as a 15-week law\u8221\'20\'1D because it would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the beginning of the 15th week of pregnancy. This \u8220\'20\'1Cdilutes\u8221\'20\'1D the votes for the access measure and makes it more likely to fail.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat protection act\u8221\'20\'1D that makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal after six weeks unless the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk, the fetus has an abnormality or the pregnancy resulted from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 These paths change the narrative, Wilson wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans have a plan! And it\u8217\'20\'19s much more reasonable than the (Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access) Initiative.\u8221\'20\'1D The plan could \u8220\'20\'1Cpull votes\u8221\'20\'1D from the access measure.\par \par \uc2 Energy increases for ballot measure\par \par \uc2 At the Mesa restaurant, some people stopped by specifically to sign the petition, asking if there were other locations they could send their family and friends to. Some, carrying babies or holding toddlers\u8217\'20\'19 hands, said they had been meaning to sign and took a pen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had women come up with three kids, and they\u8217\'20\'19re signing. And I tell them, moms are the most important signature here, because they understand what this issue is, and what pregnancy does to the body, what pregnancy does to your life,\u8221\'20\'1D Anthony said.\par \par \uc2 Others avoided eye contact or said they weren\u8217\'20\'19t interested. Some said they weren\u8217\'20\'19t registered to vote or simply didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote. Some days, a person will stroll by and call her a baby killer. Anthony doesn\u8217\'20\'19t engage \u8211\'20\'13 she\u8217\'20\'19s not trying to convince the opposition right now; she\u8217\'20\'19s trying to find the people already in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and get them to sign.\par \par \uc2 After Roe v Wade fell, Anthony, a 69-year-old retiree, made it her life\u8217\'20\'19s mission to get the Arizona \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on the ballot. Anthony, a lifelong Democrat, didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to share her political leanings when she first moved to the red state of Arizona in the 1980s. Now she spends her days sitting at tables in restaurants and businesses, even at trailheads to snag hikers before they set off. Some hikers tell her she shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be there, that they\u8217\'20\'19re just trying to enjoy nature; then others come up and say thank you.\par \par \uc2 Since the ruling this month, the energy has shifted. It\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cnight and day\u8221\'20\'1D, with people seeking out places to sign the measure, Anthony said. At a shift at a bottle shop, college students posted on social media after signing and got more people to stop by. At a boutique in Scottsdale, in a wealthier area known for business-type Republicans, signers told her, this isn\u8217\'20\'19t right, as they added their signature to the petition sheets.\par \par \uc2 The other side is going door to door and rallying at the statehouse, too. When Democrats tried to put the repeal of the 1864 ban up for a vote last week, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates filled the gallery.\par \par \uc2 At an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rally last Friday evening hosted by a handful of left-leaning groups, Democratic officials detailed the importance of the ballot measure and voting for their party to take the legislature, win the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat and go for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a sign that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on the ballot influences how other races could go.\par \par \uc2 They made clear to a few dozen attendees: the ban is now in place because of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  nominees, who overturned Roe vs Wade. The 1864 ban is still in place because Arizona Republicans explicitly voted to keep it there as recently as two years ago. It hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been repealed yet because legislative Republicans have blocked Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to do so for years.\par \par \uc2 The backlash to the ban has taken aim at Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court, too, where two justices are up for retention elections. A progressive group, Progress Arizona, is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2024/04/22/arizona-abortion-ban-campaign-wants-to-unseat-pro-1864-law-justices/73389612007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raising money}}}\uc2  for a campaign to oust the Republican justices Clint Bolick and Kathryn H King.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact is, even if we were to repeal this ban, that is not the end of the fight,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic state representative Oscar de los Santos told the crowd. \u8220\'20\'1CThis November, we have an election that isn\u8217\'20\'19t merely a choice between two parties: it is a choice between two visions, between freedom and fascism, between hope and hate, between 1864 and 2024.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anthony hopes she won\u8217\'20\'19t have to gather signatures for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access again. She thinks the measure will make it to the ballot this year, and from there, it\u8217\'20\'19s up to the voters. But a lot of other factors are in limbo, like the rights of Arizonans to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am most concerned at this point by anything that [Republicans] are going to put on there to muddy the waters, to confuse people,\u8221\'20\'1D Anthony said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19m concerned with. So I\u8217\'20\'19m anxious to hear from our folks what the strategy will be come 3 July, when we deliver the boxes to the secretary of state. So what happens then? What are we doing then?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240424ek4o001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
