\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18This is a violent attack against women\u8217\'20\'19: Florida Senate candidate seeks to channel  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  outrage\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe in Miami \par \uc2 887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2024\par 14:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2  is challenging incumbent \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and highlighting his \u8216\'20\'18unapologetic and proud\u8217\'20\'19 support for the state\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban\par \par \uc2 A round table on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, hosted by Florida\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2 , has only just begun, and already she finds herself comforting a woman in tears with a very personal story to tell.\par \par \uc2 The woman is from Colombia, and speaks softly in Spanish as she tells the intimate gathering of the Miami-Dade Hispanic Democratic Caucus about the distressing decision her daughter had to make to terminate a pregnancy after learning the fetus was not developing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Colombia, which tends to be a very conservative country, she was glad supportive medical professionals were there for her daughter in the decision, and grateful she had access to good-quality healthcare for it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mucarsel-Powell.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was traumatic and painful, but at least they could rely on that healthcare. I\u8217\'20\'19m just seeing outrage, from men and women, that here, families are faced with having to live in a state where you will not be able to get that care, because most women don\u8217\'20\'19t even know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant at six weeks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She was referring to the ruling by Florida\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court earlier this month that will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/02/florida-abortion-ban-six-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allow a six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban,}}}\uc2  with few exceptions for rape or incest, to take effect on 1 May. It will end the state\u8217\'20\'19s position as a bulwark of access to the procedure in the south-eastern US.\par \par \uc2 Yet it has also acted as rocket fuel to the campaign of Mucarsel-Powell, an Ecuador-born former congresswoman and mother of two daughters. She seized on the issue to launch a statewide Freedom Tour championing the protection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and exposing the \u8220\'20\'1Cunapologetic and proud\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/3949607-rick-scott-on-6-week-abortion-ban-if-i-was-still-governor-i-would-sign-this-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support}}}\uc2  for the ban on the part of her opponent in November, the incumbent Republican senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Hispanic Caucus event in Coral Gables was only the third of the tour, but Mucarsel-Powell said it was already clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a \u8220\'20\'1Ctop-of-mind\u8221\'20\'1D issue galvanizing voters, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/13/democrats-abortion-voter-arizona-florida"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as it is in other Republican-controlled states}}}\uc2  that have curtailed reproductive rights since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ended almost 50 years of federal protections with its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 reversal of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, her campaign announced it had raised over $3.5m in the first quarter of the year, with more than 5,300 new donors since the supreme court ruling. And Democrats across Florida are also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/florida-democrats-abortion-ban-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sensing wind in their sails}}}\uc2  as opposition to the ban, as well as support for a court-approved ballot initiative that could enshrine access to the procedure in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, hardens.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a violent attack against women, because it is fundamental for us to make that decision on our own, with our healthcare provider, with our families, with our faith,\u8221\'20\'1D Mucarsel-Powell told the Guardian in an interview following the round table.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is about protecting privacy, protecting healthcare for women, making sure that there\u8217\'20\'19s no government interference, especially from extreme politicians like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . I can tell you what people are thinking about this, and that it\u8217\'20\'19s affecting women living in the state of Florida that were sent home when they thought they were having a miscarriage, and they weren\u8217\'20\'19t able to get that healthcare.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then they got very ill, and almost died because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t receive that healthcare. So this is a top-of-mind issue, like so many other issues, but we\u8217\'20\'19ll see in November how voters decide what are going to be their priorities. I think they\u8217\'20\'19re going to make things very clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Also clear is Mucarsel-Powell\u8217\'20\'19s disdain for Scott, who she believes is vulnerable in November as he defends the seat he narrowly won from the incumbent Democrat \uc2 Bill Nelson\uc2  in 2018 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/election-results/2018/florida/senate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by only 10,000 votes}}}\uc2  from 8.2m cast.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf he goes back to the Senate, he will push for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CHis true agenda includes signing away women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights and trying to control their bodies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd he knows he will have to answer for his support of Florida\u8217\'20\'19s ban in November. The choice is going to be very clear for voters, they know who I am, they know what I stand for, and who and what \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 An Emerson College poll this week showed that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://emersoncollegepolling.com/florida-2024-poll-plurality-plan-to-vote-yes-on-abortion-protection-ballot-measure/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 42% of Florida voters}}}\uc2  planned to vote for the constitutional amendment that would overturn the Florida ban, far short of the 60% it would need to pass.\par \par \uc2 Yet Mucarsel-Powell sees hope in the 32% who say they are still unsure. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people don\u8217\'20\'19t know that this amendment is on the ballot, so the movement that has been created and has built this infrastructure on the ground is ready to make sure that everyone knows this is an issue,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe work is happening, it will continue to happen, and I think in November, the majority of Floridians will know that they have a choice. I believe they\u8217\'20\'19re going to come out and vote for freedom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240419ek4j002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
