\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arizona  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers hope 1864 ban will spark change: \u8216\'20\'18A blue wave is coming\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Terry Greene Sterling in Phoenix \par \uc2 1,267 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2024\par 14:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Providers are optimistic for a different future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state even as Republicans flounder in their response\par \par \uc2 The waiting room of the Acacia Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center in Phoenix, Arizona, was calm and quiet on Friday. Patients sat with their mothers, friends or partners, paying no mind to the slapstick Tyler Perry movie on the TV and an arrangement of Vogue magazines resting on a table.\par \par \uc2 It had been three days since the state\u8217\'20\'19s highest court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision#:~:text=The%20Arizona%20supreme%20court%20ruled,electoral%20fight%20over%20abortion%20rights."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reinstated}}}\uc2  an 1864 law that would ban almost all abortions and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to prison for up to five years.\par \par \uc2 The revival of the 160-year-old statute has kickstarted deep uncertainty over the fate of clinics like Acacia, one of the few medical centers left providing abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 Some of the women awaiting the procedure that day said they were deeply worried by the state supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision. Others didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Dr Ronald Yunis, the obstetrician and gynecologist who owns the women\u8217\'20\'19s center was nervous yet hopeful. Yes, the 1864 law is jeopardizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in his state. But he, like some other providers, believe it will eventually lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being enshrined in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. And if that happens, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Arizona will be stronger than ever.\par \par \uc2 For now, Yunis is determined to keep performing abortions, which take up about half of his practice \u8211\'20\'13 at least until June, when the law is slated to take effect. In the meantime, abortions up to 15 weeks can be performed in accordance with a restrictive 2022 state law passed by conservative Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 A conservative himself, Yunis once supported Republicans in the statehouse.\par \par \uc2 Now he calls them \u8220\'20\'1Cidiots\u8221\'20\'1D who refuse to \u8220\'20\'1Cshut their mouths about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s sure Republican lawmakers will reverse course and vote to repeal the law despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-block-repeal-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 having blocked}}}\uc2  an effort by Democrats to undo it last week. It\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctheir only political play\u8221\'20\'1D, Yunis says, given the blowback from Arizona voters and weakened GOP candidates facing tight races in November.\par \par \uc2 It would take only a few Republicans to join forces with Democrats to knock the ancient statute down, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/legislature/2024/04/16/democrats-could-overturn-arizona-1864-abortion-ban-law-this-week/73335613007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Arizona Republic reports}}}\uc2 , and lawmakers could start the process to repeal the law as early as Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 This whole episode is so emblematic of how broken our political system is\par \par \uc2 Dr Paul Isaacson\par \par \uc2 Even if the law is stricken from the books, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Arizona face deep uncertainty. There is nothing preventing the passage of more anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in the future. And in a statehouse where an election-denying GOP lawmaker recently spoke in tongues with his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cprayer team\u8221\'20\'1D in the senate chamber, anything is possible.\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general, Kris Mayes, a Democrat, has assured providers they will not be prosecuted for performing abortions as long as she is in office. But providers nevertheless fear that a subsequent attorney general might prosecute them for practicing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Mayes\u8217\'20\'19s watch if far-right leaders in the legislature either preserve the 1864 law or criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with further legislation. There\u8217\'20\'19s a seven-year statute of limitations for many crimes in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Republican leaders are flip-flopping on the issue. Kari Lake, the Arizona senate candidate, once ballyhooed the 1864 law. Now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/14/arizona-senate-kari-lake-abortion-ruben-gallego"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trailing}}}\uc2  her Democratic rival in the race, the congressman \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , in a \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race, she maintains the law is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2024/04/13/kari-lake-arizona-senate-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cout of step\u8221\'20\'1D with Arizonans.}}}\uc2  It might be an acknowledgment made too late. When polled in 2022, only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.noblepredictiveinsights.com/post/voting-impact-of-supreme-court-ruling-on-abortion-in-arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 28% of voters}}}\uc2  in Arizona approved of the 1864 law.\par \par \uc2 Amid the uncertainty, providers say they are hanging their hopes on what\u8217\'20\'19s become an increasingly effective way to undo bills passed by Republican legislators \u8211\'20\'13 relying on voters to enshrine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-ballot-amendment-ban-protection-states-2024-052ff9846f8416efb725240af22b92ec"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights in state constitutions}}}\uc2  via ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the decision to reinstate the 1864 law, a popular proposed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Arizona_Right_to_Abortion_Initiative_(2024)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measure}}}\uc2  giving Arizona voters the chance to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the state constitution in November is gaining momentum \u8211\'20\'13 even as Republicans strategize the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/legislature/2024/04/15/arizona-abortion-republicans-seek-to-undercut-ballot-measure/73332934007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 measure\u8217\'20\'19s defeat}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis whole episode is so emblematic of how broken our political system is,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dr Paul Isaacson, of Family Planning Associates Medical Group in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Isaacson, 65, a longtime \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, says his medical group will continue offering services \u8220\'20\'1Cuntil we can\u8217\'20\'19t\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Amid the uncertainty, Isaacson worries he might have to shut down for several months before the election brings more clarity about his future. If he shuts down, he will still have to pay rent and bills for leased equipment in his office. Worse, there may be painful layoffs of a small staff he calls a \u8220\'20\'1Csecond family\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very, very big deal,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 Last week was nerve-racking, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers say, but now they are taking a step back from the chaos and trying to figure out the best way to move forward in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Dr Gabrielle Goodrick, a physician who owns Camelback Family Practice, a clinic that provides one-third of the state\u8217\'20\'19s abortions, says she was holding fostered kittens in her lap when she learned of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to revive the 1864 law. Her initial reaction, she says, was \u8220\'20\'1Cgrief and shock\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Setting aside her emotions, Goodrick began researching ways for her practice to stay open. She vows the clinic will not close its doors. In the worst-case scenario, she envisions it staying open to help women go to other states for abortions and to provide legal reproductive and healthcare needs to women in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s looking for grants and funding possibilities that would help any staffers that might be let go, which would be the hardest part of any partial shutdown. Her 15 staffers, she says, \u8220\'20\'1Care fighters and I love all of them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I am absolutely certain it will pass... when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the ballot it wins\par \par \uc2 Dr Jill Gibson\par \par \uc2 Layoffs will not be necessary if Arizona voters cement \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution in November. And Goodrick has high hopes for the passage of the measure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can feel the blue wave coming,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very reassuring.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That optimism is echoed by Dr Jill Gibson, chief medical officer at a Phoenix-based Planned Parenthood Arizona. The non-profit recently resumed providing abortions in Flagstaff, a city in northern Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care desert\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been two years since \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  provided abortions in Flagstaff, and given the firestorm precipitated by the 1864 law, it could be risky to resume services. But Gibson is hanging her hopes on voters giving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  constitutional protection in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am absolutely certain it will pass,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, noting that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the ballot it wins.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yunis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider who owns the Acacia Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center, likes a good fight. He\u8217\'20\'19s fought for women\u8217\'20\'19s rights for years, he says, and he\u8217\'20\'19s not about to stop.\par \par \uc2 Several years ago, he allegedly pointed a gun at an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protester \u8220\'20\'1Cwith a history of violence\u8221\'20\'1D blocking his car. Yunis claims self-defense. Still, the incident led to a misdemeanor conviction, scrutiny by the Arizona Board of Medical Examiners and getting dropped by Medicare. But it didn\u8217\'20\'19t dampen his spirit.\par \par \uc2 Now, even amid the chaos caused by Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban machinations, he senses victory.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are going to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional amendment in Arizona,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no doubt now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240417ek4h001bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
