\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Republicans divided over Arizona near-total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban, poll shows\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 567 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2024\par 09:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Split reflects GOP\u8217\'20\'19s identity crisis, as Republican politicians struggle to talk about increasingly toxic issue for them\par \par \uc2 Republicans are nearly evenly divided over the Arizona supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision}}}\uc2  to uphold an 1864 law that would ban almost all abortions, new polling shows.\par \par \uc2 Forty-nine per cent of Republicans strongly or somewhat disapprove of the decision, while 46% of Republicans strongly or somewhat approve of it, according to polling of more than 1,000 likely voters released on Tuesday by the left-leaning thinktank Data for Progress. That split reflects the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/trump-arizona-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GOP\u8217\'20\'19s identity crisis}}}\uc2  over post-Roe v Wade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics, as Republican politicians have struggled to talk about an issue that was once their bread-and-butter but has become increasingly toxic for them.\par \par \uc2 Data for Progress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.filesforprogress.org/datasets/2024/4/dfp_az_trump_abortion_tabs.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that voters diverged along party lines on whether they believed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s statements on the issue. Trump has said that the Arizona supreme court went too far while insisting that the decision will be \u8220\'20\'1Cstraightened out\u8221\'20\'1D. He has also continued to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an issue best decided by state governments, a position {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/08/trump-abortion-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he took in a video}}}\uc2  issued just a day before the Arizona supreme court ruling.\par \par \uc2 In the group\u8217\'20\'19s polling, more than 80% of Democrats said that they believed Trump was being \u8220\'20\'1Cdishonest\u8221\'20\'1D and would sign national restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . About the same percentage of Republicans said that Trump was being \u8220\'20\'1Chonest\u8221\'20\'1D and would not restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  further.\par \par \uc2 Independents were more split. Forty-four per cent of independent and third-party voters said that they thought Trump was being honest, but 41% per cent of those voters believed that Trump was being dishonest.\par \par \uc2 Forty-one per cent of independent and third-party voters also believed that Trump would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access if elected president, while 30% thought he would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access roughly the same. Only a quarter of Republicans thought Trump would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access; about half thought he would leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access alone. Just 12% thought he would tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, with the rest of the Republican voters in the poll saying that they did not know.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, though, most voters thought that the president of the United States has relatively little to do with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Republicans, independent voters and third-party voters, in particular, think members of Congress, statewide officials and state lawmakers all have more to do with setting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roein 2022, the GOP embraced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and passed scores of restrictions that, now that Roe has fallen, have been able to take full effect and cut off access to the procedure for millions of women. But most Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should at least be available in the first trimester of pregnancy and, powered by outrage over Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters have won multiple red-state ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some Republican officials and candidates have since attempted to moderate their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; multiple Arizona Republicans with a history of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beliefs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-against-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denounced the Arizona supreme court decision}}}\uc2 . However, Republicans in the state legislature also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-block-repeal-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked an effort}}}\uc2  to repeal the 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which would only permit abortions in cases where the pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s life was in danger and does not have exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240416ek4g0018m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
FF
