\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Georgians probably benefited little from claiming fetuses on tax filings\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2024\par 16:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 People can claim $3,000 deduction for fetuses reaching six weeks gestation \u8211\'20\'13 but \u8216\'20\'18it\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics\u8217\'20\'19 not tax policy, experts say\par \par \uc2 There were more than 36,000 \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn children\u8221\'20\'1D claimed in tax filings in Georgia in 2022, the year the state enacted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that also let people claim fetuses as dependents. Claiming that fetus, however, may have only netted individuals less than a couple hundred dollars in a state where a fifth of all children live in poverty.\par \par \uc2 Georgia currently bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy, which is before many women know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant. But that ban, unlike the other dozen-plus bans that currently blanket much of the US south and midwest, includes a provision that allows people to claim a $3,000 deduction for a \u8220\'20\'1Cdependent minor\u8221\'20\'1D fetus as long as that fetus reaches six weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 Defining a fetus as a \u8220\'20\'1Cminor\u8221\'20\'1D is part of a nationwide campaign by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to bestow embryos and fetuses with \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D, or full legal rights and protections. If realized to the maximum extent, fetal personhood would not only make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal but also rewrite vast swathes of US law, including tax law, with sometimes-unforeseen consequences.\par \par \uc2 During debate over Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s ban, a lawmaker who sponsored it estimated that deductions from the bill would range somewhere between $7m and $9m. But in 2022, those deductions topped more than $109m, a spokesperson for Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s department of revenue told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Yet, given the byzantine nature of tax laws, pregnant individuals in Georgia probably benefited very little from those deductions, said \uc2 Richard Auxier\uc2 , a senior policy associate in the \uc2 Urban-Brookings Tax Policy Center\uc2 . A deduction lowers an individual\u8217\'20\'19s taxable income, not their tax bill, and the deduction\u8217\'20\'19s ultimate value varies based on tax rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a very small tax benefit. In terms of what an actual person could claim for this, we\u8217\'20\'19re talking like $150. That\u8217\'20\'19s it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Auxier, whose evaluation of individuals\u8217\'20\'19 savings is roughly in line with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/georgia-parents-can-claim-unborn-children-on-2022-state-taxes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a past analysis}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd critically, only relatively higher-income families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my opinion, I will say, this is a tell,\u8221\'20\'1D Auxier said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is why this is not tax policy. It\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics masquerading as tax policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If Georgia wanted to seriously help people ease the economic burden of having a child, Auxier suggested that Georgia implement a state child tax credit, which can directly diminish families\u8217\'20\'19 tax bills and thus keep more money in their pockets. Fifteen states as well as the federal government already offer versions of such child tax credits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA deduction can only lower your taxable income to zero. So if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have much money, or much taxable income, it\u8217\'20\'19s never going to help you,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut you can make a credit refundable. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t have much income and you don\u8217\'20\'19t have much tax liability \u8211\'20\'13 if you have very little or even zero\u8212\'20\'14we can just give it to you as a refund.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2021, in the midst of the coronavirus lockdown, Congress expanded the child tax credit to provide monthly payments to millions of US families. That credit has been linked to a historic reduction in child poverty, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/the-antipoverty-effects-of-the-expanded-child-tax-credit-across-states-where-were-the-historic-reductions-felt/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 child poverty falling to a record low}}}\uc2 , as well as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/economic-hardship-declined-in-households-with-children-as-child-tax-credit-payments-arrived.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a drop in child hunger}}}\uc2 . But that expansion lapsed at the end of 2021 and, amid a lack of Republican support, has not returned. Joe Biden is now campaigning on a promise to revive it.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to children\u8217\'20\'19s economic wellbeing, Georgia ranks 35th in the nation, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://assets.aecf.org/m/resourcedoc/aecf-2023kidscountdatabook-2023.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an evaluation}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Annie E Casey Foundation\uc2 , which considered factors like the number of children living in poverty, living in households that are burdened by housing costs, and living with parents who lack secure employment. In an evaluation of children\u8217\'20\'19s health, which examined factors like infant birth weight and the number of children living without health insurance, Georgia came in 43rd.\par \par \uc2 States that have passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, such as Georgia, also tend to have higher maternal and infant mortality rates and more maternity care deserts.\par \par \uc2 Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s budget for fiscal year 2025 tops $36bn. The state probably lost about $6m because its tax deduction for fetuses, Auxier estimated.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, the rate of preterm births in Georgia earned an F grade from March of Dimes, a national organization dedicated to improving maternal and infant health, which also found that the infant mortality rate among Black birthing people was 1.5 times higher than the rate among other people in the state. A review of maternal deaths in Georgia in 2019 and 2021 also found that 85% were preventable.\par \par \uc2 Advocates who are dedicated to curbing Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s maternal and infant mortality crisis said that they never asked for a tax deduction for fetuses \u8211\'20\'13 and that they have struggled to garner the support and money they need from the Republican-controlled state legislature.\par \par \uc2 Miriam Goodfriend, policy manager at the Healthy Mothers, Healthy Babies Coalition of Georgia, said that lawmakers view the money set aside for addressing the maternal health crisis as \u8220\'20\'1Cfinite\u8221\'20\'1D. When legislators moved to increase funding for a project for moms, they took it from the funding set aside for another project for babies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re taking from infants and giving to mothers,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodfriend said. Although the coalition was able to get the money reinstated, Goodfriend said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we ask for funding, we have to watch them extra closely to see where they\u8217\'20\'19re going to \u8216\'20\'18take\u8217\'20\'19 it from because they\u8217\'20\'19re very much so guarding the state\u8217\'20\'19s surplus budget.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This legislative session, Healthy Mothers, Healthy Babies Coalition of Georgia hoped to pass legislation that would eliminate sales taxes on menstrual products. But that proposal died in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 Precious Andrews, director of special projects and innovations at Healthy Mothers, Healthy Babies Coalition of Georgia, has given birth twice in the state. Her experience was so \u8220\'20\'1Chorrendous\u8221\'20\'1D, she said, that she will never again give birth in Georgia. Despite that experience, Andrews said, she never wished for a tax deduction.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not what I wanted. I was like: \u8216\'20\'18Dang, I\u8217\'20\'19m pregnant, I need a doula. Dang, I\u8217\'20\'19m pregnant, I need to ensure that my healthcare provider is not going to gaslight me, I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to experience medical racism,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Andrews said. \u8220\'20\'1CThose were things that I worried about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240415ek4f002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
