\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Democrats bank on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in 2024 as Arizona and Florida push stakes higher\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman and Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 1,381 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2024\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Focus on reproductive rights has yielded big wins and Democrats hope threat of more Republican bans will mobilize voters\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Friday visit to Arizona was planned before the state\u8217\'20\'19s top court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upheld}}}\uc2  a 160-year-old law that bans almost all abortions. But the news galvanized the vice-president\u8217\'20\'19s message, one that has already yielded stunning victories for liberals since Roe v Wade fell nearly two years ago.\par \par \uc2 That message is simple: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans happen when Republicans are in charge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen here live under one of the most extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in our nation. \u8230\'20\'26 The overturning of Roe was without any question a seismic event, and this ban here in Arizona is one of the biggest aftershocks yet,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said at the Tucson event. \u8220\'20\'1COverturning Roe was just the opening act of a larger strategy to take women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and freedoms \u8230\'20\'26 We all must understand who is to blame. Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The ruling from the Arizona supreme court arrived on Tuesday, just days after a Florida supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/01/florida-abortion-right-constitution-vote-november-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  cleared the way for a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a decision that will cut off access to the procedure before many women even know they are pregnant. These back-to-back rulings roiled the United States, raising the already high stakes of the 2024 elections to towering new heights. Activists in both states are now at work on ballot measures that would ask voters to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their states\u8217\'20\'19 constitutions in November.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are hopeful these efforts \u8211\'20\'13 and the potential threat of more bans under a Trump administration \u8211\'20\'13 will mobilize voters in their favor, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are popular among Americans, and Republicans have spent years pushing restrictions. Democrats have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a central issue of their campaigns in Arizona, which was already expected to be a major battleground, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/florida-democrats-abortion-ban-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}\uc2 , a longtime election bellwether that has swung further to the right in recent years.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who is struggling to generate enthusiasm among voters, turning 2024 into a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be his best shot at defeating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But it remains an open question whether the backlash to Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning will continue to drive voters in a presidential election year, when they may be more swayed by concern over the economy and immigration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn public polls that might just ask: \u8216\'20\'18What\u8217\'20\'19s your most important issue?\u8217\'20\'19 You\u8217\'20\'19re going to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the middle, maybe even towards the bottom,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tresa Undem, a co-founder of the polling firm PerryUndem who has studied public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for two decades. \u8220\'20\'1CBut when you talk to core groups that Democrats need to turn out, it\u8217\'20\'19s front and center.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-biden-poll-swing-states-ad594acb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent Wall Street Journal poll}}}\uc2  found that Trump held double-digit leads when swing state voters were asked who would best handle the economy, inflation and immigration, but they trusted Biden more on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.foxnews.com/foxnews.com/content/uploads/2024/03/Fox_March-7-11-2024_Arizona_Topline_March-13-Release.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fox News poll in March}}}\uc2  found that most voters in Arizona believe Biden will do a better job handling the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it was less of a priority than the economy, election integrity and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 For Biden, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe best issue for him right now\u8221\'20\'1D, Undem said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of the data I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen on this upcoming election, young people are not nearly as motivated to vote as they were in 2020. And so in places like Arizona, the total ban \u8211\'20\'13 and I don\u8217\'20\'19t make predictions ever \u8211\'20\'13 I do think it is going to turn out young people, especially young women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign has released {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xhZIdq7ZJyc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -focused ads}}}\uc2  this week, including one that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H0BVeL_jzKE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 features a Texas woman}}}\uc2  who was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after her water broke too early in pregnancy. (She ended up in the ICU.) Indivisible, a national grassroots organization with a local presence in states across the country, said volunteer sign-ups to knock on doors in Arizona spiked 50% following the state supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. Its members in Arizona are helping to organize rallies in support of reproductive rights as well as events to collect signatures for the ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 When Roe fell, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\u8217\'20\'19 grip on voters was far from guaranteed. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Senate Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 longtime leader and an architect of the conservative supreme court majority that overturned Roe, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/12/1098484020/mitch-mcconnell-supreme-court-conservatives-abortion-midterms#:~:text=Abortion%20and%20the%20midterms&text=%22I%20think%20it%20will%20be,a%20wash%2C%22%20McConnell%20said."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brushed off outrage}}}\uc2  over its demise as \u8220\'20\'1Ca wash\u8221\'20\'1D in federal elections. Although most Americans support some degree of access to the procedure, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters were more likely to say the issue was important to their vote than pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights voters.\par \par \uc2 The fall of Roe changed that. Anger over Roe was credited with halting Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 much-promised \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D in the 2022 midterm elections, while pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures triumphed, even in crimson states such as Kansas and Kentucky. Last year, when Virginia Republicans tried retake control of the state legislature by championing a \u8220\'20\'1Ccompromise\u8221\'20\'1D 15 week-ban, they failed. Democrats now control both chambers in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen Republicans offer compromises, I think a lot of voters are inclined not to see those as what the Republican party really wants long-term but what the Republican party thinks is necessary to settle for in the short term,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a University of California at Davis School of Law professor who studies the legal history of reproduction. \u8220\'20\'1CThey know that Republicans are aligned with the pro-life movement and the pro-life movement wants fetal personhood and a ban at fertilization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the hours after the Arizona decision, several Republican state lawmakers and candidates with long records of opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rushed to denounce the near-total ban (which has not yet taken effect). The Republican Senate candidate Kari Lake, who once called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktar.com/story/5052440/heres-what-arizona-gubernatorial-candidate-kari-lake-says-about-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimate sin}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D and said Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s impending near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban was \u8220\'20\'1Ca great law\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/11/kari-lake-arizona-abortion-reversal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attempted to clarify}}}\uc2  her position on the issue in a meandering, five-minute-plus video. The ban she once favored \u8211\'20\'13 which passed in 1864, before Arizona even became a state or women gained the right to vote \u8211\'20\'13 is now \u8220\'20\'1Cout of line with where the people of this state are\u8221\'20\'1D, Lake said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe issue is less about banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more about saving babies,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, as instrumental music swelled against images of pregnant women and pregnancy tests. She repeatedly stressed the importance of \u8220\'20\'1Cchoice\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 language associated with people who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 while simultaneously invoking the value of \u8220\'20\'1Clife\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Lake also emphasized that she \u8220\'20\'1Cagrees with President Trump\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Over the course of his campaign, Trump has alternated between taking credit for overturning Roe \u8211\'20\'13 since he appointed three of the justices who ruled to do so \u8211\'20\'13 toying with the idea of a national ban, and insisting that states can decide their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/08/trump-abortion-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he did in a video this week}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In that video, released on Monday, Trump declined to endorse a federal ban on the procedure, after months of teasing his support. On Wednesday, Trump criticized the Arizona law and predicted that state lawmakers would \u8220\'20\'1Cbring it back into reason\u8221\'20\'1D. Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban, he suggested, was \u8220\'20\'1Cprobably, maybe going to change\u8221\'20\'1D. He reiterated his criticism on Friday, posting on his social media platform that the Arizona supreme court went \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo far\u8221\'20\'1D in upholding an \u8220\'20\'1Cinappropriate law from 1864\u8221\'20\'1D and calling on the Republican-led state legislature to \u8220\'20\'1CACT IMMEDIATELY\u8221\'20\'1D to remedy the decision. \u8220\'20\'1CWe must ideally have the three Exceptions for Rape, Incest, and Life of the Mother,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. (The 1864 ban only includes an exception to save the life of the pregnant person.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s simply trying to have it, I think, both ways,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said of Trump.\par \par \uc2 Come November, Democrats are counting on the real-world consequences of the bans overriding other concerns. \u8220\'20\'1CThe economy is still important. Immigration is still important, but this is immediate,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stacy Pearson, an Arizona-based Democratic strategist.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA woman just wants to be in her OB-GYN\u8217\'20\'19s office, having a conversation with her doctor about her medical care without concerns about whether or not old white men in cowboy hats were right in 1864,\u8221\'20\'1D Pearson added. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s nuts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240413ek4d001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
