\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  blames \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for Arizona  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -ban debacle in speech\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 640 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2024\par 23:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They\u8217\'20\'19ve turned the clock to the 1800s\u8217\'20\'19, said the US vice-president as she excoriated ex-president as \u8216\'20\'18architect of healthcare crisis\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pinned the blame for Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban squarely on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who she described as the \u8220\'20\'1Carchitect of this healthcare crisis\u8221\'20\'1D in a speech at a campaign event in Tucson on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The state was left reeling after the Arizona supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  earlier this week that a civil war-era law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state with almost no exceptions is now enforceable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere in Arizona they have turned back the clock to the 1800s to take away a woman\u8217\'20\'19s most fundamental right \u8211\'20\'13 the right to make decisions about her own body,\u8221\'20\'1D the vice-president said, adding that women in the state now \u8220\'20\'1Clive under one of the most extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in our nation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe overturning of Roe was, without any question, a seismic event and this ban here in Arizona is one of the biggest aftershocks yet,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The decision in Arizona will have major implications for reproductive healthcare across the US south-west and will likely make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a defining issue of the 2024 race. Republicans in the state, who cheered the overturning of Roe v Wade and once passionately supported a ban, including the far-right Senate candidate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/11/kari-lake-arizona-abortion-reversal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kari Lake}}}\uc2 , have suddenly backpedalled.\par \par \uc2 The ban was made possible by Trump, Harris argued, who had said he is \u8220\'20\'1Cproudly\u8221\'20\'1D responsible for the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning}}}\uc2  Roe v Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFormer president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this. During his campaign in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said women should be punished for seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s ban is a new inflection point, Harris said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has demonstrated once and for all that overturning Roe was just the opening act of a larger strategy to take women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and freedoms, part of a full-on attack, state by state, on reproductive freedom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The law in Arizona, which predates its statehood, does not provide exceptions for rape or incest and abortions are permitted only if the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Trump, like other high-profile Republicans, has argued Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s court went too far: \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19ll be straightened out, and as you know, it\u8217\'20\'19s all about states\u8217\'20\'19 rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lake, who had previously expressed her support for the ban and even cited the number of the 1864 code, said this week that she agrees with Trump and has urged lawmakers to repeal it.\par \par \uc2 Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US has been at record highs. Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Republican lawmakers, with their sudden changes of heart, seem to be aware that the state\u8217\'20\'19s drastic ban is deeply unpopular among voters and could have repercussions in November.\par \par \uc2 Among those who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-against-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 came out against}}}\uc2  the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision was Republican state representative Matt Gress. \u8220\'20\'1CThis decision cannot stand,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gress. \u8220\'20\'1CI categorically reject rolling back the clock to a time when slavery was still legal and we could lock up women and doctors because of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gress had tried to bring forth a bill to repeal the ban but then voted with other Republicans to move to recess.\par \par \uc2 The law does not have the support of Arizonans, Lake said in a video on Thursday. Political analysts have said that the near-total ban would help draw moderate voters to Democrats while mobilizing young voters and voters of color.\par \par \uc2 The ban has fired up Democrats, who have already made Arizona a top priority in 2024 and hope the issue will mobilize voters in the state and across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona, this November up and down the ballot reproductive freedom is at stake, and you have the power to protect it with your vote,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240412ek4c0048u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
