\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 For the future of US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights under a second Trump presidency, look to Arizona\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Sullivan \par \uc2 876 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2024\par 12:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans haven\u8217\'20\'19t \u8216\'20\'18moderated\u8217\'20\'19 their extreme stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but much of the media is eager to give that impressionGet Margaret Sullivan\u8217\'20\'19s latest columns delivered straight to your inbox\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, in 2024 America, you have to pinch yourself to make sure you\u8217\'20\'19re not in a long-running dystopian nightmare. Then again, maybe we all are. And no amount of pinching will help.\par \par \uc2 Two scenes from this week stand out.\par \par \uc2 One, thoroughly bizarre, was on the floor of the Arizona senate, where \u8211\'20\'13 led by a Republican state senator, Anthony Kern - a fundamentalist Christian prayer group \u8220\'20\'1Cspoke in tongues\u8221\'20\'1D as they knelt together over the state seal, praying for a civil war-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to become law again. Kern and the group got their wish; a day later, the Arizona supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  to allow the law to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Kern, naturally, is one of those under investigation for falsely claiming to be an Arizona elector as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to overturn the 2020 election. He also got an Arizona bill passed allowing the Ten Commandments to be posted and read out loud in the state\u8217\'20\'19s public school classrooms. If you had any lofty notions about the separation of church and state, consider them laid to rest.\par \par \uc2 The other memorable scene was on the \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  Show on the Fox Business channel, as three middle-aged white guys {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/acyn/status/1777799954339188880?s=46"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kicked around}}}\uc2  the aforementioned ruling by the Arizona supreme court. That 4-2 decision reinvigorates a 160-year-old law that says virtually all abortions are felonies. On the broadcast, radio host Mark Simone was blithe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBuying a bus ticket to go somewhere to get it is not the worst thing in the world,\u8221\'20\'1D Simone \u8211\'20\'13 someone who will never be in that situation and apparently lacks the empathy to imagine it \u8211\'20\'13 opined.\par \par \uc2 The bus-ticket solution might not even be an option. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is elected again, a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is far from unlikely.\par \par \uc2 Just a day before the Arizona ruling, the former US president came out with his long-promised, supposedly new stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, trying to spin up a moderate position. Declining to address whether he would support a national ban, he merely bragged about his role in the demise of Roe v Wade and suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would now be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/08/trump-abortion-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 up to the states}}}\uc2 , skipping over the obvious reality that they already are.\par \par \uc2 He also blatantly lied about various things, like how Democrats think it\u8217\'20\'19s fine to execute babies and how the entire spectrum of legal experts agreed that Roe should be overturned.\par \par \uc2 Too many in the mainstream media swallowed this whole, at least in all-important headlines, presenting Trump\u8217\'20\'19s position not only as news but as a politically savvy move toward the center.\par \par \uc2 But something more like the truth was available if you turned your gaze from Washington to Arizona, where, in a matter of days, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers can be sentenced to multiple years in prison for providing medical care.\par \par \uc2 Some saw the meaning clearly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis decision should serve as a warning for the rest of the country,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2024/04/arizona-abortion-ban-trump-wins-2024-comstock-lan.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  lawyers Dahlia Lithwick and Mark Joseph Stern on Slate. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the hands of a far-right court, a dead, openly misogynistic, wildly unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban can spring back to life with a vengeance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 How it all will play out is unclear. Since Roe was overturned, voters have expressed their displeasure. Pro-choice measures have carried the day in state after state, including some bright-red ones like Kentucky and Kansas. Next up is Florida, where voters will decide in November whether to override a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with one that allows access until 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Americans in the rightwing media bubble may not hear much about the Arizona ruling. Fox News gave it a mere 12 minutes on Tuesday (as opposed to two hours across eight shows on CNN), according to Media Matters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mediamatters.org/fox-news/fox-news-buries-court-decision-implementing-near-total-abortion-ban-arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2 , and none of Fox\u8217\'20\'19s big-name opinion hosts addressed it on their evening shows. Apparently, the highest priority is getting the cult leader elected again.\par \par \uc2 The draconian decision in Arizona has the potential to deliver at least one swing state \u8211\'20\'13 maybe more \u8211\'20\'13 to Joe Biden. As my colleague at Columbia Journalism School, professor Bill Grueskin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bgrueskin/status/1777757151764570112?s=46"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quipped}}}\uc2  Tuesday: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not too early for the Fox News decision desk to call Arizona for Biden.\u8221\'20\'1D (Fox famously made that controversial \u8211\'20\'13 though accurate \u8211\'20\'13 call on election night 2020, much to team Trump\u8217\'20\'19s angry displeasure.)\par \par \uc2 Contradictions abound. Trump, having unleashed the dogs on longstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with his supreme court appointments, is simultaneously taking credit for that, and denying that it could go any further. The rightwing media protects him; the mainstream media lets him portray his position as moderate and somehow consistent with the public\u8217\'20\'19s preferences.\par \par \uc2 As for non-politicians, particularly women of child-bearing age, the reality could get much, much worse.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a mess. But that\u8217\'20\'19s life in our national nightmare. Let\u8217\'20\'19s hope enough Americans wake up by November to reverse some of the damage.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Margaret Sullivan is a Guardian US columnist writing on media, politics and culture\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240411ek4b001eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
