\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Stephen Colbert on Republicans and  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans: \u8216\'20\'18Backpedaling like a tweaked-out unicycle chimp\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff \par \uc2 846 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2024\par 16:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Late-night hosts discuss Arizona re-enacting a 160-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to appear more moderate\par \par \uc2 Late-night hosts talked Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s 1864 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s backpedaling on overturning Roe v Wade and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s state dinner with the prime minister of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Colbert\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/stephen-colbert"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stephen Colbert}}}\uc2  didn\u8217\'20\'19t mince words when it came to the Arizona supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting to reinstate a 160-year-old \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThat is crazy,\u8221\'20\'1D the Late Show host said on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CBut remember, it\u8217\'20\'19s Arizona, so it\u8217\'20\'19s a dry crazy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court ruled that because Roe v Wade had been overturned, there was nothing stopping them from enforcing the 1864 law, which was passed before women had the right to vote. \u8220\'20\'1CTo which the Arizona supreme court said, \u8216\'20\'18don\u8217\'20\'19t worry \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19ll work on that one next,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert joked.\par \par \uc2 The law is so old, in fact, that it predates the invention of the cowboy hat, the urinal, the paper clip, the machine that makes paper bags and Arizona as a state. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t enforce state laws from before it was a state!\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert exclaimed. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you could, people in Massachusetts could be arrested for failure to buckle hat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The ruling, by an all-Republican court, is expected to backfire at the ballot box, where it will probably face a referendum this November. Since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in June 2022, \u8220\'20\'1Cthis ruling has been a disaster for all the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 attempts to keep suburban voters,\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert explained. \u8220\'20\'1CSo GOP candidates are now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-against-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backpedaling}}}\uc2  like a tweaked-out unicycle chimp.\par \par \uc2 The far-right Arizona Senate candidate Kari Lake, for example, announced she opposed the ruling and called on the legislature to \u8220\'20\'1Ccome up with an immediate commonsense solution that Arizonans can support\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhich is a surprising position to take,\u8221\'20\'1D Colbert noted, because before the Dobbs decision, Lake bragged that Arizona \u8220\'20\'1Calready has great legislation on the books\u8221\'20\'1D so that \u8220\'20\'1CArizona will not be a state where abortions are happening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Seth Meyers\par \par \uc2 On Late Night, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/seth-meyers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Seth Meyers}}}\uc2  blasted Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to moderate his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after securing the end of Roe. \u8220\'20\'1CThere could be no escaping accountability. Trump told everyone he would overturn Roe, he nominated three justices who overturned Roe, and then he bragged about overturning Roe,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNormally, it would be very hard to pin something on Trump because he\u8217\'20\'19s so good at wriggling his way out of everything, but this is as straightforward as it gets,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like one of those conspiracy theory boards with one string going straight from Trump to Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd yet Trump is now trying to trick everyone into thinking he\u8217\'20\'19s actually a moderate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  who opposes a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D he added, after Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgrotesque\u8221\'20\'1D ruling reinvigorating a near total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from 1864. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the fuck \u8211\'20\'13 states can enforce laws written before they were even states?!\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers fumed. \u8220\'20\'1CDoes that mean any state in the Louisiana Purchase is now subject to the laws of 18th-century France?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is exactly the situation Trump said he supports \u8211\'20\'13 leaving it up to the states,\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers said. Indeed, after the ruling, Trump told reporters: \u8220\'20\'1CAs you know it\u8217\'20\'19s all about states\u8217\'20\'19 rights, that\u8217\'20\'19ll be straightened out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat do you mean it will get straightened out?! You broke it! This is your fault,\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers retorted. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had a landmark supreme court ruling in place that protected the health and bodily autonomy of all Americans, and you undid it. You don\u8217\'20\'19t get to clog a toilet at a party and then say \u8216\'20\'18I think we can all agree someone else will fix it.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\par \par \uc2 And in Los Angeles, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2024/apr/05/jimmy-kimmel-trump-hush-money-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel}}}\uc2  recapped \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s state dinner with the prime minister of Japan and his wife. \u8220\'20\'1CHis state dinners are a little different than usual,\u8221\'20\'1D Kimmel joked. \u8220\'20\'1CFor one thing, they start at 4.30, and you can only get in if you have a \uc2 Groupon\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The dinner, which was in honor of the Washington cherry trees gifted by Japan in 1912, was graced with entertainment by Paul Simon. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s big. Trump was lucky to get the surviving members of O-Town for one of his state dinners, but Japan got an evening of Simon and Joe-funkel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kimmel.\par \par \uc2 Trump, meanwhile, \u8220\'20\'1Ctook a break from trying to derail his porn star hush-money trial\u8221\'20\'1D to make a surprise stop at a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  in Atlanta. \u8220\'20\'1CHe likes to go in to fast-food restaurants and buy everyone food like a big shot,\u8221\'20\'1D Kimmel noted. \u8220\'20\'1CHe bought 30 milkshakes and some chicken and then started passing it around.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump also took the time to proclaim that he \u8220\'20\'1Cdid everything\u8221\'20\'1D to help historically Black colleges and universities (HBCUs). \u8220\'20\'1CJust a former president muttering, \u8216\'20\'18I did everything, I did everything,\u8217\'20\'19 to himself at a fast-food chicken restaurant,\u8221\'20\'1D Kimmel laughed. \u8220\'20\'1CNothing unusual there!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240411ek4b00233\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
