\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arizona Republicans denounce revived 1864  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban in sudden reversal\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman and Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 1,055 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2024\par 13:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the party struggles to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , response marks fastest and strongest rebuke of bans since the fall of Roe\par \par \uc2 Hours after Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court declared on Tuesday that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 160-year-old \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban is now enforceable}}}\uc2 , Republicans in the state took a surprising stance for a party that has historically championed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 they denounced the decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis decision cannot stand,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matt Gress, a Republican state representative. \u8220\'20\'1CI categorically reject rolling back the clock to a time when slavery was still legal and we could lock up women and doctors because of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 First passed when Arizona was still a territory, the ban only permits abortions to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life and does not have exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s Arizona supreme court decision reinstating an Arizona territorial-era ban on all abortions from more than 150 years ago is disappointing to say the least,\u8221\'20\'1D said TJ Shope, a Republican state senator.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI oppose today\u8217\'20\'19s ruling,\u8221\'20\'1D added Kari Lake, a Republican running to represent Arizona in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loyalist. Lake called on the state legislature to \u8220\'20\'1Ccome up with an immediate commonsense solution that Arizonans can support\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, leading the GOP to stumble in the 2022 midterms and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to win a string of ballot measures, including in purple and red states, Republicans have struggled to find a way to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without turning off voters. But their response to the ruling on the 1864 ban may mark their fastest and strongest rebuke of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans since Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an earthquake that has never been seen in Arizona politics,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barrett Marson, a Republican consultant in Arizona, of the decision. \u8220\'20\'1CThis will shake the ground under every Republican candidate, even those in safe legislative or congressional seats.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The 1864 ban is not currently in effect, and may not go into effect for weeks due to legal delays. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently allowed in Arizona up until 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some of the criticisms of the Tuesday ruling came from politicians who had previously supported the 1864 ban or cheered the end of Roe v Wade. Lake previously called the ban a \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat law\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2022/oct/12/katie-hobbs/az-gop-governor-hopeful-lake-called-near-total-abo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to PolitiFact}}}\uc2 . \uc2 David Schweikert\uc2 , an Arizona congressman who is facing one of the most competitive House races in the country this November, said on Tuesday that he does not support the ruling and wants the state legislature to \u8220\'20\'1Caddress this issue immediately\u8221\'20\'1D, but in 2022 said the fall of Roe \u8220\'20\'1Cpleased\u8221\'20\'1D him.\par \par \uc2 The speaker of the Arizona state house and the president of the state senate, who are both Republicans, also released a joint statement saying that they would be \u8220\'20\'1Clistening to our constituents to determine the best course of action for the legislature\u8221\'20\'1D. In contrast, on the day Roe fell, the Republican-controlled state senate released a statement declaring that the 1864 ban was in effect immediately. That statement unleashed confusion and chaos among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Arizona, prompting them to stop offering the procedure out of an abundance of caution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are trying to play it both ways. They\u8217\'20\'19re trying to have this illusion that they\u8217\'20\'19re moderate to get votes, because they know that Arizonans do not want a total ban,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Gabrielle Goodrick, one of the providers who temporarily stopped performing abortions when Roe fell. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is just ridiculous. Now they\u8217\'20\'19re saying that they oppose it? Yeah, yeah \u8211\'20\'13 a little too late.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arizona is one of roughly a dozen states where voters may be able to directly decide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights come November. Activists in the state have now collected more than half a million signatures in favor of giving Arizona residents a chance to vote on a ballot measure that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hope that turnout for this proposal, which has yet to be officially added to the ballot, will also lead to surge in support for their candidates, including Joe Biden. A similar dynamic is at play in Florida, whose state supreme court recently paved the way for a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and where voters will be able to vote in November to constitutionally protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision also exposed the deepening rift between Republicans and their longtime allies in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. As Arizona Republicans raced to distance themselves from the long-dormant law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents cheered the decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe celebrate the Arizona supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision that allows the state\u8217\'20\'19s pro-life law to again protect the lives of countless, innocent unborn children,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jake Warner, a senior counsel with \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , who argued the case before the court in favor of the ban.\par \par \uc2 Trump, the presumptive Republican presidential nominee who disappointed religious conservatives on Monday when he said states should {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/08/trump-abortion-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decide their own \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  laws}}}\uc2 , did not immediately weigh in on the Arizona ruling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are popular in Arizona: nearly one-third of Arizona voters in the 2022 midterm elections said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the issue that mattered most in helping them decide who to vote for, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Elections/arizona-exit-polls-2022-us-governor-election-results-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  exit polling}}}\uc2 . By a two to one margin, voters in the state said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, and 40% said they felt \u8220\'20\'1Cangry\u8221\'20\'1D about the supreme court decision ending the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A poll conducted in late February by the Phoenix-based firm, Noble Predictive Insights, found that 40% of Arizona voters expected Trump, if elected, to attempt to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether, while 45% expected Biden, if re-elected, to increase access.\par \par \uc2 The vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , will go to Arizona later this week, in a visit that was planned ahead of the Tuesday decision. She blamed the impending state ban on Trump, whose three supreme court appointees voted to eliminate the federally guaranteed right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona just rolled back the clock to a time before women could vote \u8211\'20\'13 and, by his own admission, there\u8217\'20\'19s one person responsible: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris said in a campaign statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThe alarm is sounding for every woman in America: if he has the opportunity, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would sign off on a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240410ek4a001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
