\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump says Arizona supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling will be \u8216\'20\'18straightened out\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly in Washington \par \uc2 898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par 20:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ex-president who boasts about Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall say matter is \u8216\'20\'18about states\u8217\'20\'19 rights\u8217\'20\'19 but concedes revived 160-year-old ban goes too far\par \par \uc2 Insisting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/trump-arizona-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  should be left to state governments, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nonetheless said the rightwing Arizona supreme court went too far when it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/trump-arizona-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled on Tuesday}}}\uc2  that a 160-year-old near-total ban could be enforced.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYeah, they did [go too far],\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said on Wednesday to reporters at an airport in Atlanta, Georgia. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19ll be straightened out, and as you know it\u8217\'20\'19s all about states\u8217\'20\'19 rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Only on Monday, a day before the Arizona court said the 1864 ban could go into effect, Trump issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/08/trump-abortion-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lengthy statement}}}\uc2  seeking to stake out his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Such rights should be a matter for state governments, he said, while refusing to back rightwing calls for a national ban, a stance which has angered some close allies.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s political opponents were swift to point out that he regularly boasts about being the architect of Dobbs v Jackson, the 2022 supreme court ruling in which three \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices he appointed voted to bring down Roe v Wade, the 1973 ruling which protected federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and other Democrats repeatedly point to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s role in making the Dobbs ruling possible, thereby triggering draconian state bans including the one in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in his words with reporters on Wednesday, Trump said authorities in Arizona would \u8220\'20\'1Cbring it back into reason and that it will be taken care of, I think, very quickly\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is a swing state, key to the looming presidential and congressional elections. Though the Arizona supreme court is controlled by the right, the state\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , and attorney general, Kris Mayes, are Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Mayes decried \u8220\'20\'1Can existential crisis for our residents\u8221\'20\'1D and told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/all-in/watch/arizona-ag-says-she-won-t-prosecute-women-doctors-under-draconian-abortion-ban-208666693703"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NBC}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CMillions of Arizonans \u8211\'20\'13 men and women, Republicans, Democrats, independents \u8211\'20\'13 woke up this morning to a decision that drags us back to 1864 \u8230\'20\'26 I have said I will not prosecute anyone under this draconian law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, Trump claimed \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s the will of the people\u8221\'20\'1D that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be left to the states. But national public opinion remains heavily in favour of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a winning campaign issue for Democrats since federal rights were removed.\par \par \uc2 Since Dobbs, Democrats have enjoyed significant election wins while campaigning on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and ballot measures safeguarding such rights have been approved, even in Republican-run states. More such measures are due to be decided this year.\par \par \uc2 Leading Arizona Republicans including Kari Lake, a Trump-backed election denier now running for \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/arizona-republicans-against-state-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backed away from}}}\uc2  the state supreme court decision.\par \par \uc2 On the national stage, Trump must walk a political tightrope, needing to satisfy hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners, dominant in his Republican base, while appealing to moderates and independents.\par \par \uc2 His personal views on the subject are a matter of constant speculation. In 2016, running for president and under fire for suggesting women who received abortions should be punished, he was asked by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  columnist Maureen Dowd if he had ever been involved with anyone who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSuch an interesting question,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/apr/02/donald-trump-marueen-dowd-interview-abortion-past-partners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump said.}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CSo what\u8217\'20\'19s your next question?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eight years on, in Georgia, Trump was asked if doctors should be punished for performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll let that be to the states,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou know everything we\u8217\'20\'19re doing now is states and states\u8217\'20\'19 rights and what we wanted to do is get it back to the states because for 53 years [actually 51 since Roe] it\u8217\'20\'19s been a fight and now the states are handling it and some have handled it very well and the others will end up handling it very well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign communications director, Michael Tyler, issued a statement in response.\par \par \uc2 He said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  owns the suffering and chaos happening right now, including in Arizona, because he proudly overturned Roe \u8211\'20\'13 something he called \u8216\'20\'18an incredible thing\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18pretty amazing\u8217\'20\'19 just today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump lies constantly \u8211\'20\'13 about everything \u8211\'20\'13 but has one track record: banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  every chance he gets. The guy who wants to be a dictator on day one will use every tool at his disposal to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, with or without Congress, and running away from reporters to his private jet like a coward doesn\u8217\'20\'19t change that reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amid widespread incredulity and alarm over the Arizona supreme court decision, the Boston College historian Heather Cox Richardson pointed out in her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://heathercoxrichardson.substack.com/p/april-9-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Letters from an American newsletter}}}\uc2  that the law concerned, which \u8220\'20\'1Cwill begin to be enforced in 14 days\u8221\'20\'1D, was \u8220\'20\'1Cwritten by a single man in 1864\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn 1864,\u8221\'20\'1D Cox Richardson wrote, \u8220\'20\'1CArizona was not a state, women and minorities could not vote, and doctors were still sewing up wounds with horsehair and storing their unwashed medical instruments in velvet-lined cases. And, of course, the United States was in the midst of the civil war.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 The law that Arizona will use to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care seemed designed to keep men in the chaos of the civil war from inflicting damage on others \u8211\'20\'13 including pregnant women \u8211\'20\'13 rather than to police women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive care \u8230\'20\'26 Written to police the behavior of men, the code tells a larger story about power and control.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240410ek4a003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
