\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says each US state should determine different  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore, Lauren Gambino and Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par 01:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a Truth Social post outlining his view of reproductive rights, former US president declined to endorse a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  on Monday announced his belief that individual US states should decide the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining to endorse a national ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The former president\u8217\'20\'19s stated position dashed hopes of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, who want a federal ban, and drew the ire of Democrats, who blame Trump for outright bans and severe restrictions already in place across the south and midwest.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s announcement had been highly anticipated by Democrats, who believe the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights helps them at the polls, and Republicans, who failed to push Trump to endorse a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban during their party\u8217\'20\'19s primary.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/112235238031827342"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a four-minute video post}}}\uc2  on Truth Social, Trump said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cup to the states to do the right thing\u8221\'20\'1D while also touting his work to confirm the conservative supreme court justices who ultimately overturned Roe v Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates will determine by vote or legislation, or perhaps both,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump, the presumptive Republican presidential nominee, said in the videotaped address. \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever they decide must be the law of the land, or in this case the law of the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany states will be different, many will have a different number of weeks, some will be more conservative than others,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued. \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day this is all about the will of the people. You must follow your heart, or in many cases your religion or faith.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo what\u8217\'20\'19s right for your family, and do what\u8217\'20\'19s right for yourself,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  seized on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s announcement, saying Trump had \u8220\'20\'1Cmade it clear once again today that he is \u8211\'20\'13 more than anyone in America \u8211\'20\'13 the person responsible for ending Roe v Wade\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump is scrambling. He\u8217\'20\'19s worried that since he\u8217\'20\'19s the one responsible for overturning Roe the voters will hold him accountable in 2024. Well, I have news for Donald. They will,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In a press call, a spokesperson for the Biden-Harris campaign said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and severe restrictions are \u8220\'20\'1Cthe reality facing women in this country because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade\u8221\'20\'1D, and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cowns the state of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in our country\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The campaign promised to restore federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point of viability, or when a fetus can live outside the womb, if Biden is re-elected. Viability is generally considered at 24 weeks gestation. A full term pregnancy is 39 weeks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s comments today are an endorsement of the nightmare we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing play out in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 21 states with \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kaitlyn Kash, who was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas in spite of severe abnormalities in the fetus she was carrying.\par \par \uc2 At a 13-week ultrasound, Kash found out her fetus had skeletal dysplasia, a condition that meant her child\u8217\'20\'19s bones would be crushed in utero. If she gave birth, she was told, the baby would likely suffocate soon afterward.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is unthinkable to me that anyone could cheer on the cruel \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that threaten women\u8217\'20\'19s lives,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kash, who was forced to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state. She is one of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/zurawski-v-texas-plaintiffs-stories-11-14-23/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several women suing Texas}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For more than a year Trump has declined to say when in a pregnancy he would try to draw the line, even as Republican-led states have ushered in a wave of new restrictions and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups pressured him and other Republican presidential candidates to endorse a federal ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In his statement, Trump did not say whether he would sign into law a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if he were president and Congress passed a federal limit. Neither did he say how he, as a resident of Florida, would vote on a ballot measure that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into that state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Senior Republican senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  declared his opposition to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s position Monday, saying that leaving the issue entirely to the states \u8220\'20\'1Cruns contrary to an American consensus that would limit late-term abortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will continue to advocate that there should be a national minimum standard limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks because the child is capable of feeling pain, with exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother,\u8221\'20\'1D Graham said.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Monday, Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of SBA Pro-Life America, one of the most powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in the country, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sbaprolife.org/newsroom/press-releases/leading-pro-life-group-responds-president-trump-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  she was \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply disappointed\u8221\'20\'1D in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s position.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnborn children and their mothers deserve national protections and national advocacy from the brutality of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that allowing states to decide the issue \u8220\'20\'1Ccedes the national debate to the Democrats\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dannenfelser had previously warned that her group would not support any candidate who refused to support a national ban. On Monday, she said her group would \u8220\'20\'1Cwork tirelessly\u8221\'20\'1D to defeat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in November.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, who have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central issue of the election, said Trump supported laws in the more than two dozens states that have imposed outright bans or restrictions on the procedure since Roe v Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is endorsing every single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the states, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with no exceptions,\u8221\'20\'1D Ammar Moussa, director of rapid response for the Biden campaign, wrote on X. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd he\u8217\'20\'19s bragging about his role in creating this hellscape.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the video, Trump thanked the six conservative supreme court justices \u8211\'20\'13 three of whom he appointed as president \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cfor having the courage to allow this long-term, hard fought battle to finally end\u8221\'20\'1D by striking down Roe v Wade nearly 50 years after it was decided.\par \par \uc2 He also sought to portray Democrats as \u8220\'20\'1Cradical\u8221\'20\'1D for opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access restrictions. But he simultaneously blamed Republicans for being too extreme on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Since the 2022 ruling, Democrats and pro-choice voters have won big at the ballot box, including in conservative states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have proved unpopular. Meanwhile public polling has consistently found that a majority of Americans generally favor access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In articulating his position, Trump said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly in favor of exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CYou must follow your heart on this issue but remember you must also win elections to restore our culture and, in fact, to save our country,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Trump emphasized his support for in vitro fertilization (IVF), after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/29/alabama-embryo-ivf-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February court ruling in Alabama}}}\uc2  declared frozen embryos people in a decision that threatened to shut down fertility treatments in the state. The decision sparked widespread backlash and Republicans raced to distance themselves from it, even though some in Congress have supported legislation that would recognize embryos as humans entitled to legal protections. Trump praised the Republican-dominated Alabama state legislature for passing a law protecting IVF after the outcry. Reproductive medicine specialists said the law did not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 Trump has long argued that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision overturning Roe gave those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8220\'20\'1Ctremendous power to negotiate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said he wanted to use that leverage to strike a deal that he hoped would \u8220\'20\'1Cmake both sides happy\u8221\'20\'1D and bring the country \u8220\'20\'1Ctogether\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 even though the issue is one of the most contentious in American politics, with opponents viewing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a form of murder and proponents seeing it as a fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 Trump suggested last month in a radio interview that he was leaning toward supporting a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at about 15 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 which is early in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe number of weeks now, people are agreeing on 15. And I\u8217\'20\'19m thinking in terms of that,\u8221\'20\'1D he said on \uc2 WABC radio\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19ll come out to something that\u8217\'20\'19s very reasonable. But people are really, even hard-liners are agreeing, seems to be, 15 weeks seems to be a number that people are agreeing at.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Trump seemed reluctant to embrace a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody agrees \u8211\'20\'13 you\u8217\'20\'19ve heard this for years, all the legal scholars on both sides agree: it\u8217\'20\'19s a state issue. It shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be a federal issue, it\u8217\'20\'19s a state issue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, Trump has been ambivalent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He routinely takes credit for appointing the supreme court justices who set the stage for the elimination of Roe v Wade, which he has called a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral and unconstitutional atrocity\u8221\'20\'1D. He has also called himself the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost pro-life president in American history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But he has repeatedly dismissed as too extreme fellow Republicans who oppose exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape, incest and when the life of the pregnant person is at risk. And he has said being too hard-line on the issue cost Republicans at the polls in the 2022 midterms and could do so again when he challenges Biden in November\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240408ek48001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
