\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Extreme\u8217\'20\'19 US anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  group ramps up lobbying in Westminster\b0\par\par\uc2 Shanti Das, Home affairs correspondent \par \uc2 1,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2024\par 01:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The UK branch of the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  has increased its spending and is forging ties with key MPs\par \par \uc2 A rightwing Christian lobby group that wants \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be banned has forged ties with an adviser to the prime minister and is drawing up \u173\'00\'ADpolicy briefings for politicians.\par \par \uc2 The UK branch of the US-based {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/19/alliance-defending-freedom-lgbtq-rights-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom (ADF)}}}\uc2  has more than doubled its spending since 2020 and been appointed a stakeholder in a parliamentary group on religious freedoms in a role that grants it direct access to MPs.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ADF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s efforts to boost its UK influence are revealed as part of an Observer analysis that shows a surge in activity within the wider anti-\u173\'00\'ADabortion movement.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/feb/23/mps-to-get-free-vote-on-decriminalising-abortion-in-england-and-wales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historic vote on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  later this spring, in which MPs will vote on a law that would abolish the criminal offence associated with a woman ending her own pregnancy in England and Wales, several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign groups have expanded their teams, ramped up advertising and coordinated mass letter-writing campaigns targeting MPs.\par \par \uc2 The findings have led to calls for greater transparency and accountability over the groups\u8217\'20\'19 funding and lobbying activities. The \uc2 ADF\uc2  in particular is an influential player on the US Christian right and part of a global network of hardline {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/02/us-anti-abortion-groups-uk-far-right"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 evangelical groups that were a driving force}}}\uc2  behind the repeal of Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 the supreme court ruling that gave women the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was overturned in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The group \u8211\'20\'13 which also supports {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/fighting-hate/extremist-files/group/alliance-defending-freedom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlawing sexual acts}}}\uc2  between consenting LGBTQ+ adults and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/30/christian-hate-group-funding-us-anti-lgbtq-anti-abortion-organizations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funds US fringe groups}}}\uc2  attacking gay, trans and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 has faced claims its funding is not transparent due to its use of donor advised funds: a loophole in US charity law that allows people to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/5050/donor-advised-funds-daf-us-charity-law-loophole-bankroll-hate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 give millions anonymously}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest financial accounts for its UK entity ADF International UK, published last week, show it spent almost \u163\'00\'A31m in the year to June 2023, up from \u163\'00\'A3392,556 in 2020, and that its income almost doubled between 2022 and 2023, from \u163\'00\'A3553,823 to \u163\'00\'A31,068,552.\par \par \uc2 ADF International UK, which has argued publicly against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/C33Bl0PvLOY/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decriminalising \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , has sought to develop closer relationships with MPs. Its latest accounts show a focus of its UK activity has been attempting to engage with \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant decision-makers\u8221\'20\'1D and that staff provided \u8220\'20\'1Cbriefing material and legal analysis\u8221\'20\'1D to several MPs ahead of a vote on introducing buffer zones to prevent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 In September 2023 it spent \u163\'00\'A31,737.92 flying the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s special envoy on freedom of religion and beliefs, \uc2 Fiona Bruce\uc2  MP, paying for her hotel and travel to attend an unspecified conference. Last month Bruce \u8211\'20\'13 who reports directly to \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 appeared at an event {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adfinternational.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/20240308-HRC55-Side-Event-Concept-Note.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sponsored by ADF International}}}\uc2  on religious freedom, speaking remotely alongside two members of the charity.\par \par \uc2 Number 10 did not respond to questions about the links between the \uc2 ADF\uc2  and Bruce, who declared the donations in the MPs register of interests and previously voted against legalising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and same-sex marriage in Northern Ireland. Calls and emails to her office went unanswered late last week.\par \par \uc2 Heidi Beirich, co-founder of the Global Project Against Hate and Extremism, said the \uc2 ADF\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cramped up its spending\u8221\'20\'1D in the UK and Europe \u8220\'20\'1Caggressively\u8221\'20\'1D in recent years and that there was \u8220\'20\'1Cno transparency\u8221\'20\'1D around \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere the money\u8217\'20\'19s actually coming from\u8221\'20\'1D. She said its relationship with MPs raised \u8220\'20\'1Chuge concerns\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy are politicians openly working with an organisation that has such a hateful agenda?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rose Whiffen, senior research officer at Transparency International UK, said the donations to Bruce raised questions about conflicts of interest and that her association with the group could give it credibility in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Copson, chief executive of Humanists UK, said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery concerning\u8221\'20\'1D that the UK\u8217\'20\'19s envoy on religious freedoms was \u8220\'20\'1Caccepting donations from organisations that use religious liberty as a way of denying others their human rights\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Christian nationalist movement is increasingly investing in the UK on a number of fronts, and all supporters of freedom and choice should take seriously the threat to human rights that this represents,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 ADF International UK said it was committed to protecting \u8220\'20\'1Cliberties dear to the British people\u8221\'20\'1D including free speech and freedom of religion, and that its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect the lives of both mother and baby in every pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CLike much of the British public, we are concerned about political initiatives to further liberalise \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson added.\par \par \uc2 The charity, which has an office in Westminster, said it received funds from many countries, like \u8220\'20\'1Cmany UK charities on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate\u8221\'20\'1D; that claims it was not transparent about its funders were \u8220\'20\'1Cbaseless\u8221\'20\'1D and that it complied with all charity regulations. It did not comment on its link to the PM\u8217\'20\'19s special envoy.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Lord, co-chair of the British Society of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Providers and a consultant gynaecologist, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve known for some time that these extreme groups from America are infiltrating the UK, having been emboldened following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s actions removing women\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there. However the scale of their spending and influence in the UK is disturbing, especially as we know they are actively lobbying MPs and want to restrict women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights, whether that is fertility treatment, contraception or access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have also ramped up activity here in recent months. Right to Life, a leading UK anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  charity, has been coordinating a lobbying campaign encouraging people to write to their MPs to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and spent \u163\'00\'A3117,000 on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  ads in 2023, 10 times the amount in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The charity \u8211\'20\'13 whose overall spending overall has risen from \u163\'00\'A3200k in 2019 to \u163\'00\'A3705k last year \u8211\'20\'13 also provides the secretariat to the Pro-Life all-party parliamentary group and aims to \u8220\'20\'1Cdeepen and expand relationships with parliamentarians\u8221\'20\'1D, according to its latest accounts. It is currently advertising vacancies for eight full-time staff and says in one ad that the role will include \u8220\'20\'1Cproducing briefings\u8221\'20\'1D for MPs and peers.\par \par \uc2 The Centre for Bio-Ethical Reform UK \u8211\'20\'13 another anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, which notoriously launched a billboard campaign featuring graphic images in pro-choice MP \uc2 Stella Creasy\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s constituency \u8211\'20\'13 has increased its staff numbers from four to 12 since 2017. Due to its status as a small company, it does not have to publish details of its income but said it was happy to engage in public debate about its \u8220\'20\'1Cfunding, growth and activities\u8221\'20\'1D and that its targeting of Creasy \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not equate to animosity towards her as a fellow human being\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 MPs are due to vote in the coming weeks on proposed changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that would see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decriminalised in England and Wales, as it is in Northern Ireland, Australia, France and New Zealand. Under a Victorian-era law that remains in place today, it is an offence to procure your own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There are exemptions under the 1967 \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases where two doctors agree that continuing the pregnancy would be risky for the physical or mental health of the woman. But the old law was never repealed and is still used today to prosecute and jail women for terminating pregnancies without sign-off from medics or after the 24-week limit.\par \par \uc2 The proposal on decriminalisation from backbench Labour MP \uc2 Diana Johnson\uc2  has cross-party support and is expected to pass. However some in the Labour party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/23/senior-labour-figures-seeking-water-down-plans-decriminalise-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fear it could be counterproductive}}}\uc2  and further embolden anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigning on related issues, such as the remote access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was introduced during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A government spokesperson said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely sensitive issue\u8221\'20\'1D with \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly held views on all sides of the discussion\u8221\'20\'1D, and that MPs would have a free vote on the proposed law change. \u8220\'20\'1CBy longstanding convention, any change to the law in this area would be a matter of conscience for individual MPs rather than the government,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240409ek47002kz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
