\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Her  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  experience was \u8216\'20\'18bizarre and painful\u8217\'20\'19. Now she\u8217\'20\'19s suing Tennessee\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,347 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2024\par 14:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kathryn Archer could not terminate her pregnancy in her home state and, instead, had to wait more than three weeks for an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic\par \par \uc2 In January 2023, whenever Kathryn Archer took her young daughter out to the local playground in Nashville, Tennessee, strangers often noticed her visibly pregnant stomach and wanted to make small talk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen are you due?\u8221\'20\'1D they would ask Archer. \u8220\'20\'1CDo you know if you\u8217\'20\'19re having a boy or a girl?\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1COh, I bet your daughter\u8217\'20\'19s so excited to be a big sister.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Archer did not know how to tell them the truth: in early January, Archer\u8217\'20\'19s fetus had been diagnosed with several serious anomalies that made a miscarriage likely. If Archer did give birth, her baby could only be treated with surgeries and lifelong help \u8211\'20\'13 pain that Archer was unwilling to put a newborn through. Without those surgeries, which the infant might not survive, Archer\u8217\'20\'19s baby would die shortly after birth.\par \par \uc2 But due to Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Archer could not terminate her pregnancy in her home state and, instead, had to wait more than three weeks for an appointment at an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to confide in a stranger that I\u8217\'20\'19m having to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because my baby can\u8217\'20\'19t survive outside of my womb and I can\u8217\'20\'19t get the care that I need as soon as I need it,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer recalled thinking. \u8220\'20\'1CThose three weeks were really bizarre, challenging, painful \u8211\'20\'13 beyond what it needed to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In the nearly two years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, 16 states have implemented near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. In theory, these bans have exceptions that permit people to get medically necessary abortions \u8211\'20\'13 but in practice, doctors and patients say, these exceptions are worded so vaguely that they are unworkable, prompting doctors to delay care out of a fear of running afoul of the law. Dozens of women from states like Texas, Idaho and Oklahoma have come forward to say that they were denied abortions they should have been legally entitled to.\par \par \uc2 Archer is now one of seven women in Tennessee who, along with two doctors, have sued the state, asking a court to clarify the scope of the state\u8217\'20\'19s exception for medically necessary abortions.\par \par \uc2 Currently, Tennessee doctors can use their \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable medical judgment\u8221\'20\'1D to perform abortions in severe emergencies, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters say that the standard is vague and subject to interpretation, leaving well-meaning doctors vulnerable to prosecution. Lawyers from the Center for Reproductive Rights, which is representing the women, instead hope to establish that doctors can use their \u8220\'20\'1Cgood faith medical judgement\u8221\'20\'1D to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when continuing a pregnancy would be unsafe. They also want to allow abortions if a fetus is unlikely to survive a pregnancy and sustain life after birth \u8211\'20\'13 as in Archer\u8217\'20\'19s case.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, almost all of the women in the lawsuit will gather at a courthouse in Nashville for a hearing in the case.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like if I can share our story and join this lawsuit, and even just change one person\u8217\'20\'19s experience, that would be worth it to me,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer said.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Archer and her husband decided to start trying for a second child right around the time Roe fell. A longtime supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Archer was aware of the risks that Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban posed to her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18What if I have a miscarriage? What if something were to happen in the pregnancy?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she recalled thinking. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I was pretty much only thinking about the first trimester.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Archer\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy appeared normal throughout her first trimester of pregnancy and into her second. In early January 2023, at her 20-week anatomy scan, Archer learned that she would be having a girl.\par \par \uc2 Then the doctor told her: \u8220\'20\'1CThere are diagnosable abnormalities from head to toe in this baby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A follow-up visit with a specialist confirmed that the fetus, who Archer and her husband named Cecilia, had a litany of health issues, including brain damage, a bladder located outside the body and severe spina bifida that left the spinal cord and nerves exposed. These anomalies, the specialist told Archer, made it unlikely that Cecilia would make it to birth, according to Archer\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Continuing the pregnancy, rather than ending it, would also pose more risks to Archer\u8217\'20\'19s own health.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter that scan, both my husband and I felt really strongly that the best thing for us to do and for our family would be to terminate the pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer said. \u8220\'20\'1COur doctors were incredibly supportive. They cared for us in so many ways, but their response was, essentially: \u8216\'20\'18Our hands are tied now. We can\u8217\'20\'19t help you any more.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Archer and her husband immediately started trying to find an appointment at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in another state. Since so many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics had shuttered in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s downfall, multiple clinics were booked up for weeks , pushing Archer deeper into a pregnancy that could threaten her own health and increase the risk she would be forced to give birth to a stillborn baby.\par \par \uc2 US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics performed more than 1m abortions in 2023, a record high. But that number clouds a more complex reality: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have shuttered numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the south and midwest, leaving the remaining clinics on the coasts deluged by a flood of patients from states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Kansas, where Archer initially tried to book an appointment, now treat roughly double the number of monthly patients that they saw before Roe fell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SFPWeCountPublicReport_2.28.24.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one recent analysis found}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Only a handful of US clinics provide abortions past about 24 weeks of pregnancy. Abortions also take longer and become more expensive later on in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Archer was able to secure an appointment at a Washington DC, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, about three and a half weeks after her 20-week anatomy scan. Due to the wait, \u8220\'20\'1Cit went from a one-day procedure to a two-day procedure,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey give her a shot to essentially stop her heart. And then I had to come back 24 hours later, so there was this period of time where she was no longer alive, but she was still in my body \u8230\'20\'26 Had I been able to get the procedure sooner, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have had the longer, slightly more complex procedure.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund and a church helped Archer and her husband defray some of the costs, but they still had to pay more than $10,000 out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had really good care because we have resources,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer continued. \u8220\'20\'1CHad we not had those resources and that privilege, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what we would have done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One of the other women involved in the Tennessee lawsuit also learned that her pregnancy was unlikely to result in a healthy baby, but was unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Against her will, the woman continued her pregnancy and delivered a stillborn son 31 weeks into her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Archer is now pregnant again and set to give birth in May \u8211\'20\'13 almost exactly one year after Cecilia\u8217\'20\'19s due date.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Archer and her husband promised to scatter Cecilia\u8217\'20\'19s ashes near water. \u8220\'20\'1CI just felt like, on a spiritual level, that I was really connected to her when I was near the element of water,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer said. They also pledged to take a hike every year in her honor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe did a lot of ritual around death and saying goodbye,\u8221\'20\'1D Archer said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt felt like \u8211\'20\'13 at every stage, for her and for our family \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re doing the right thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 4 April 2024 to correct a quotation from Kathryn Archer in which she said a one-day procedure turned into two-day procedure, not into a three-day procedure as originally quoted.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240404ek44001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
