\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Florida just crushed  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights. But it also created a tool to fight back\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par 10:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Conservatives don\u8217\'20\'19t understand that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is controversial only in theory; faced with a material choice, Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s hard to imagine a scenario in which the Florida state supreme court would not have allowed Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go into effect. In a challenge to a previous 15-week ban, the court\u8217\'20\'19s seven judges, all of whom were appointed by DeSantis, overturned 35 years of precedent this week in order to find that the right to privacy enshrined in the state constitution does not protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as Florida state law has acknowledged it does since 1989.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s approval of the 15-week ban will allow a stricter, previously stayed six-week ban to go into effect on 1 May. Justice Charles Canady did not recuse himself from the case, despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tampabay.com/news/florida-politics/2023/06/28/florida-supreme-court-abortion-case-canady-recuse-wife-law-sponsor/?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calls for him do so}}}\uc2  from no less an authority than the Florida supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s former chief justice, Barbara Pariente. Justice Canady\u8217\'20\'19s wife, the state representative Jennifer Canady, is a legislative co-sponsor of the newly approved six-week ban. There is no rape or incest exception.\par \par \uc2 The harm this decision will do to women across the American south is immeasurable. Like many states, Florida dramatically restricted access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, in the wake of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs v Jackson decision that overturned Roe v Wade. Florida\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/04/14/1084485963/florida-abortion-law-15-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed into law by DeSantis}}}\uc2  in April 2022, went into effect just days after the ruling. Despite those new restrictions, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained much more available in Florida than it did elsewhere in the south.\par \par \uc2 The south-east corner of the continental United States is home to some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/policies/west-virginia/abortion-policies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most restrictive \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2 . A woman living in Florida finds herself without a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy as far west as Texas and Oklahoma and as far north as Missouri, Indiana and West Virginia. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also banned outright in Arkansas, Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama, Kentucky and Tennessee; it is banned after six weeks in Georgia and South Carolina. Florida\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week ban was a dramatic reduction of women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare rights from the pre-Dobbs status quo. But due to the even more sadistic anti-choice zealotry of its neighbors, it was among the most permissive states in the region.\par \par \uc2 Under these circumstances, Florida became a haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers. Despite the new 15-week ban, abortions soared in Florida in the year after Dobbs, as women from across the south fled their homes in search of the care that was still legal in the Sunshine state. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception data project #WeCount, the number of abortions in Florida {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/miami/2023/10/26/abortions-increase-florida-data-statistics-map-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  by a total of 20,460 in the 12 months following the Dobbs decision. Now, both Floridian women and those who traveled from out of state for care will be forced to look further afield, to more distant and more expensive locations, in search of legal abortions. Many will not be able to get them.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to this catastrophe for women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, the Florida court also upended this fall\u8217\'20\'19s election in the state. That\u8217\'20\'19s because, in a narrow vote, the justices allowed a ballot measure to appear before voters this fall that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights explicitly in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The proposed constitutional amendment would declare Floridians have a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D, the medically imprecise but politically palatable standard that governed the Roe v Wade legal regime for 30 years and is usually interpreted to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until about 24 weeks of pregnancy. After that, women whose medical providers declare their pregnancies a danger to their health would also be able to receive abortions.\par \par \uc2 The ballot measure would need to receive approval from at least 60% of Florida voters in order to be enshrined in the state constitution. But if the measure is successful, it would invalidate both the six- and 15-week bans, and could theoretically be used to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access even beyond pre-Dobbs levels.\par \par \uc2 The addition of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measure to the November election has dramatically changed the political calculus in Florida overnight. Long the home of a growing sunbelt Republican base and an uncommonly weak state Democratic party, Florida has been considered a shoo-in for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who won the state by three points in 2020. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures have proven a persistent electoral winner, with measures to preserve or expand access to the procedure winning every time they have been put to voters since Dobbs \u8211\'20\'13 including in heavily Republican districts such as Kansas, Kentucky and Ohio, as well as in swing states like Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And the salience of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an election has been an excellent predictor of Democratic success since Dobbs, with the 2022 midterms and subsequent special elections all delivering Democratic victories whenever the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question is at the front of voters\u8217\'20\'19 minds.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, the new ballot measure may not just influence the presidential election, but the re-election bid of the Republican senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a one-time governor and fierce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights opponent who has said that he would have signed the six-week ban if he were still in the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion. That stance has come under harsh criticism in Florida, where even the comparatively less strict 15-week ban has had horrible human costs.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/04/10/pprom-florida-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Anya Cook}}}\uc2 , a Florida woman whose water broke too early in her pregnancy, almost died from blood loss after the 15-week ban prevented her doctors from administering miscarriage care. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abcactionnews.com/news/state/a-family-tragedy-a-moms-choice-a-hospital-groups-denial-and-floridas-15-week-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deborah Dorbert}}}\uc2  was forced to remain pregnant for months after her fetus was given a fatal diagnosis; she delivered an infant who died in her arms. These stories have made an indelible impression on the public. In Florida, more than 60% of voters say that they oppose the six-week ban. Now that it is slated to actually go into effect, that opposition is likely to grow.\par \par \uc2 If the post-Dobbs era has shown us anything, it is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is controversial only in theory. When faced with the material consequences of banning it, Americans find themselves unequivocally on freedom\u8217\'20\'19s side.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, then, may find that they have an unusual asset in the Republican opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bans are consistently unpopular, and the issue has proved persistently salient, moving voters to the polls even two years after the Dobbs decision. But this political boon for the Democrats has come at an unbearable cost: women\u8217\'20\'19s health, happiness and freedom.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240403ek43001e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
