\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Florida  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers brace for six-week ban: \u8216\'20\'18Where are these 80,000 patients gonna go?\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2024\par 01:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In separate decision, state supreme court agrees to allow voters to decide on enshrining rights in constitution in November\par \par \uc2 Florida, the last bastion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the south-eastern United States, will ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy starting next month, leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and their supporters in the state and across the country scrambling to deal with the fallout for patients.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Florida state supreme court upheld a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a move that removed the barriers for a separate, six-week ban that takes effect on 1 May. In a separate ruling, the court also agreed to let Florida residents weigh in on the issue through a November ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution \u8211\'20\'13 a decision that opens a new front in an election that is already sure to be dominated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all holding out hope for November, but realizing that from May to November, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be turning patients away at unprecedented rates,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Chelsea Daniels, a family medicine physician and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Miami, Florida. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like a punch to the gut.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Georgia is the closest state in the region to still permit the procedure, but it also bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, before many women know they are pregnant. After that ban took effect, the number of abortions performed in the state fell by almost half, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/SFPWeCountPublicReport_2.28.24.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to one recent analysis}}}\uc2 . Once Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban takes effect, it will be even stricter than Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s. While both states require \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients to wait 24 hours between getting counseling about the procedure and actually receiving it, Florida requires that counseling to be in person \u8211\'20\'13 meaning people have to make two trips to the clinic rather than one.\par \par \uc2 Daniels estimates that, at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic where she works, three-quarters of her patients are past six weeks of pregnancy. Since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, her clinic has also treated patients from states such as Texas, Alabama, Kentucky, Tennessee and Mississippi, which have all banned almost all abortions. There were more than 80,000 abortions performed in Florida in 2023.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere are these 80,000 patients gonna go?\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniels, who is also a fellow with Physicians for Reproductive Health. \u8220\'20\'1CWith the number of states that have restrictions, there are just not enough states protective of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to accommodate this volume.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, too, are concerned that once Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban takes effect, they will run out of money and be unable to fulfill the requests they expect to receive for support for travel. Many funds are already facing intense financial pressures, since so many people must now go out of state for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Loraine Pi\u241\'00\'F1eiro, the executive director of the Florida Access Network \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund, said she estimates that costs per patient could increase from $1,500 to $3,000. Megan Jeyifo, executive director of the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund, expects that costs for her fund will rise by more than $100,000 per month after the six-week ban takes effect. Her fund has helped roughly 2,000 people from Florida and its neighboring states come to Illinois for abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we lose Florida, we just lose this huge lifeline for not just Floridians but for people in the South,\u8221\'20\'1D Jeyifo said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are on the brink of turning people away for the first time in five years. The rage donations have dried up. The interest in this issue, when there are so many other competing crises in our country and in the world, has waned.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe, the Tampa Bay \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund saw \u8220\'20\'1Cthousands and thousands of dollars pouring in\u8221\'20\'1D, said McKenna Kelley, a board member and volunteer for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund. But in the hours since the Florida supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, Kelley said that to her knowledge, the fund has only received roughly $1,000 \u8211\'20\'13 enough to likely pay for a single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s really what this is going to come down to. We\u8217\'20\'19re anticipating at least 90% of our callers are going to have to go out of state. And it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of money,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelley said.\par \par \uc2 For now, Daniels said that she is focused on caring for as many patients as possible. Although her clinic also provides services such as infertility and prenatal care, she expects the clinic to shift to prioritize as many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients as possible before May. The clinic will also likely expand its hours.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like I\u8217\'20\'19ve gone through all stages of grief in the last 16 hours. I\u8217\'20\'19ve cried and I\u8217\'20\'19ve yelled and I\u8217\'20\'19ve had really difficult conversations, not just professionally with my employer, but also with my loved ones,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. But, she added: \u8220\'20\'1CUntil they pull the instruments out of my hands, I\u8217\'20\'19ll be providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care till 15 weeks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240402ek42002jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
