\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Republican party has become a full-fledged anti-sex movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Rebecca Solnit \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par 10:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The conservative obsession with purity and control is being achieved by increasingly punitive means\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  cited the Comstock Act, named after the 19th-century anti-vice campaigner Anthony Comstock, in last week\u8217\'20\'19s case about access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t know who Anthony Comstock was or what his law did, that might not have alarmed you. But it should have.\par \par \uc2 The Comstock Law has come up a lot lately, and it\u8217\'20\'19s part of the Republican war on sex, and to put it that way might sound overly dramatic. But there is such a war, and parts of it \u8211\'20\'13 against sex education, against access to birth control, against the healthcare provider \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and of course against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have long been out in the open along with a war against the rights of women and on the rights and very existence of queer and trans people.\par \par \uc2 Comstock was reputed to be driven by religious shame over masturbation to become his era\u8217\'20\'19s most extreme anti-sex crusader. He rose to prominence in the early 1870s, when he convinced Congress to make it a crime to advertise, sell or mail contraceptives or give out contraceptive information, even orally, or to mail anything \u8220\'20\'1Cimmoral\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a term whose vagueness allowed widespread prosecution, including of a feminist newspaper reporting on sexual abuse whose prominent publishers, Victoria Woodhull and Tennessee Claflin, he got sent to prison. Like modern-day rightwingers he was a book-burner, and he boasted that he had driven 15 people to suicide.\par \par \uc2 Too many people thought that Roe v Wade wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t really be overturned just like they thought Trump wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t really be elected\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen fascism comes to America, it will be wrapped in the flag and carrying a cross\u8221\'20\'1D is a handy quote and it does seem to describe well Donald Trump, who has hugged {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gettyimages.com/photos/trump-hugging-flag"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lot}}}\uc2  of US flags onstage and last week was hawking Christian nationalist Bibles. But if fascism really comes to America, it won\u8217\'20\'19t come just as a single figure. It will come sneaking in, as local, state and federal laws and the gradual erosion of rights pushed by many players at many levels. In fact it has been coming at us all along. It is right now, among other things, a full-fledged anti-sex movement.\par \par \uc2 Too many people thought that Roe v Wade wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t really be overturned just like they thought Trump wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t really be elected. The assumption that norms will persist is these days a dangerous obtuseness, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s about climate, domestic policy, society or the international order. While the backlash to Roe\u8217\'20\'19s June 2022 overturning has been spectacular, with Democratic election victories and blue-state legislation strengthening reproductive rights, that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t spare women in red states from the horrific consequences of the decision.\par \par \uc2 At this point we all know they include prosecution for miscarriages suspected of being abortions, let alone for actual abortions, and lack of timely care from medical providers, who, fearful of prosecution themselves, sometimes wait for miscarrying patients to go critical from infection or loss of blood before offering care. As the law journalist Mark Joseph Stern {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mjs_DC/status/1773101269906776070"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  on 27 March, \u8220\'20\'1CThe anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement\u8217\'20\'19s end goal is to let doctors refuse treatment \u8211\'20\'13 including life-saving emergency care \u8211\'20\'13 for patients whom they deem to be sinful and morally impure.\u8221\'20\'1D The patients, largely women, are supposed to die for their sins.\par \par \uc2 As if that weren\u8217\'20\'19t enough, in May 2023, \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  declared on social media, \u8220\'20\'1CConservatives have to lead the way in restoring sex to its true purpose, & ending recreational sex & senseless use of birth control pills.\u8221\'20\'1D It\u8217\'20\'19s a fanatical statement: the vast majority of sex had by the vast majority of human beings does not have reproduction as its goal, though the term recreational disparages what can be a joy, a profound connection, or a transcendence of self, among other things.\par \par \uc2 The far right \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  belongs to is, nevertheless, driving toward this goal by striving to take away birth control and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make sex punitively risky for anyone who might get pregnant. Taking away women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive freedom takes away other freedoms, social, economic and educational, and rebuilds a society of gender inequality, which is clearly the goal. The right has also made noise about ending no-fault divorce and marriage equality, and introduced hundreds of anti-trans bills this year and last.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.project2025.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2  agenda for a rightwing coup, should Trump win this November, declares that the \uc2 USAid\uc2  office of gender equality and women\u8217\'20\'19s empowerment \u8220\'20\'1Cshould remove all... language on \uc2 USAid\uc2  websites, in agency publications and policies, and in all agency contracts and grants that include\u8221\'20\'1D terms including \u8220\'20\'1Cgender and gender equality\u8221\'20\'1D and should also remove references to \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive health\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Csexual and reproductive rights\u8221\'20\'1D. The threats are in plain sight; I hope people notice them.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not a coincidence that the authoritarian right is obsessed with both the border and women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies; they\u8217\'20\'19d like to increase the patrolling of both, and essentially shut them both down. It\u8217\'20\'19s an obsession with purity and control to be achieved by punitive and sometimes homicidally violent means. And it\u8217\'20\'19s a roadmap straight back to the terrible inequality women were already campaigning against in Anthony Comstock\u8217\'20\'19s time.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Solnit is a Guardian US columnist. She is the author of Orwell\u8217\'20\'19s Roses and co-editor with Thelma Young Lutunatabua of the climate anthology Not Too Late: Changing the Climate Story from Despair to Possibility\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240402ek42001h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
