\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Florida supreme court clears way for both  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ballot measure and six-week ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par 00:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Court gave voters a chance to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state constitution, but also paved way for six-week ban within 30 days\par \par \uc2 Florida residents will get the chance to vote on a ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution, the Florida state supreme court ruled on Monday, teeing up a dramatic electoral showdown over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in a state that has become a haven for women fleeing southern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 The decision is a victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists \u8211\'20\'13 but one that was significantly tempered by a second decision also issued on Monday, in which the state supreme court ruled to uphold Florida\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. That decision paves the way for a separate six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 passed in 2023 but on hold pending the outcome of the case over the 15-week ban \u8211\'20\'13 to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The six-week ban will take effect in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 In upholding the 15-week ban, which was passed shortly before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, the Florida state supreme court upended a 1989 decision that had found that women had the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the second trimester of pregnancy, thanks to privacy protections in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe privacy clause of the Florida constitution does not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or include a word or phrase that clearly incorporates it. Era-appropriate dictionary definitions and contextual clues suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not naturally fit within the rights at issue,\u8221\'20\'1D reads the majority opinion, which was written by Justice Jamie Grosshans. \u8220\'20\'1CReliable historical sources, like the technical meaning of the terms contained in the provision, the origin of the amendment, and the framing of the public debate, similarly do not support a conclusion that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be read into the provision\u8217\'20\'19s text.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Grosshans was appointed by Florida\u8217\'20\'19s current governor, Republican \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . In total, five of the seven justices on the Florida state supreme court were appointed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The fight over the constitutional amendment will now become even more urgent \u8211\'20\'13 if it passes, it will restore the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access set to be curtailed by the six-week ban. Under the constitutional amendment proposed by the ballot measure, access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be protected until fetal viability, which is generally around 24 weeks of pregnancy, as well as in cases when a doctor deems an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  necessary to protect a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the ban is set to have a staggering impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the southern US, since people outside Florida rely heavily on the state for abortions. Almost 10,000 people who received abortions in Florida in 2023 hailed from outside Florida, according to a recent analysis by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . That\u8217\'20\'19s more than double the out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients seen in Florida in 2020, before Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2 , Florida\u8217\'20\'19s Republican attorney general, had asked Florida\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court to stop the ballot measure from reaching voters, arguing that, in part, the term \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D can have too many meanings.\par \par \uc2 The language of the ballot measure summary, \uc2 Moody\uc2  had argued in court documents, is so vague as to be misleading and is part of a \u8220\'20\'1Csimilar overall design to lay ticking time bombs that will enable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proponents later to argue that the amendment has a much broader meaning than voters would ever have thought\u8221\'20\'1D. By allowing healthcare providers to determine if an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to protect a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health or whether a fetus has reached viability, the ballot measure would strip power away from the government \u8211\'20\'13 and does not clearly convey that shift in power to voters, \uc2 Moody\uc2  argued in court records.\par \par \uc2 In a \u8220\'20\'1Cper curiam\u8221\'20\'1D opinion \u8211\'20\'13 meaning it was not authored by a specific judge \u8211\'20\'13 the Florida justices rejected that argument.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat the proposed amendment\u8217\'20\'19s principal goal and chief purpose is to limit government interference with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is plainly stated in terms that clearly and unambiguously reflect the text of the proposed amendment,\u8221\'20\'1D they wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd the broad sweep of this proposed amendment is obvious in the language of the summary. Denying this requires a flight from reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Almost 1 million Florida voters signed petitions to add the measure to the November ballot, according to an unofficial tally from the Florida division of elections.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures are currently in the works in about a dozen states across the country, including swing states such as Arizona and Nevada. Democrats are hoping that referendums to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will amp up turnout and help propel their candidates to victory, since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have become a winning electoral issue after the downfall of Roe v Wade in 2022. Outrage over the fall of Roe was credited with lessening a much-heralding \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D in the 2022 elections, while voters have backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights even in deep-red states like Kansas and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Florida supreme court was right to let the ballot initiative go before voters \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s a good thing they did because voters will need to head to the polls to undo the damage the court is causing with its decision to allow an extreme ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go into effect,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\uc2 , \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2  president, said in a statement on Monday. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is devastating news for access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the state and the entire south.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240401ek41002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
