\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How rightwing groups used junk science to get an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  case before the \uc2 US supreme court\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza in New York \par \uc2 1,405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2024\par 19:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  researchers \u8216\'20\'18exaggerate\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18obfuscate\u8217\'20\'19 in their scientific papers \u8211\'20\'13 but by the time they\u8217\'20\'19re published, it\u8217\'20\'19s too lateExplainer: the mifepristone caseTell us: have you used an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill in the US?\par \par \uc2 A pharmacy professor who strenuously avoids heated political discussions is an unlikely candidate to get involved in a fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly one as high stakes as a case now before the supreme court: the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)\uc2  v the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine (AHM).\par \par \uc2 But when the professor Chris Adkins of South University in Georgia emailed his concerns about an academic article to the editors of Health Services Research and Managerial Epidemiology, that\u8217\'20\'19s exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 The article had been published by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  research institute and, perhaps unsurprisingly, concluded that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was far less safe than the accepted scientific consensus \u8211\'20\'13 one established by more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/01/health/abortion-pill-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 100 peer-reviewed studies}}}\uc2  across multiple continents and two decades of real-world use.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe way this study used this situation to exaggerate, and I\u8217\'20\'19ll say obfuscate, the truth behind mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s safety profile is where I thought: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ll reach out to the journal and say I\u8217\'20\'19ve got these issues,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Adkins, referring to the drug targeted by researchers. Mifepristone is one half of a two-pill regimen that treats miscarriage and ends early pregnancy, and its future hangs in the balance of the supreme court case, to be heard this week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI honestly didn\u8217\'20\'19t think I would be the first to do that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Adkins.\par \par \uc2 Within a couple days of Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 complaint, the global academic publisher Sage, which publishes the journal, began investigating. Within weeks, Sage retracted not one but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://retractionwatch.com/2024/02/06/papers-used-by-judge-to-justify-abortion-pill-suspension-retracted/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three papers by the anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  researchers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 concerns go to the heart of a problem that has bedeviled scientists for at least a decade: the judicial system\u8217\'20\'19s repeated adoption of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://academic.oup.com/book/36759/chapter-abstract/321866919?redirectedFrom=fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poor-quality evidence}}}\uc2  to justify litigation and legislation to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Often that evidence is produced by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement itself.\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2  v AHM is scheduled for oral arguments on Tuesday. The suit, brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors, seeks to force the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to reverse decisions that relaxed restrictions on prescribing mifepristone. The Biden administration and the medication\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturer argue the doctors have no right to sue in the first place.\par \par \uc2 The study Adkins complained about is central to the doctors\u8217\'20\'19 case, and was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.digital-science.com/tldr/article/science-misused-in-the-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cited heavily by a federal district court}}}\uc2  in Amarillo, Texas, that kicked off the government\u8217\'20\'19s appeal when it found in favor of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors.\par \par \uc2 How the supreme court decides the case could have profound implications. A finding in favor of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors could reshape \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access again in the US, including in Democratic-led states that might have considered themselves immune from restrictions. It also holds the potential to upend the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s authority, which could call into question the future of all kinds of controversial drugs, from contraception to vaccines to treatments for HIV.\par \par \uc2 Researchers are skeptical that Sage\u8217\'20\'19s retractions alone will make a difference in the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s frustrating, it\u8217\'20\'19s depressing, it\u8217\'20\'19s maddening and quite honestly it\u8217\'20\'19s frightening,\u8221\'20\'1D said the obstetrician and gynecologist \uc2 Daniel Grossman\uc2  of the \uc2 University of California at San Francisco\uc2  (\uc2 UCSF\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Grossman is also a professor and the director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health}}}\uc2 , one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s foremost reproductive health research groups. His own work has been taken out of context by attorneys arguing to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in court briefs, he said, and he has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/nejmp1805927"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published pieces to criticize}}}\uc2  the poor quality of evidence produced by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and researchers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJudges don\u8217\'20\'19t have expertise to be able to review the science, just like I don\u8217\'20\'19t have all the expertise to understand all the legal maneuvering that\u8217\'20\'19s happening in this case,\u8221\'20\'1D said Grossman.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement pours money into research groups such as the Charlotte Lozier Institute, whose raison d\u8217\'20\'19\u234\'00\'EAtre is to produce articles its activists can cite in litigation, legislation and promotional materials. The institute was founded in 2011 by one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy groups, \uc2 Susan B Anthony\uc2  Pro-life America, and its researchers are responsible for the three now-retracted articles flagged by Adkins.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a professor of law at the \uc2 University of California at Davis\uc2  and a leading legal historian of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, says the movement has spent decades investing in its own research arm. Campaigners started fringe publications, such as the journal Issues in Law and Medicine, a peer-reviewed publication produced by the the National Legal Center for the Medically Dependent and Disabled. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/1985/05/19/political-opposites-join-sides-on-hospital-issue/62763537007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 That organization}}}\uc2  was founded by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/07/podcasts/the-daily/anti-abortion-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 James Bopp}}}\uc2 , a lawyer who has campaigned against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for decades, and is now the lead council of the National Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://issuesinlawandmedicine.com/about/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 journal\u8217\'20\'19s current editor}}}\uc2 , Barry Bostrom, is an attorney who fought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for decades. Bostrom has served as director and general counsel of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rewirenewsgroup.com/2017/03/23/issues-law-medicine-one-stop-journal-anti-vaccine-anti-abortion-pseudoscience/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indiana Right to Life}}}\uc2 , and at least once represented National Right to Life before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fec.gov/files/legal/murs/6133/10044262126.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Election Commission}}}\uc2  in 2009, alongside Bopp.\par \par \uc2 But \u8220\'20\'1Cthat\u8217\'20\'19s not the business model anymore\u8221\'20\'1D, Ziegler said. The movement is no longer limiting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  research to its own journals.\par \par \uc2 Now, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  researchers also seek to place their research in journals published by academic publishers such as Sage or, in another example, the British Journal of Psychiatry, published by the \uc2 Royal College of Psychiatrists\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the latter example, an American researcher found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts for a substantial increase of risk in adverse mental health outcomes. However, the researcher\u8217\'20\'19s analysis depended in part on a \u8220\'20\'1Cdebunked\u8221\'20\'1D paper, overestimated risk and did not follow published guidelines for the kind of analysis performed.\par \par \uc2 Researchers have repeatedly raised concerns to the British Journal of Psychiatry and even recently published an article in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bmj.com/content/384/bmj-2023-076518.full?ijkey=wzVO2EBacCMYznx&keytype=ref"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 British Medical Journal}}}\uc2  (\uc2 BMJ\uc2 ) calling for a retraction. So far, they have been rebuffed by the \uc2 Royal College of Psychiatrists\uc2 , the publisher of the British Journal of Psychiatry.\par \par \uc2 In spite of their efforts, the researcher\u8217\'20\'19s work has been repeatedly cited as evidence of the harms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before state courts and federal courts. In 2022, the researchers\u8217\'20\'19 work was cited in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/184712/20210722163259351_41205%20pdf%20Parker%20I%20br.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a brief to the supreme court}}}\uc2  in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the case that ended nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50 years of constitutional protection}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has also used the researcher as an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox2detroit.com/news/oakland-county-judge-rips-pro-life-witnesses-says-both-are-not-credible"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expert witness in court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But fighting poor-quality evidence can feel like a losing battle. Responding in a well-respected journal can be a lengthy process that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t always pay off.\par \par \uc2 Ushma Upadhyay, a public health social scientist trained in demography, and a professor in the department of obstetrics and gynecology at \uc2 UCSF\uc2 , contributed to both the \uc2 BMJ\uc2  article that failed to secure a retraction, and co-authored an article in the journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.contraceptionjournal.org/article/S0010-7824(24)00070-2/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Contraception}}}\uc2  with Adkins on the flaws in the now-retracted Sage articles.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe worked on it over Thanksgiving break. My mom was visiting, and I was like: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m really sorry, we have to get this out,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Upadhyay. \u8220\'20\'1CThe stakes were so high.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Evaluating scientific evidence is difficult under the best of circumstances. To the untrained eye, academic journals are a thicket of unknown quality, and \u8220\'20\'1Cpeer review\u8221\'20\'1D is a lofty term but is only as strong as the people doing the reviewing.\par \par \uc2 Even when researchers make a compelling case, journals can be loath {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/03/20/science/columbia-cancer-research-retractions.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to correct the scientific record}}}\uc2 . That allows a contested article to be further cited and compounds the damage of poor evidence..\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor every one paper that is retracted, there are probably 10 that should be,\u8221\'20\'1D Ivan Oransky, co-founder of Retraction Watch, recently told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Retraction Watch maintains a database of more than 47,000 retracted studies.\par \par \uc2 Should the court choose to undermine the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , it will be the result of a tragic irony \u8211\'20\'13 that one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s most respected arbiters of science could be undone by research that would never meet its standards.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 28 March 2024 because calls for the retraction were rebuffed by the \uc2 Royal College of Psychiatrists\uc2 , the publisher of the British Journal of Psychiatry, rather than by British psychiatrists as an earlier version said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240323ek3n0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
