\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Even the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  was baffled by conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 attack on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,266 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par 17:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The anti-choice case relies on outlandish legal leaps. And if they can\u8217\'20\'19t win there, they\u8217\'20\'19ll redouble efforts to win the White HouseUS supreme court \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill hearing: key takeawaysAbortion rights: where laws stand in every US state\par \par \uc2 It is a testament to how weak the plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 case is that the justices seemed so skeptical. Erin Hawley, a lawyer for the far-right antifeminist litigation shop \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  and the spouse of the conservative US senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , usually gets a much warmer reception at One First Street. But in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s oral arguments in Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine v FDA \u8211\'20\'13 a lawsuit which seeks to challenge \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone, and specifically to reverse regulatory changes that made the drug more easily accessible \u8211\'20\'13 she was on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 The three Democratic appointees, along with the Republican justices Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Roberts, all signaled at least some skepticism of her clients\u8217\'20\'19 claims to legal standing. \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , the Trump appointee known for her maximalist religious commitments, struggled to help Hawley establish a convincing merits case to restrict access to the drug. And the far-right extremists \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  spent their question time signalling their support for the Comstock Act, a long-obscure and once-forgotten 1871 statute that some anti-choice lawyers say could be used to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide by executive order.\par \par \uc2 Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine has always been a strange case, one whose path to the court was marked by controversy, strained argument and dramatically lowered legal standards. For one thing, the plaintiffs, a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors who make outlandish and empirically disproven claims about the supposed dangers of mifepristone, hand-picked their own trial judge. They filed their lawsuit in the northern district of Texas, a federal court in Amarillo that has only one judge: Matthew Kacsmaryk, a young Trump appointee with a history of militant anti-choice activism who has become famous for his extreme deference to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  litigants.\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk ignored the fact that the physician plaintiffs could not show any injury that would entitle them to sue, and promptly issued a national injunction revoking \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug \u8211\'20\'13 an unprecedented judicial intervention that threatened to end access to a medication that is used in more than half of US abortions.\par \par \uc2 Above him, the far-right fifth circuit, in an opinion authored by the aspiring supreme court nominee James Ho, upheld the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s initial approval of the drug but ruled that interventions in 2016 and 2021 that had made it more accessible were illegal, a move that would have made the pills dramatically more difficult to get in a post-Dobbs world. In his opinion, Ho not only bypassed the case\u8217\'20\'19s initial standing problems, but made bizarre arguments justifying the right of virtually anyone to sue over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication \u8211\'20\'13 including for what he called \u8220\'20\'1Caesthetic injuries\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that is, the harm allegedly done by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to people who are deprived of the opportunity to look at more babies.\par \par \uc2 At the supreme court, it was the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s post-2016 moves to lower barriers of access to mifepristone that were supposedly at issue. And in theory, this should have been catnip to the revanchist supreme court, which has in recent years enthusiastically taken up legal challenges meant to erode \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, curtail civil rights, and weaken federal agencies like the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . But with the court\u8217\'20\'19s approval at an all-time low in the wake of Dobbs, and with a looming November election to be determined in a large part by public outrage over women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, even the court\u8217\'20\'19s most enthusiastic enemies of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and federal regulation found themselves with limited appetite to allow plaintiffs to limit access to a safe and popular drug nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And so it was that on Tuesday, the supreme court rediscovered an area of the law that it has recently been content to ignore: standing doctrine. The minor, inconvenient fact that the plaintiffs have experienced no injury and have no legal right to sue had been hand-waved away in the district court and at the fifth circuit, but it became an issue of prolonged attention in the oral arguments at the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  noted that the plaintiff\u8217\'20\'19s theory of standing was \u8220\'20\'1Chighly probabilistic\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning that it relied on a series of hypotheticals and contingencies about potential harms that might happen, somehow, at some indeterminate point in the future, to someone, somewhere.\par \par \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  issued some of her most pointed questions since joining the court \u8211\'20\'13 a high bar \u8211\'20\'13 over the asymmetry between the plaintiff\u8217\'20\'19s stated injury of a hypothetical future conscience harm and their proposed remedy for that injury \u8211\'20\'13 a nationwide restriction on the way all American women can use the drug. Jackson was joined in this line of thought by the Trump appointee \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , her sometimes odd-couple ally, who asked the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  camp why they had filed such a broad petition, instead of a narrow one, in a tone I can only describe as scolding.\par \par \uc2 Roberts signaled a preoccupation with the standing question; even Kavanaugh, a justice with little skill in making a point, asked a question that seemed aimed at getting a fact of established law on the record: don\u8217\'20\'19t these physicians already have a legal right to decline to perform abortions? Hawley answered in the affirmative.\par \par \uc2 The court seems poised to throw out the case on standing grounds; if the opinion is written by a conservative, it will likely operate as something of an instruction manual, describing the kind of case that the conservative legal movement could bring that would successfully overturn the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone. A future case \u8211\'20\'13 just not this one.\par \par \uc2 But oral arguments on Tuesday did make news: they signaled the first time that the anti-choice movement\u8217\'20\'19s preferred strategy for banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide has cheerleaders on the supreme court. The case that the court heard on Tuesday was specifically not supposed to concern the federal Comstock Act, a long-unenforced law left over from the Victorian era that imposed a ban on sending contraception or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  implements through the mail or trading them via interstate commerce. But both \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  brought up the act, which plaintiffs mentioned in their briefs and which was the focus of several amici curiae who submitted in the case.\par \par \uc2 Since Dobbs, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  litigants have been advancing a novel, never-before-enforced idea that the Comstock Act could be interpreted broadly to functionally ban all abortions nationwide \u8211\'20\'13 as well as several kinds of birth control and possibly implements that are also used in other kinds of routine gynecological care, like speculums and curettes. Alito signaled with his questions that he felt the act applied to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , who had failed to heed its prohibitions when they approved the drug; Thomas suggested that mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturer had violated it when selling and advertising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 These interpretations will likely not be controlling opinion in this lawsuit. But they signal how this court may rule under a future Republican administration. After all, if Republicans want to enforce the Comstock Act as a nationwide total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, they don\u8217\'20\'19t need to win control of Congress. All they need is the White House.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 27 March 2024 to correct the name of a \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justice who had been misidentified.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240327ek3r001ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
