\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  hearing not a success for anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  doctors, experts say\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman and Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,115 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par 16:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices appear unconvinced by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors\u8217\'20\'19 standing to sue during hearing over access to \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved pill mifepristone\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  hearing that held the potential to reshape \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s authority did not go well for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors behind the case, legal experts said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The consensus is a positive sign for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, who feared the case would curtail access to medication abortions, which now account for the majority of all abortions nationally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very possible that they will just toss the lawsuit out because the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors didn\u8217\'20\'19t have legal standing to sue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lawrence Gostin, a professor at Georgetown Law School and an expert in global public health law, said about the justices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view, the lawsuit was absurd on its face and deserves to be thrown out because these anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors had very little injury,\u8221\'20\'1D Gostin added.\par \par \uc2 The case deals with \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulation of the drug mifepristone, one-half of a two-drug regimen used to terminate an early pregnancy. A group representing the doctors, called the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, has sought to roll back \uc2 FDA\uc2  decisions that expanded mifepristone access, such as allowing doctors to prescribe it via telehealth.\par \par \uc2 The Alliance argued its doctors could be forced to deal with complications of a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thus doctors had the legal right, or standing, to bring the case.\par \par \uc2 But over the course of 90 minutes of oral arguments, even members of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative supermajority seemed skeptical of the claim.\par \par \uc2 Justices \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  asked the US solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , whether the doctors, represented by the powerhouse conservative law firm \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , were already protected by laws that shield healthcare providers from violating their consciences. Fellow conservative \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  indicated that, in asking for a nationwide change, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors were asking for too much.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis case seems like a prime example of turning what could be a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on an \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule,\u8221\'20\'1D Gorsuch said.\par \par \uc2 Eva Temkin, an attorney at the law firm Paul Hastings who wrote a brief to the court on behalf of pharmaceutical executives and companies and a former \uc2 FDA\uc2  attorney, said the hearing underscored the importance of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work as an independent arbiter of science.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court was sort of appropriately skeptical of the plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 standing in this case, and also appropriately skeptical of the idea that \uc2 FDA\uc2  had abused its authority by reaching the scientific judgment it reached,\u8221\'20\'1D said Temkin.\par \par \uc2 Many legal experts agreed the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors lacked the standing to sue. But they were less sure that the supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 which overturned Roe v Wade less than two years ago \u8211\'20\'13 would not side with the doctors anyway.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen recently is groups like \uc2 ADF\uc2  essentially saying: \u8216\'20\'18We have a conservative supermajority. Let\u8217\'20\'19s see if any of the old rules still apply. Let\u8217\'20\'19s see if any of the procedural guardrails still exist,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2  who studies the legal history of reproduction. \u8220\'20\'1CI think you had a lot of the conservative justices saying: \u8216\'20\'18Yes, we\u8217\'20\'19re conservative on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but you still don\u8217\'20\'19t have standing. Sorry!\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not every justice appeared so dubious of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors\u8217\'20\'19 standing. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , the most conservative justices, seemed more willing to side with the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJustice Alito\u8217\'20\'19s vote is not even remotely in question. The plaintiff here could be a Barbie doll and he would find standing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elizabeth Sepper, a professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, she added: \u8220\'20\'1CI still think if I were a bookmaker, I would have odds on the government prevailing on standing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Experts were also struck by Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s and Alito\u8217\'20\'19s references to the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-obscenity law that, in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have argued can be used to implement a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Although the Comstock Act has not been enforced in roughly 80 years, Thomas asked a lawyer for Danco Laboratories, a manufacturer of mifepristone, to explain why Danco was not violating the Comstock Act by selling and advertising mifepristone. (She demurred, saying that such an issue was not before the court.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe suggested to the lawyer for Danco that the company is committing criminal acts, currently, on an ongoing basis,\u8221\'20\'1D Sepper said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s effectively what that exchange came down to, and that\u8217\'20\'19s sort of stunning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether the supreme court sides with the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and Danco, or with the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors, Thomas, Alito or both will probably end up writing an opinion that mentions the Comstock Act, multiple experts said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s getting raised in briefs, it\u8217\'20\'19s getting raised in cases where it\u8217\'20\'19s of dubious relevance,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joanna Grossman, a law professor at the Southern Methodist University Dedman School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s in the conversation. Do I think they probably have the votes to make it more than part of the conversation? Probably not. But I\u8217\'20\'19ve been wrong before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  prevails in the mifepristone case, it is unlikely to be out of the woods. The supreme court is currently deliberating over another case that attacks the administrative state and that could jettison the Chevron Doctrine, a legal concept that requires judges defer to expert agencies when the law is ambiguous.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re really beginning to see the crumbling of health and safety agencies that have kept Americans healthy and safe for nearly a century \u8211\'20\'13 and all of that is beginning to wither away right in front of our eyes,\u8221\'20\'1D Gostin said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is not out of the woods, and neither are their counterparts at other health and safety and scientific agencies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The justices are also set to hear oral arguments in yet another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case next month, which will ask the justices to decide whether a federal law that requires hospitals to stabilize patients in medical emergencies pertains to emergency abortions. Regardless of how that case or the mifepristone case ultimately plays out, experts are confident that the supreme court will be asked to weigh in on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  again and again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf standing ends up being the way that this case ends, that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean this kind of challenge won\u8217\'20\'19t happen again,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nicole Huberfeld, a health law professor at Boston University\u8217\'20\'19s School of Public Health. \u8220\'20\'1CThe courthouse doors remain open.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240327ek3r000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
