\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill hearing: key takeaways\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza and Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,341 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par 21:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices appear skeptical of arguments against mifepristone as pharmaceutical industry considers case existential threatCourt seems skeptical of arguments against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone\par \par \uc2 Supreme court justices seemed to express skepticism towards arguments by a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors in a case that could reshape the availability of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and undermine the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) authority.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court heard oral arguments on Tuesday in Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine v FDA, the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case to reach the supreme court since it overturned Roe v Wade almost two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The court will decide whether a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors could force the agency to roll back \uc2 FDA\uc2  decisions from 2016 and 2021 that expanded access to a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, mifepristone. The pill is generally used as part of a two-drug regimen in medication abortions, which account for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/19/abortion-increase-2023-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 63% of all US abortions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The justices will probably issue a decision by summer 2024, just months ahead of the US election \u8211\'20\'13 in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be a major issue.\par \par \uc2 Here are some key takeaways from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing:\par \par \uc2 Even conservative justices expressed skepticism\par \par \uc2 Conservative justices on the court hold a 6-3 majority. So, while all of the court\u8217\'20\'19s liberal members appeared plainly dubious of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors\u8217\'20\'19 claims, at least two conservatives must join them to avoid a decision in favor of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors.\par \par \uc2 Much of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the argument}}}\uc2  focused on standing, or whether doctors had the right to sue in the first place, and whether the proposed remedy \u8211\'20\'13 rolling back the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s authority to change how a drug is prescribed \u8211\'20\'13 went far beyond what was necessary to protect a small group of doctors who can already invoke federal conscience protections if they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in cases of exceedingly rare complications from mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , one of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives, said he was uncertain why the best course of action was to roll back the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision. Was throwing out \uc2 FDA\uc2  rules the equivalent of, \u8220\'20\'1Cturning a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on an \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule, or any other federal government action?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  also spent a significant amount of time confirming that federal conscience laws already protect anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo I just want to be clear, the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s position is that... such doctors would have recourse to the conscience protections of federal law?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked the US solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , who represented the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  sought reassurance. \u8220\'20\'1CUnder federal law, no doctors can be forced against their consciences to perform or assist in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , correct?\u8221\'20\'1D he asked Prelogar, who represented the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prelogar said that was correct, and that the government believed conscience laws provide \u8220\'20\'1Cbroad coverage here\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s liberal justices also appeared dubious, with \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the members of the court\u8217\'20\'19s liberal wing \u8211\'20\'13 questioning the foundations of the case.\par \par \uc2 Kagan asked Erin Hawley, the attorney for the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, whether she believed that the injuries she is \u8220\'20\'1Ctrying to prove\u8221\'20\'1D could be \u8220\'20\'1Ctraceable to the 2016 and 2021 actions you\u8217\'20\'19re challenging \u8230\'20\'26 [that] these incidents are caused by whatever incremental increase in risk there is from the 2016 and 2021 actions\u8221\'20\'1D. Hawley told Kagan that the 2016 and 2021 actions, which among other things allowed mifepristone to be prescribed via telehealth and delivered by mail, \u8220\'20\'1Cincreased the risk of harm\u8221\'20\'1D. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has found no significant safety concerns related to the changes.\par \par \uc2 Jackson then questioned why doctors needed a remedy as broad as undermining the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s authority and forcing a rollback of their evaluations.\par \par \uc2 The case has a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant mismatch\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbetween the claimed injury and the remedy that\u8217\'20\'19s being sought... They\u8217\'20\'19re saying, \u8216\'20\'18Because we object to being forced to participate in this procedure, we\u8217\'20\'19re seeking an order preventing anyone from having access to these drugs at all.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson said she was trying to understand \u8220\'20\'1Chow they could possibly be entitled to that, given the injury that they have alleged.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments underscored the fears of the pharmaceutical industry\par \par \uc2 Continually evaluating the safety and efficacy of a medication, and adjusting its regulations based on that evidence, are part of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s core duties.\par \par \uc2 The pharmaceutical industry considers this case an existential threat, because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/25/abortion-pill-case-fda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 if a court can roll back evidence-based}}}\uc2  prescribing changes, what stops judges from parsing science from the bench, even when they have no qualifications to do so?\par \par \uc2 Two separate exchanges highlighted these fears. First, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , a conservative, asked why the agency didn\u8217\'20\'19t provide a more comprehensive comment on increased emergency room visits from prescriptions of mifepristone via telehealth, which have been permitted by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  since 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe increase in ER visits is just of no consequence? It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even merit some comment?\u8221\'20\'1D Alito asked Prelogar.\par \par \uc2 To researchers, it was an obvious example of a proxy measure: emergency room visits might indicate the possibility of adverse events, but may also indicate only a woman seeking reassurance, particularly in connection with a drug like mifepristone which induces bleeding. This is exactly the kind of complicated parsing the \uc2 FDA\uc2  considers every day when looking at studies on the safety and efficacy of drugs.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Jackson gave Danco\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer Jessica Ellsworth some space to explain why pharmaceutical companies have lined up in support of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceutical companies have \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant concerns\u8221\'20\'1D about judges parsing science from the bench, Ellsworth said, because they rely on the \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s gold standard review process\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have a district court that, among other things, relied on one study that was an analysis of anonymous blogposts,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ellsworth. Other studies \u8220\'20\'1Chave since been retracted for lack of scientific rigor and misleading data\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPrecisely because judges are not experts in statistics and the methodologies used for studies in clinical trials \u8211\'20\'13 that is why \uc2 FDA\uc2  has many hundreds of pages of analysis in the record of what the scientific data showed, and courts are just not in a position to parse\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Csecond guess\u8221\'20\'1D that, she said.\par \par \uc2 The most conservative justices want to talk about the Comstock Act\par \par \uc2 Alito and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who make up the most conservative faction of the court, both brought up the Comstock Act, an 1873 anti-obscenity law that bans mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials.\par \par \uc2 Although portions of the act are technically still on the books, the Comstock Act has long been regarded as an antique from another era of US history, as past court decisions narrowed its anti-obscenity provisions and Congress rolled back its restrictions on contraception.\par \par \uc2 But now that Roe no longer provides a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists claim the Comstock Act should be enforced and have seeded references to it in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  litigation, including the lawsuit over mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you respond to an argument that mailing your product and advertising it would violate the Comstock Act?\u8221\'20\'1D Thomas asked Ellsworth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that this case presents an opportunity for the court to opine on the reach of the statute,\u8221\'20\'1D Ellsworth replied, after some back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Thomas may see this case as an invitation to do just that, as he has in the past taken the opportunity to write minority opinions with far-reaching arguments. In the case overturning Roe, Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s opinion suggested the court should re-evaluate landmark cases establishing constitutional rights to same-sex marriage, same-sex intimacy and contraception.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has issued guidance declaring that the Comstock Act only applies if someone intends to break the law. If fully enforced \u8211\'20\'13 such as by a future president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the Comstock Act would result in a nationwide de facto ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , legal experts have said, since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, providers and patients rely heavily on the mail for equipment, medicine and other materials that are fundamental to providing care.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240326ek3q002pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
