\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  seems skeptical of arguments against  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,211 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par 20:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Key takeaways: \uc2 US supreme court\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill hearingDecision in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors\u8217\'20\'19 favor would apply across US and would probably make the drug more difficult to acquire\par \par \uc2 The supreme court on Tuesday seemed skeptical of arguments made by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors asking it to roll back the availability of mifepristone, a drug typically used in US medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The arguments were part of the first major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case to reach the justices since a 6-3 majority ruled in 2022 to overturn Roe v Wade and end the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rightwing groups that brought the case argued that the justices should roll back measures taken since 2016 by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) to expand the drug\u8217\'20\'19s availability. A decision in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors\u8217\'20\'19 favor would apply nationwide, including in states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and would probably make the drug more difficult to acquire.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now accounts for almost two-thirds of abortions performed in the US.\par \par \uc2 Much of Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s arguments focused on whether the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors who sued the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , a coalition known as the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, have standing, or the right to bring the case in the first place. The doctors claim they will suffer harm if they have to treat women who experience complications from mifepristone, an argument the Biden administration, which appealed the case to the court, has rejected as too speculative.\par \par \uc2 The US solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , who defended the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , argued that the doctors do not come within \u8220\'20\'1C100 miles\u8221\'20\'1D of having the legal right to bring the case, arguing that their case rests on a \u8220\'20\'1Clong chain of remote contingencies\u8221\'20\'1D. Under their argument, Prelogar said, a woman would have to face complications from a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that were so serious that she needed emergency care at a hospital \u8211\'20\'13 an unlikely scenario, given mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s proven safety record \u8211\'20\'13 and then end up in the care of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctor who was somehow forced into taking care of her in such a way it violated the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s conscience.\par \par \uc2 A number of the justices \u8211\'20\'13 even the ones who ruled to overturn Roe two years ago \u8211\'20\'13 seemed skeptical that the doctors met the threshold required to establish standing. Justices \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  seemed to seek assurances that the doctors represented by the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine were already covered by laws that protect doctors from having to undertake cases that violated their consciences.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  seemed to express concern over the sweeping implications of the doctors\u8217\'20\'19 request of the court. \u8220\'20\'1CThis case seems like a prime example of turning what could be a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on an \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule or any other federal government rule,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has historically rejected standing arguments based on such potential harm. However, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  raised the possibility that perhaps the court\u8217\'20\'19s own threshold for standing was too strict.\par \par \uc2 His fellow conservative \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  also seemed incredulous of Prelogar\u8217\'20\'19s argument. \u8220\'20\'1CIs there anybody who can sue and get a judicial ruling on whether what \uc2 FDA\uc2  did was lawful?\u8221\'20\'1D he asked. \u8220\'20\'1CShouldn\u8217\'20\'19t somebody be able to challenge that in court?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe in June 2022, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than a dozen states}}}\uc2  have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The result has been legal and medical chaos. Dozens of women have come forward to say that they were denied medically necessary abortions. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in states that still allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are overwhelmed by the flood of patients fleeing states with bans. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/19/abortion-increase-2023-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 1m abortions were performed}}}\uc2  in the US in 2023, a record high.\par \par \uc2 The availability of medication abortions, which are usually performed using mifepristone as well as another drug called misoprostol, has helped soften the impact of the bans. Telehealth medication abortions, permitted by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  since the pandemic, helped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/24/hawaii-mifepristone-abortion-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ease some of the burden}}}\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics; shield laws, passed in a handful of states, even allowed providers to offer telehealth abortions to people living in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 But the accessibility of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also made it the next target of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement after Roe was overturned. In 2022 the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine challenged the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2000 approval of mifepristone. The group, which includes anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and is being defended by the Christian powerhouse legal firm the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , argued that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  overstepped its authority and that mifepristone is unsafe. ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/01/health/abortion-pill-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 100 studies have concluded}}}\uc2  that mifepristone can be safely used to terminate a pregnancy.)\par \par \uc2 A federal judge ruled in favor of the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, a move that could have yanked mifepristone off the market entirely. But an appeals court narrowed that ruling, deciding that it was too late to challenge mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s original 2000 approval.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the appeals court ruled to rewind later measures taken by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  that expanded access to mifepristone, including by removing requirements that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers dispense mifepristone in person. A recent analysis found 16% of all US abortions are facilitated through telehealth.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s availability has remained unchanged as litigation has progressed.\par \par \uc2 During oral arguments, Thomas and Alito also raised the specter of the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-vice law that bans the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials. Long regarded as a relic, the Comstock Act has now seen a resurgence of post-Roe support among anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who believe it is the key to a de facto nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you respond to an argument that mailing your product and advertising it would violate the Comstock Act?\u8221\'20\'1D Thomas asked Jessica Ellsworth, a lawyer for Danco Laboratories, a manufacturer of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 After some back and forth, Ellsworth told Thomas: \u8220\'20\'1CThat statute has not been forced for nearly 100 years and I don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that this case presents an opportunity for the court to opine on the reach of the statute.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Regardless of what the supreme court decides, Americans will still be able to order mifepristone online from suppliers who help people \u8220\'20\'1Cself-manage\u8221\'20\'1D their own abortions. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/19/abortion-increase-2023-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A study released on Monday}}}\uc2  found self-managed abortions had soared since Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 Outrage over the overturning of Roe has turned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a key election issue, since most Americans support at least some degree of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Voters in multiple states, including conservative strongholds, have voted in ballot initiatives in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights; roughly a dozen states are now expected to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures to voters come November. Democrats are hoping that the issue will bolster turnout for their candidates, including Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is expected to issue a ruling in the mifepristone case by the summer, just months ahead of the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 The case\u8217\'20\'19s consequences could stretch far beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the justices greenlight attempts by ideologically driven groups to second-guess the authority of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , the agency\u8217\'20\'19s regulation of all manner of drugs \u8211\'20\'13 such as contraception and vaccines \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/25/abortion-pill-case-fda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could be challenged in court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ellsworth, the Danco attorney, argued that the doctors\u8217\'20\'19 argument in the case \u8220\'20\'1Cis so inflexible it would upend not just mifepristone, but virtually every drug approval\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240326ek3q0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
