\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Tip of the iceberg\u8217\'20\'19: US self-managed abortions soar post-Roe, study shows\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2024\par 16:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Analysis of data from suppliers outside US health system suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills used by more people than thought to evade bansExplainer: the mifepristone caseTell us: have you used an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill in the US?\par \par \uc2 In the six months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, roughly 26,000 more Americans used pills to induce their own at-home abortions than would have done so if Roe had not fallen, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2816817?resultClick=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a new study}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Published on Monday in Jama, one of the leading peer-reviewed medical journals in the United States, the study comes ahead of a key Tuesday hearing at the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  at which the justices will hear oral arguments in a case that could determine the future of a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Pills are used in 63% of all abortions within the US healthcare system, and the study suggests they are being used by even more people than previously known in order to evade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that now blanket much of the US.\par \par \uc2 Analyzing data from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill suppliers who operate outside of the US healthcare system, the study provides a rare window into the growing practice known as \u8220\'20\'1Cself-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. Although definitions of self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can vary, the practice generally refers to abortions that take place outside the formal healthcare system, without the aid of a US-based clinician.\par \par \uc2 The June 2022 decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, which overturned Roe and unleashed a wave of near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans across much of the US south and midwest, led to an explosion of interest in self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the study found.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is in place, it\u8217\'20\'19s very common for large numbers of people to look outside the formal healthcare setting to meet their needs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Abigail Aiken, the lead author on the study and an associate professor of public affairs at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhat was once considered a very sort of marginal practice now seems mainstream.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will consider arguments to roll back recent measures taken by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) to dramatically expand access to mifepristone, such as erasing requirements for people to pick up the pill in person. Those changes paved the way for US providers to start offering telehealth abortions within the formal healthcare system, including to people who live in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans; a recent analysis found that telehealth abortions now account for 16% of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 However, regardless of what the court does, its actions will not affect the thriving market for self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because suppliers tend to source their pills from overseas pharmacies based in countries such as India.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s not a way for the government to really police that system very effectively,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elisa Wells, another author on the paper and the co-director of Plan C, which runs an online database of organizations that offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to have much impact at all, except to maybe drive up the demand for those services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wells also believes that, should the supreme court try to curtail access to mifepristone, the resulting outrage will strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill suppliers. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery time there\u8217\'20\'19s an egregious court decision, that fuels activists and providers for finding new routes of access to help people access pills in the United States,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Medical experts widely agree that it is physically safe to self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  early on in pregnancy, and the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  even offers a protocol for doing so. It is also technically legal to end your own pregnancy in almost every state, including in the 16 states that have enacted near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. (Nevada has a law that explicitly criminalizes self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later on in pregnancy.) \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans generally target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, not patients.\par \par \uc2 However, experts have long warned that, if a prosecutor wants to punish someone for a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they will find a statute that is elastic enough to use. Even before the fall of Roe, people had faced criminal consequences over alleged self-managed abortions. Between 2000 and 2020, 61 people were criminally investigated or arrested on suspicion of ending their own pregnancies or helping someone else do so, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/30/self-managed-abortions-arrest-investiagtion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an October 2023 report found}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The study released on Monday examined the provision of pills from three different kinds of providers: community networks, which are groups of volunteers who usually offer pills free of charge; telemedicine organizations that provide pills with the aid of clinicians who are based outside the United States; and online vendors, which are straightforward businesses that simply sell pills.\par \par \uc2 The study\u8217\'20\'19s authors obtained data directly from most of these sources, although the authors also used statistical modeling to estimate how many pills may be provided from other sources that did not supply data to researchers. Including those estimates, researchers reported that these suppliers provided almost 40,000 pills in the six months following the demise of Roe. Had Roe not fallen, the researchers estimated that they would have provided closer to 10,000 pills.\par \par \uc2 In the six months after Roe, suppliers provided almost 6,000 pills for self-managed abortions each month, the study found. That\u8217\'20\'19s a monthly increase of more than 300% compared with before the fall of Roe. More than half of all these pills were provided by community networks.\par \par \uc2 Using past studies as well as providers\u8217\'20\'19 own estimates of how often their pills were used, researchers also calculated how many more self-managed abortions took place after Roe compared with how many would have taken place had the landmark ruling survived. Ultimately, they estimated that 26,055 more self-managed abortions took place than if Roe had not fallen.\par \par \uc2 The landscape of self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has probably changed dramatically even since the end of the period captured in the study, which ended in December 2022, Aiken and Wells said. More and more vendors have entered the marketplace, driving down the cost of pills. Community networks are seeing increased requests this year, Wells added.\par \par \uc2 The numbers in the Monday study, she said, \u8220\'20\'1Care the tip of the iceberg in terms of what\u8217\'20\'19s happening out there\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240325ek3p002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
