\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Pregnant Arizona lawmaker shares fight to get  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 , highlighting \u8216\'20\'18cruel\u8217\'20\'19 laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 995 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2024\par 20:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Eva Burch, who gave 10-minute speech on state senate floor, may be first pregnant state lawmaker to detail plans since fall of Roe\par \par \uc2 When Eva Burch learned that her pregnancy was not progressing and decided to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Burch and her husband quickly made another decision: Burch, who is a Democratic state senator in Arizona, was going to speak up about it \u8211\'20\'13 from the floor of the state senate.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, as her voice shook and a group of women surrounded her, Burch gave a 10-minute speech about her decision and the struggle to navigate Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ccoercive\u8221\'20\'1D maze of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. With her speech, Burch joined the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/16/abortion-ban-lawsuits-pregnancy-complication-emergency-kate-cox"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ranks of women who have spoken out}}}\uc2  since the fall of Roe v Wade about their battles to get abortions even for nonviable pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Yet Burch, who was still pregnant when she spoke on the floor as well as during an interview, is believed to be the first pregnant state lawmaker to go into such detail about her plans to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I can take this sad situation that I find myself in and make it actionable, help get people engaged, help people to turn out to vote, help people to care about this and to pay attention to their local governments \u8211\'20\'13 where so many of these decisions are being made \u8211\'20\'13 then it\u8217\'20\'19s worth it to me,\u8221\'20\'1D Burch said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn spite of the tragedy of it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Burch, who has two young sons, has experienced infertility for years. She was not trying to get pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 but when she discovered she was, Burch and her husband were happy and hopeful.\par \par \uc2 That happiness would dissolve as tests revealed Burch\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy was not advancing and would end in a miscarriage, Burch said. She had experienced a miscarriage two years earlier and knew immediately she would prefer to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona bans \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  after 15 weeks}}}\uc2 , without exceptions for rape or incest. Burch was well below that limit, but still, she said, she had to overcome an obstacle course to get care.\par \par \uc2 Arizona law mandates that patients visit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic for counseling 24 hours before undergoing the procedure. Although Burch said she had already received an ultrasound and did not need another, she said she still had to undergo a transvaginal ultrasound, which involves inserting a wand into a patient\u8217\'20\'19s vagina. State law also required Burch\u8217\'20\'19s provider to read a statement to her about alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of the statements read to me, for example, advised me that there are alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including adoption or parenting, which in my case is particularly unfair and unkind and cruel, since parenting is an option I would love to take on,\u8221\'20\'1D Burch said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe intent is clear: they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to make sure that it is accurate and appropriate care. They want patients to make a different decision. They want patients to make a decision that the legislators feel more comfortable with.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Notably, Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws remain up in the air. In 1864, before Arizona became a state, its territorial government enacted a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that only permits abortions to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life. While Roe was the law of the land, that ban went dormant; in 2022, legislators passed the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which Roe also blocked from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 When Roe fell, confusion broke out over whether the near-total ban or the 15-week ban was in effect; amid the chaos, all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state temporarily shut down as courts warred over the two bans. The state supreme court is now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/12/arizona-abortion-ban-supreme-court#:~:text=The%20ban%20blocks%20people%20from,exceptions%20for%20rape%20or%20incest."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weighing whether to reinstate the near-total ban}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Burch was nearly caught up in that upheaval. In June 2022, she became pregnant with another nonviable pregnancy and scheduled an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The night before, Burch recalled, she started to bleed heavily and went to the emergency department. There, she learned that providers could no longer detect any activity from the tissue that would \u8211\'20\'13 in a healthy pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 ultimately become a heart. But a doctor there refused to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because, Burch said, she was not bleeding heavily enough.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey had offered to give me some medication to make me start bleeding again and said if I bled enough, I could have had a procedure there in the hospital,\u8221\'20\'1D said Burch, who is also a nurse practitioner and has worked in a women\u8217\'20\'19s health clinic. \u8220\'20\'1CThat sounded awful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Instead, she underwent her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a clinic the next day.\par \par \uc2 Burch\u8217\'20\'19s ordeal unfolded just two weeks before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe and Arizona clinics shut down.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHad the situation taken place two weeks later, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been able to go access that care,\u8221\'20\'1D Burch said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I had needed to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I would have had to leave the state to do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She continued: \u8220\'20\'1CThis time around, I knew what to expect. I went in there eyes wide open. But it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make it any easier. The laws here are not going to become palatable here because patients know what\u8217\'20\'19s coming. We just deserve better care than what we\u8217\'20\'19re getting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters in Arizona are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/10/democrats-ballot-measures-protect-abortion-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now gathering signatures}}}\uc2  for a ballot measure that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution. Advocates in roughly a dozen states are hoping to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the ballot come November; so far, every time Americans have directly voted on post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters have won.\par \par \uc2 Because Arizona is an anticipated swing state in the 2024 elections, the push for the ballot measure \u8211\'20\'13 which Democrats also hope will boost turnout amid lackluster support for Joe Biden \u8211\'20\'13 is sure to attract nationwide attention.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI absolutely hope that by sharing my story,\u8221\'20\'1D Burch said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe can move the needle for voters in November.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240320ek3k002mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
