\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s seen as a war zone\u8217\'20\'19: the lone  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  activist defying militias and the far right\b0\par\par\uc2 Cassidy Randall \par \uc2 2,734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2024\par 13:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jen Jackson Quintano is her region\u8217\'20\'19s only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizer. Faced with a \u8216\'20\'18culture of silence\u8217\'20\'19, she\u8217\'20\'19s platforming women \u8211\'20\'13 and changing minds\par \par \uc2 Last January, Jen Jackson Quintano stepped into a theater in Sandpoint, a tiny city in northern Idaho, to debut a production that could best be described as The Vagina Monologues meets The Moth \u8211\'20\'13 a night of Idahoans sharing stories about their own reproductive agency.\par \par \uc2 Quintano was nervous. Idaho, where Republicans outnumber Democrats five to one, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/13/idaho-extreme-abortion-ban-medical-emergency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of the most punitive \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2  in the country. Further, Quintano lives in a region of the state that keeps making national headlines for bold displays of armed intimidation by militia, white supremacists, and Christian nationalists. This was not necessarily a safe place to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So that afternoon, as people began filing into the theater, she considered worst-case scenarios \u8211\'20\'13 even though she\u8217\'20\'19d promoted the event mostly by word of mouth to avoid alerting disruptors, ensured law enforcement had patrols in the area, and brought in a peacekeeper force of local volunteers trained in de-escalation tactics. One attendee, wearing high heels, stashed sneakers in her bag in case she had to run. Another kept her coat on and her purse on her shoulder for a hasty exit.\par \par \uc2 Quintano wasn\u8217\'20\'19t a full-time activist; she\u8217\'20\'19d pulled this event together in her spare time between running a chainsaw and driving one-ton trucks for her family\u8217\'20\'19s arborist business. She empathized with attendees\u8217\'20\'19 apprehension: \u8220\'20\'1CMy husband had volunteered as a peacekeeper, my mother-in-law was in the audience, we were all there. And I had this morbid thought: what is my daughter left with if shit goes down?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Quintano, 44, isn\u8217\'20\'19t an inconspicuous target. Tall and lanky, with a purple streak in her blond hair and a silver ring glinting in her nose, she\u8217\'20\'19s easy to pick out of a crowd. In a year-plus as north Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s lone \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizer, she\u8217\'20\'19s had no qualms about showing herself; her face appears all over the website and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theprovoiceproject.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pro-Voice Project}}}\uc2 , the organization she founded last March to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storytelling in Idaho. \u8220\'20\'1CHow can I ask other people to put themselves out there, if I\u8217\'20\'19m not willing to do that myself?\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 But nothing out of the ordinary happened at the performance \u8211\'20\'13 except for 200 people attending in a town with a population of just 9,000. Afterwards, Quintano walked into the local pub, where a group of men who\u8217\'20\'19d been in attendance were talking openly about reproductive rights. A couple weeks later, a man cornered her in the grocery store and told her about a woman he had been with years ago having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19d never told anyone this, and he said, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m going to write it down now, it\u8217\'20\'19s time for me to share it with my kids,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Quintano says. Another woman, who\u8217\'20\'19d submitted a story for the performance anonymously and even deleted the file from her computer after she\u8217\'20\'19d printed it out, has since become a vocal advocate for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 They were glimmers of hope in a pocket of the country that\u8217\'20\'19s a stronghold for extremism. In the last few years, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sandpointreader.com/area-youths-protest-george-floyd-killing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an armed militia descended}}}\uc2  on a Black Lives Matter march in Sandpoint led by high schoolers, and a librarian in a nearby town was forced to resign after gun-toting local residents packed the library where she refused to censor books. People regularly drove past the house of the former Sandpoint mayor Shelby Rognstad, a relatively progressive politician, to hurl obscenities at his children; one of his sons escaped an attempted kidnapping, according to the family. The ultra-conservative Christ Church subjugates women \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/m7ezwx/inside-the-church-that-preaches-wives-need-to-be-led-with-a-firm-hand"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sometimes through sexual violence}}}\uc2 , as Vice reported in 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/02/christ-church-idaho-theocracy-us-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 steadily gaining ground}}}\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 How can I ask other people to put themselves out there, if I\u8217\'20\'19m not willing to do that myself?\par \par \uc2 Jen Jackson Quintano\par \par \uc2 These are only a smattering of the examples that all add up to \u8220\'20\'1Ca culture of silence\u8221\'20\'1D in north Idaho, Rognstad\u8217\'20\'19s wife, Katherine Greenland, tells me.\par \par \uc2 The same fear-based politics dominate the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/maps/state/idaho/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 begins at conception}}}\uc2  and subjects physicians to revoked medical licenses and felony convictions; OB-GYNs have since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/22/abortion-idaho-women-rights-healthcare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fled the state}}}\uc2 . The ban also allows family members, including those of rapists, to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/11/idaho-abortion-law-blocked"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attempts to criminalize}}}\uc2  helping minors get abortions outside the state without parental consent. Bills are on the table in the current legislative session to remove the rape and incest exception from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and redefine language in Idaho law from \u8220\'20\'1Cfetus\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Cpreborn child\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Recent Planned Parenthood polling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/planned-parenthood-alliance-advocates/issues/idaho-opinion-research-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shows}}}\uc2  that 65% of Idahoans believe that women should have access to all available reproductive healthcare options, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and 45% of Idahoans identify as pro-choice. But many people here are afraid to speak out, often for fear of literally being shot, an environment that makes it difficult to organize against far-right policies that endanger women.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been in touch with a lot of reporters, and Idaho \u8211\'20\'13 north Idaho specifically \u8211\'20\'13 tends to be approached as something of a war zone,\u8221\'20\'1D Quintano says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a sense from the outside, and even in our community, that this is really dangerous work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation is extreme, but it isn\u8217\'20\'19t isolated: In April, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  will consider whether Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violates the only federal protection for women needing emergency abortions, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (Emtala). Women in need of life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care have been turned away from emergency rooms all over the country since Roe v Wade was overturned \u8211\'20\'13 and if the supreme court eviscerates Emtala, women with serious pregnancy complications will be at even greater risk. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jezebel.com/anti-abortion-movement-details-its-2025-plans-and-theyr-1851209007"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are eager}}}\uc2  for a Trump win in November, which would give them a better shot at restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like Idaho did across the country (Trump himself reportedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/02/16/us/politics/trump-abortion-ban.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wants}}}\uc2  a 16-week ban). But Quintano says that even in the strictest states, the future of reproductive rights shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be written off.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 A year after that inaugural Pro-Voice Project performance in Sandpoint, Quintano drops her nine-year-old daughter off at school and settles in at her donated downtown office space. Though she raised over $30,000 last year in small donations, which she put toward the Pro-Voice Project\u8217\'20\'19s eight community storytelling events in 2023, major funding hasn\u8217\'20\'19t materialized. She\u8217\'20\'19s been running mostly on passion and anger.\par \par \uc2 When the Dobbs decision overturning Roe came down from the supreme court in June 2022, Quintano was in the midst of drafting a memoir about being drugged and raped, part of an abusive relationship, and needing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result of that relationship.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was writing on these themes of lack of agency, lack of voice, lack of autonomy, as a woman in this world,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. When she saw that same lack of agency institutionalized for an entire nation of women, \u8220\'20\'1Ca massive amount of rage bubbled up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But there was nowhere to channel it into action in north Idaho. \u8220\'20\'1CI think probably all of us, when Dobbs happened, looked around and thought, \u8216\'20\'18Someone\u8217\'20\'19s got this, right? There are trained people who\u8217\'20\'19ve been waiting for this to happen who will pick up the ball and run with it,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she recalls. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we were all just looking at each other.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So Quintano picked up the ball, starting the Pro-Voice Project with the belief that public storytelling could make a difference even where legislated policy change seemed impossible. Then, in spring 2023, Bonner General hospital in Quintano\u8217\'20\'19s hometown {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/22/abortion-idaho-women-rights-healthcare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closed}}}\uc2  its labor and delivery ward, leaving 50,000 people in north Idaho without obstetrical care. ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://idahocapitalsun.com/2023/11/28/idahos-infant-mother-death-rate-is-rising-new-report-finds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s maternal mortality rate shot up 122%}}}\uc2  from 2019 to 2021; in 2023, Idaho {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://healthandwelfare.idaho.gov/about-dhw/boards-councils-committees/maternal-mortality-review-committee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disbanded}}}\uc2  the committee tasked with looking into the causes of maternal mortality.) National media and reproductive rights organizations reached out to her as the authority in the region, local women looked to her for leadership, and Quintano suddenly became a full-time activist.\par \par \uc2 We can\u8217\'20\'19t forget how powerful story is in making people understand complicated topics, and in changing hearts and minds\par \par \uc2 Dr Amelia Huntsberger\par \par \uc2 To mourn the closing of Bonner General\u8217\'20\'19s maternity ward, Quintano organized an UnHappy Hour at a local brewery in April. The day before the event, security footage captured a figure in camouflage casing the venue with a camera. She again ensured a local police presence, but she didn\u8217\'20\'19t consider canceling. Nine years ago, when her home birth went awry, she had been rushed to Bonner General for an emergency C-section, without which she and her daughter might have died. Now women in her situation have to drive 45 miles to the nearest hospital or be helicoptered in an air ambulance.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 400 people packed the UnHappy Hour sharing Quintano\u8217\'20\'19s sadness and anger. And again, nothing violent occurred.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1CUnHappy Hour\u8221\'20\'1D branding was pure Quintano, who meets fear and intimidation with dark wit: employing slogans like \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho is clit-erally the worst\u8221\'20\'1D, calling Sandpoint \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the four horsemen of the women\u8217\'20\'19s health apocalypse\u8221\'20\'1D, and offering funny facts to warm up crowds (\u8220\'20\'1CDid you know that \uc2 Nasa\uc2  asked Sally Ride if 100 tampons was enough for a week in space?\u8221\'20\'1D).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an issue where people want to shut down because they\u8217\'20\'19re afraid. Humor is a way to combat that, a way to open people up,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CPlus, if we\u8217\'20\'19re not laughing, we\u8217\'20\'19re crying, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not sustainable to feel like we\u8217\'20\'19re being punched in the gut every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theprovoiceproject.com/stories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stories people share}}}\uc2  through the Pro-Voice Project \u8211\'20\'13 about the agony of forced pregnancy, about miscarriages and being shamed by pharmacists for using birth control \u8211\'20\'13 can also feel like a punch in the gut, many of them are about the common reasons women and families seek abortions: financial insecurity, becoming pregnant despite taking every precaution, being trapped in abusive relationships.\par \par \uc2 Dr Amelia Huntsberger, one of the OB-GYNs who fled Idaho last year, and who is now an advisor to the Pro-Voice Project, says, \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t forget how powerful story is in making people understand complicated topics, and in changing hearts and minds. That\u8217\'20\'19s Jen\u8217\'20\'19s gift to Idaho, and beyond its borders.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Storytelling about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool used by both national and grassroots organizing groups, though its success is hard to quantify \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s subversive by nature, Quintano says. But she does share a concrete example: in Sandpoint, people who were afraid even to say the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D have now told their stories, emboldening others to do the same.\par \par \uc2 Quintano won\u8217\'20\'19t allow herself to burn out (even though she feels close lately, with all this unpaid labor). She\u8217\'20\'19s playing the long game: while up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/issue-brief/addressing-abortion-access-through-state-ballot-initiatives/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 13 other states}}}\uc2  will have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot this election cycle, there\u8217\'20\'19s barely a murmur of a ballot initiative in Idaho. Quintano sees it as one of her end goals, but legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is five or more years off for her state, she thinks. Step one is to make sure women don\u8217\'20\'19t die, by convincing politicians to at least walk back the most egregious language of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 After rushing from her office to lead a volunteer meeting and then to chat with an author working on a book about whisper networks, Quintano drives through the early dark of the Sandpoint winter to the home of one of her board members. Cynthia Dalsing is a retired nurse midwife, although she says that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have robbed her of a peaceful retirement. Quintano and Dalsing are hosting a viewing that evening of On the Brink, the recent Nightline episode on the impact of restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws across the country.\par \par \uc2 Quintano invited several people in the healthcare community to the viewing, both from private practices and Bonner General, which has gone largely silent on any aspect of women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare. Only two physicians show: psychiatrists, both young mothers themselves, who provide counseling to pregnant women and new mothers. They say they\u8217\'20\'19ve stopped documenting in their notes when a patient expresses ambivalence or negative thoughts about pregnancy. Even though research shows {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/abortion-restrictions-may-increased-suicide-risk-younger-women-rcna63358"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forced pregnancy may lead}}}\uc2  to increased risk of suicide, these psychiatrists feel they can\u8217\'20\'19t document any negative aspect of patients\u8217\'20\'19 attitudes for fear that the state could pull such records and use them against patients.\par \par \uc2 Then another guest arrives: Jim Woodward, a Sandpoint Republican who served in the Idaho state senate from 2019-2022. He voted yes on all anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, but lost his seat in the 2022 election to Scott Herndon, a far-right \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolitionist who believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be treated as homicide and punished with jail time or even the death penalty. In the 2023 session, Herndon tried to remove Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s rape and incest exception and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ktvb.com/article/news/local/idaho-press/idaho-senator-unsuccessfully-tries-to-remove-rape-incest-abortion-exemptions/277-871e7800-09c3-4b35-8c13-8ddfae0bb81f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called rape}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Can opportunity to have the child \u8230\'20\'26 if the rape actually occurred\u8221\'20\'1D. (Herndon declined to be interviewed for this story.)\par \par \uc2 Woodward is running to retake the seat this year. He says he and many other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators didn\u8217\'20\'19t foresee the ramifications of the bills they voted for. Part of his platform, which promises to reclaim his community from extremism, is to somewhat ease legislative language that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take the mother\u8217\'20\'19s future health into account and calls for felony convictions for physicians. He didn\u8217\'20\'19t hesitate to accept Quintano\u8217\'20\'19s invitation to the viewing party. \u8220\'20\'1CWe should be able to talk about all of these things, and do it in a nice way,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. Two days later, he shares stories from the Nightline episode with attendees at a campaign meet and greet.\par \par \uc2 This is what Quintano means about challenging people\u8217\'20\'19s assumptions: otherwise, every newly outrageous development \u8211\'20\'13 like South Carolina Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/10/republican-wave-state-bills-homicide-charges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 push for legislation}}}\uc2  to execute a woman for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the Alabama supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/23/fertility-doctors-reaction-alabama-embryo-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  that frozen embryos are children \u8211\'20\'13 will further normalize the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such moves aren\u8217\'20\'19t relegated to extremist pockets of the nation, either. \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho has been a frontrunner on a lot of other states\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kelly O\u8217\'20\'19Neill, Idaho litigation attorney with the organization Legal Voice and advisor for the Pro-Voice Project. \u8220\'20\'1CEven in a state where there are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections right now, it might not be that way for ever. We can\u8217\'20\'19t get complacent, and when people hear \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories from their next-door neighbor or their sister-in-law, or their son\u8217\'20\'19s preschool teacher, it is a powerful way to remind people that politics are affecting you every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Quintano has spoken about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in venues flying Confederate flags and been barred altogether from other engagements. The Pro-Voice Project just hosted a \u8220\'20\'1Crally for repro\u8221\'20\'1D at Bonner General and is gearing up to mark the \u8220\'20\'1Cone-year anniversary of the loss of our OB-GYNs\u8221\'20\'1D. People still tell Quintano they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to come to her storytelling performances for fear of violence. A full year into leading very public organizing efforts, though, she\u8217\'20\'19s yet to see any major backlash.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to paint the picture that there\u8217\'20\'19s no danger in speaking out,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. As her work gains visibility, she thinks it might attract harmful attention. \u8220\'20\'1CBut if I can do this really publicly, with little in the way of blowback, then that means your average citizen has a lot of room to be safely vocal about their beliefs. If we are silenced, the other side has won.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 In the US, you can call or text the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://988lifeline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Suicide Prevention Lifeline}}}\uc2  on 988, chat on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://988lifeline.org/chat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 988lifeline.org}}}\uc2 , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.crisistextline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 text HOME}}}\uc2  to 741741 to connect with a crisis counselor. In the UK and Ireland, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.samaritans.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samaritans}}}\uc2  can be contacted on freephone 116 123, or email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.org}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.ie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.ie}}}\uc2 . In Australia, the crisis support service {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lifeline.org.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lifeline}}}\uc2  is 13 11 14. Other international helplines can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.befrienders.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 befrienders.org}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240312ek3c001mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
