\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union: raucous, strident and insistently optimistic\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2024\par 17:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 His remarks were designed to demonstrate Biden\u8217\'20\'19s vitality. They succeeded\par \par \uc2 Like a budget, a State of the Union speech is a moral document: it reflects a president\u8217\'20\'19s values and priorities, distilling his own view of his administration for the American people. On Thursday night, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made his moral case for re-election: he views America as a besieged but worthy global leader, one whose tradition of democracy deserves to be defended and rebuilt. Referring to his opponent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  only as \u8220\'20\'1Cmy predecessor\u8221\'20\'1D, Biden repeatedly contrasted his own vision of a more equitable and prosperous nation with the Republican agenda. The point was to offer Americans an optimistic and inclusive vision \u8211\'20\'13 and to remind them of the cynicism, sadism and depravity of the Trump worldview, which threatens to undermine women\u8217\'20\'19s freedoms, make interracial democracy impossible, and use the machinery of government for little else but to further Republicans own self regard and greed.\par \par \uc2 The 90-minute speech was raucous, strident and insistently optimistic; it appeared designed to demonstrate Biden\u8217\'20\'19s vitality, and to launch in earnest a presidential campaign that has previously been somewhat tepid and sluggish. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m here to wake up the Congress,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said as he began, declaring the nation to be in \u8220\'20\'1Can unprecedented moment\u8221\'20\'1D. Maybe he was there to wake up his own campaign, too.\par \par \uc2 Biden opened his speech outlining the three major issues which his campaign sees as the greatest emergencies: the foreign threat to Democracy, as represented by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s threat to Nato; the decline of democracy at home, as represented by January 6 and Republican lies about the 2020 election (\u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t love your country only when you win,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden bellowed; an early applause line); and reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time that Biden gave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pride of place in the speech, reflecting his campaign\u8217\'20\'19s belated awareness, in the wake of the 2022 midterms, of the issue\u8217\'20\'19s salience. The attention he paid to the issue reflected his ambivalence toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hostility toward the feminist case for it. The section began not with a question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with IVF: an Alabama court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to grant frozen embryos the status of legal persons, thus briefly banning the treatment in the state, seems to have opened a new avenue in the post-Dobbs debate that is more comfortable for Biden.\par \par \uc2 He moved on to telling the story of Kate Cox, a Texas mother who was forced to flee the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the ban in place there put her at risk for catastrophic health complications. Republicans, he noted, were planning to impose a national ban on reproductive freedom. \u8220\'20\'1CMy God,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1CWhat other freedoms would they take away?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s pitch to Americans on Thursday night often seemed to have female voters in mind\par \par \uc2 It was not what reproductive rights advocates were hoping for: the speech made no mention of women\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of equality and dignity, casting \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive rights\u8221\'20\'1D, as Biden exclusively referred to them, as matters of bare health and dutiful family building. Still, Biden is not making these more robust endorsements of women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive freedoms because he does not think he has to: his campaign is betting that voters are galvanized enough by the issue that half measures will deliver their votes.\par \par \uc2 They might be right. Indeed, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s pitch to Americans on Thursday night often seemed to have female voters in mind. His proposed tax increases for corporations and the wealthiest strata of Americans were pitched not as mere fairness, but as a means to generate investment in care infrastructure \u8211\'20\'13 childcare, paid family leave and eldercare \u8211\'20\'13 the neglect of which has led to a nationwide crisis of overburdened and economically straightjacketed women.\par \par \uc2 Roe, too, was framed as an invitation for women to not just vote their interests but avenge their citizenship. \u8220\'20\'1CThose bragging about overturning Roe v Wade have no idea about the power of women,\u8221\'20\'1D said Biden, referring to a now famous line in \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s majority opinion on Dobbs saying that \u8220\'20\'1Cwomen are not without political or electoral power\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re about to find out just how powerful women are.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The main thrust of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech was meant to flaunt his economic accomplishments, to reshape the popular story of the American economy \u8211\'20\'13 one where consumers are hampered by inflation and nobody can buy a house \u8211\'20\'13 into a story of a remarkable post-pandemic recovery. He flaunted the growth of small businesses and the low unemployment rate; he tipped his hat to the economic \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D engineered by Jerome Powell, which has kept the US out of a long-predicted recession. He made a mild dig at the media as he tried to rewrite their own story: \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people are writing the greatest comeback story ever told.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The speech was strong; every position taken was not. Biden fell apart when he tried to talk about the border, touting his own sadistically cruel bill by way of bragging that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had scared all the Republicans out of voting for it. His indifference to the human lives of migrants was at times chilling: he referred offhandedly to \u8220\'20\'1Cillegals\u8221\'20\'1D, and engaged in a bizarre and unnecessary bit of theater with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , decked out in garish Maga gear, who yelled at Biden about a woman murdered by an undocumented immigrant.\par \par \uc2 He stumbled, too, when he spoke of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war on Gaza, dwelling in lurid detail on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s atrocities on 7 October and only offhandedly acknowledging that more than 30,000 Palestinians have been murdered by Israel in the past five months. The issue has proved an albatross for Biden, who is hemorrhaging support among young voters and voters of color over his support for Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war. As he spoke about Gaza, \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , the only Palestinian American in Congress, wept. But this, too, may be an issue on which Biden relies on the horror of his alternative: his campaign seems to be betting that these voters will return to Biden in spite of his stance on Gaza, because Trump, who spoke recently of a desire to \u8220\'20\'1Cfinish the problem\u8221\'20\'1D in Palestine, is so much worse.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had reason to be nervous about Biden\u8217\'20\'19s performance ahead of the speech. The past few weeks of the news cycle have been dominated by internal Democratic fears about Biden\u8217\'20\'19s age, a worry that seems to stand in for all sorts of other, perhaps more pertinent, worries about his ability to hold together his massive and internally fractious coalition. But to the extent to which the claims of concern over Biden\u8217\'20\'19s age were sincere, he seemed determined to put them to rest: he contrasted his own presidency with that of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s on every issue except his age.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know I don\u8217\'20\'19t look it,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said towards the end of his speech, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut I\u8217\'20\'19ve been around a while\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a joke reminiscent of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s famous quip, \u8220\'20\'1CI refuse to exploit my opponent\u8217\'20\'19s youth and inexperience.\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not how old we are,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the age of our ideas. It\u8217\'20\'19s a line that seems certain to be repeated throughout the campaign, as the Democrats seek to make the presidential election less a referendum on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s age than on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s intolerable proposed future.\par \par \uc2 Indeed Biden did seem energized, enthused. His gait was stiffer than last year and his stutter persists, but he came alive, oddly enough, when he was being heckled. He retorted gamely and happily when Republicans screamed at him from the audience; he appeared most comfortable, most confident, when he was being yelled at. \u8220\'20\'1CTurning setback into comeback \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s what America does,\u8221\'20\'1D he said at one point in his speech. He was talking about the post-Covid economy. But he could have been talking about his re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240308ek38002gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
