\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US pharma group opposing  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill restrictions also backs Republicans attacking drug\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 987 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2024\par 14:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Group advocating to preserve \uc2 FDA\uc2  judgment in supreme court case also gave $125,000 to Republicans opposing mifepristone\par \par \uc2 A top trade group for pharmaceutical companies has asked the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  not to shred the power of the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an upcoming case}}}\uc2  that could cut access to a drug commonly used in abortions. But in a move that appears to undermine its own position, it has also given more than $125,000 to a GOP organization that backs the Republicans who want the supreme court to do exactly the opposite.\par \par \uc2 The trade group, \uc2 Pharmaceutical Research and Manufacturers of America\uc2 , or \uc2 PhRMA\uc2 , lobbies on behalf of brand-name drug manufacturers. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23-235/284838/20231012134701542_PhRMA%20Amicus%20Br.%20ISO%20Petitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October}}}\uc2  and January, it filed briefs in an upcoming supreme court case that threatens to curtail access to a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug called mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/13/abortion-pill-us-supreme-court-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high court will consider a ruling}}}\uc2  by the conservative fifth circuit court of appeals that, if upheld, would significantly roll back access to the drug, which has been expanded in a series of regulatory actions by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . The supreme court justices may also rule on some of the more technical elements of the case, such as whether the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have the legal right to bring the case in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Dragging the \uc2 FDA\uc2  into the United States\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars could have vast consequences for all kinds of drugs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23-235/299289/20240130165252454_2024.01.30%20-%20Nos.%2023-235%2023-236%20-%20PhRMA%20Amicus%20Br.%20ISO%20Petitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 PhRMA warned in its January}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23-235/299289/20240130165252454_2024.01.30%20-%20Nos.%2023-235%2023-236%20-%20PhRMA%20Amicus%20Br.%20ISO%20Petitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brief}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor decades, biopharmaceutical companies, healthcare providers, patients, and other stakeholders have relied on \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expert scientific judgment on drug approval, labeling, and post-approval marketing requirements,\u8221\'20\'1D lawyers for \uc2 PhRMA\uc2  wrote. The federal appeals court ruling, they added, \u8220\'20\'1Cthreatens limitless litigation by inviting virtually any healthcare provider to bring suit to challenge any drug approval or subsequent change\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yet according to research by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://accountable.us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Accountable.US}}}\uc2  that has been independently verified by the Guardian, in May 2023, \uc2 PhRMA\uc2  gave more than $125,000 to the Republican Attorneys General Association (Raga), which seeks to elect and re-elect GOP state attorneys general. Just a few weeks before that donation, 19 of the Republican state attorneys general within Raga filed a brief with the supreme court alleging that the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone had \u8220\'20\'1Cbasic legal flaws\u8221\'20\'1D. The agency\u8217\'20\'19s approach to mifepristone, the attorneys general suggested, even defied federal law.\par \par \uc2 In January 2023, several of those attorneys general also sent a letter to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s commissioner accusing the agency of having \u8220\'20\'1Cabdicated its responsibility to protect women\u8217\'20\'19s health\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A few months later, in April 2023, a Texas federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/07/abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone-trump-judge-matthew-kacsmaryk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  in favor of a coalition of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups who asked him to entirely suspend the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2000 authorization of mifepristone. The fifth circuit narrowed that ruling, rolling back more recent measures by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  that, since 2016, have expanded access to mifepristone. The Biden administration and mifepristone\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturer have appealed that decision to the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 More than 100 different studies, conducted over several years and countries, have concluded that mifepristone is safe, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/01/health/abortion-pill-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 2023 New York Times review found}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 PhRMA\uc2  claims to oppose this lawsuit that could deny women access to the safe, widely-used mifepristone, yet they bankrolled the top political group for anti-choice attorneys general who further threaten access,\u8221\'20\'1D Caroline Ciccone, president of the non-partisan government watchdog group Accountable.US.\par \par \uc2 Trade groups like \uc2 PhRMA\uc2  don\u8217\'20\'19t typically give donations to pursue some ideological goal, according to Sarah Bryner, director of research and strategy of OpenSecrets, which tracks money in US politics. They are interested in getting their lobbyists into the offices of whoever is in power.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re seeking access to members [of Congress] and elected officials, once those people take office,\u8221\'20\'1D Bryner said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve heard from members in the past saying that there\u8217\'20\'19s 24 hours in a day and you have to sleep, so who are you going to have meetings with? Typically, you\u8217\'20\'19ll want to have meetings with constituents and then also the people who give you money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To better their chances of maintaining that access, corporations and the groups that represent their interests frequently and quietly donate to both sides of the aisle in US politics. Three weeks after it made a donation of $125,000 to the Republican Attorneys General Association, \uc2 PhRMA\uc2  gave another $125,000 to the group\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic rival, the Democratic Attorneys General Association.\par \par \uc2 Even political earthquakes rarely shake their approach to political giving. When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, major US companies that indicated they would help employees evade state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans continued to financially support political candidates who backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/06/us-companies-abortion-rights-donation-anti-abortion-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Guardian analysis found}}}\uc2 . After the US Capitol riots on 6 January 2021, several major companies vowed to suspend or reconsider their donations to politicians who declined to certify the results of the 2020 election. But by early 2023, more than 70 companies who\u8217\'20\'19d made such promises {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/01/06/corporations-election-objectors-donations-00076668"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gave more than $10m}}}\uc2  to election deniers, Politico reported.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou might hear messaging, you might see temporary breaks in giving and you might see people not give to people who are very outspoken and at the fringes like Marjorie Taylor Greene, for example, or people in Alabama right now,\u8221\'20\'1D Bryner said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think, generally, bipartisan giving is not going anywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe engage with different groups who have a wide array of different policy opinions and priorities,\u8221\'20\'1D Alex Schriver, senior vice-president of public affairs at \uc2 PhRMA\uc2 , said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe may not agree on every issue, but we believe engagement and dialogue is important to promoting a health care policy environment that supports innovation, a highly-skilled workforce and access to life-saving medicines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The rulings curtailing the availability of mifepristone are now on hold. The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  will hear oral arguments in the case in late March.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240308ek38001me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
