\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rates stable even as states that restrict procedure see steep decline\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani \par \uc2 601 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2024\par 14:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 States next to other states with total ban have seen largest increase in rates, while US telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates also grew\par \par \uc2 Abortions have dramatically declined in the 14 states that have criminalized the health procedure since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, with thousands of women traveling out of state for reproductive care, new figures reveal.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the total number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abortions}}}\uc2  carried out with medical supervision in the US remained consistent at about 80,000 a month between June 2022 and September 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 but where and how patients access this urgent medical care has changed, according to #WeCount, a research initiative measuring the impact of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization decision, which allowed states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any point in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant people in the south are struggling the most to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which can be life-saving, with the steepest declines in procedures recorded in Texas, Georgia, Tennessee, Louisiana and Alabama.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been banned in 14 states, an estimated 120,930 abortions would have been carried out in Alabama, Arkansas, Idaho, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, Missouri, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, West Virginia and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 The data suggests that people were forced to travel long distances in order to access safe, legal medical care. States with the largest cumulative increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over 15 months included Illinois (28,665), Florida (15,155) and California (12,515).\par \par \uc2 In most cases, abortions rose in the states bordering states with total bans. But there were also significant increases in New Jersey, New York and Massachusetts, suggesting that some pregnant people are crossing multiple state lines for medical care.\par \par \uc2 The findings are consistent with research by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/goatsandsoda/2022/05/27/1099739656/do-restrictive-abortion-laws-actually-reduce-abortion-a-global-map-offers-insigh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 World Health Organization and the Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 , which found \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in countries with total bans like El Salvador and the Dominican Republic are more or less the same as in countries where the procedure is legally available. In other words, criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes it harder and more dangerous for people to obtain the procedure, but it does not stop them from accessing the medical care they need.\par \par \uc2 The report also reveals an uptick in telehealth or virtual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care as pregnant people and professionals look for innovative ways to provide urgent medical care. There were about 14,000 US telehealth abortions a month between July and September 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 triple the number before the supreme court decision and amounting to 16% of all abortions. This number includes virtual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care by bricks-and-mortar clinics and virtual-only providers, and includes patients living in states with total or partial \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans which have shield laws protecting telehealth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the post-Dobbs era, healthcare providers in some states are forging innovative new solutions to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Ushma Upadhyay, #WeCount co-chair and professor at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health program.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTelehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now a central pillar in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care landscape \u8211\'20\'13 and the continued availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care demands that we must ensure equitable access to this essential healthcare service.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The #WeCount data includes clinician-provided medication or procedural abortions completed by a licensed clinician within the formal healthcare system in a clinic, private medical office, hospital or virtual-only clinic. It does not reflect self-managed abortions, defined as ending a pregnancy outside the formal healthcare system using medications, herbs or other medicaments, or obtaining pills from friends or online without clinical assistance.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240228ek2s002bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
