\par\par \uc2 Television & radio\par \b \uc2 John Oliver on Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s IVF ruling: \u8216\'20\'18It is chaos\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff \par \uc2 615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2024\par 16:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last Week Tonight host speaks about \u8216\'20\'18seismic\u8217\'20\'19 decision defining embryos as \u8216\'20\'18children\u8217\'20\'19 and Republicans pushing for it\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/john-oliver"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Oliver}}}\uc2  has criticised the past week\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in Alabama that classified frozen embryos as children, referring to the situation as \u8220\'20\'1Cchaos\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On Last Week Tonight, he said that those within the state were \u8220\'20\'1Creeling after a major decision\u8221\'20\'1D which saw a court put those intending to use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/ivf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 IVF}}}\uc2  in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 He said that referring to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/20/alabama-supreme-court-frozen-embryos-children-ruling-ivf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 frozen embryos as people}}}\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Cwrong for a bunch of reasons \u8211\'20\'13 mainly if you freeze an embryo it\u8217\'20\'19s fine, if you freeze a person, you have some explaining to do\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In the US, about 2% of babies are born via IVF and the ruling has led to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/22/alabama-fertility-pause-ivf-embryo-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multiple fertility clinics pausing treatment}}}\uc2 , but Oliver said \u8220\'20\'1Cyou can\u8217\'20\'19t just hit pause and wait out a court case\u8221\'20\'1D, referring to it as \u8220\'20\'1Ca seismic decision\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It all goes back to the case of a patient who destroyed a number of frozen embryos in a clinic, which is now being sued for wrongful death, and while Oliver admitted that it\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8220\'20\'1Cgenuinely horrible\u8221\'20\'1D story it \u8220\'20\'1Cjust isn\u8217\'20\'19t murder\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cif anything, it sounds like the script for a pretty tasteless Mr Bean sequel but that is it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He continued: \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason clinics are pausing treatment right now is that nobody quite knows what it means for an embryo to be the legal equivalant of a person going forward. What happens if an embryo is stored improperly? What if they\u8217\'20\'19re, as inevitably happens, left over or destroyed in the implantation process? What about genetic testing, which can reduce the risk of miscarriage but does carry a slight risk of damaging embryos? Would that now be considered a wrongful death? It is chaos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other states are reportedly considering a similar ruling but \u8220\'20\'1Cnone of this should be that surprising\u8221\'20\'1D as \u8220\'20\'1Cthis ruling is a natural outgrowth of the concept of fetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Oliver spoke about the far-right groups who have been influencing politicians who are now scrambling to defend and explain the latest decision. He said that Republicans were in \u8220\'20\'1Ca tough spot right now trying to hold on to hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces while not alienating the majority of Americans\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  has come out in support of IVF yet has also been behind a push for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/16/trump-16-week-abortion-ban-exceptions-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban.}}}\par \par \uc2 If he wins this year\u8217\'20\'19s election then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  are allegedly hoping to use his presidency as a way to bring back the Comstock Act, an 1873 law that criminalises the shipping of anything aiding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s already been referenced by \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , leading Oliver to joke: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s old and it\u8217\'20\'19s there basically sums up \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It would mean that the decision, which would ban all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication being shipped, could be brought into law without going through Congress, an \u8220\'20\'1Calarming\u8221\'20\'1D eventuality.\par \par \uc2 But while allies have been pushing it behind closed doors, they are also keen for Trump and his public-facing cronies to avoid talking about it as it could deter voters as a result of its extremity. \u8220\'20\'1CWhenever they don\u8217\'20\'19t want you to talk about something, it\u8217\'20\'19s probably worth you knowing about it,\u8221\'20\'1D Oliver said.\par \par \uc2 It comes from Anthony Comstock, who was an anti-sex crusader and a chronic masturbator who called for a ban of obscene materials, including anything aiding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Oliver called it a \u8220\'20\'1Cwild law pushed for a deeply weird, dangerously horny man\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said that Republicans were now \u8220\'20\'1Cdesperately trying to distance themselves from extreme policies they have enabled\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240226ek2q00234\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
