\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18My embryos aren\u8217\'20\'19t safe here\u8217\'20\'19: US patients struggling with infertility scramble after Alabama IVF ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2024\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the wake of the state\u8217\'20\'19s ruling that embryos are \u8216\'20\'18extrauterine children\u8217\'20\'19, people in the US are terrified their state will be next\par \par \uc2 Tucker Legerski and his wife, Megan, have spent more than two years and tens of thousands of dollars trying to have a baby.\par \par \uc2 Married since 2021, the Alabama couple last year embarked on in-vitro fertilization (IVF). After they retrieved eggs, a specialist combined those eggs with sperm to create four quality embryos. Last fall, they successfully transferred one embryo, resulting in a pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 but eight weeks in, it ended in a miscarriage. The couple had planned to attempt another embryo transfer in the fall.\par \par \uc2 But now, in the wake of an Alabama supreme court decision that ruled that embryos are \u8220\'20\'1Cextrauterine children\u8221\'20\'1D, the Legerskis don\u8217\'20\'19t know what will happen to their hopes of having children in Alabama. At least three Alabama IVF providers, including the Legerskis\u8217\'20\'19, have paused their IVF operations, leaving the Legerskis\u8217\'20\'19 remaining three embryos stuck in icy storage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe three embryos that we have, that are frozen right now, that we can\u8217\'20\'19t use as of right now, are very important to us and our best hope,\u8221\'20\'1D Tucker Legerski said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know if we would be able to afford another egg retrieval and transfer or creating more embryos, because of the emotional and financial costs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Legerskis are far from the only prospective parents wondering what the future of IVF holds, both inside Alabama and beyond its borders. IVF patients and advocates in Alabama are rushing to figure out what the ruling and subsequent cessation of care means for their deeply time-sensitive plans. Outside Alabama, people are petrified that similar restrictions could soon surface in their state now that Alabama has blazed a trail.\par \par \uc2 For many people struggling with infertility, uncertainty and powerlessness are familiar agonies. But now, in addition to being at the mercy of biological and medical forces, they feel they\u8217\'20\'19re also being buffeted by politicians, jurists and rightwing activists determined to embed their belief that life begins when a sperm fertilizes an egg into every aspect of American life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe watched the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access get taken away slowly and we\u8217\'20\'19ve heard people say: \u8216\'20\'18You don\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about IVF, IVF is safe and fine.\u8217\'20\'19 And here is an exact story showing how IVF is not fine,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kristin Dillensnyder, an infertility coach who underwent IVF in Alabama and subsequently had a daughter.\par \par \uc2 Since the ruling, Dillensnyder said she\u8217\'20\'19s heard from clients in South Carolina, where she now lives, who are terrified that their state will be next. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re in a red state, it really just feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s just a matter of time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Tara Harding, a doctorate family nurse practitioner and fertility coach based in North Dakota, has also heard from clients from across the country since the ruling. They\u8217\'20\'19re no longer sure where to undergo IVF; they\u8217\'20\'19re afraid that their embryos will become trapped in a state that may limit the procedure. On Friday, \uc2 Resolve: The National Infertility Association\uc2  announced that nationwide embryo shipping services have indicated that they will stop transporting embryos in and out of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Harding previously travelled to Colorado to undergo IVF. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I have to go through IVF again, I will go back to Colorado, because I know my embryos are safe there,\u8221\'20\'1D Harding said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re not safe up here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Alabama ruling enshrines tenets of \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D into state law. The push for fetal personhood, which seeks to endow embryos and fetuses with full legal rights and protections, is a long-term goal of many in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, given that opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is predicated on the belief that life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 However, such protections can lead the alleged rights of embryos to clash even with the people who might be carrying them.\par \par \uc2 Alabama has long been a laboratory for fetal personhood measures. Between 1973 and 2022, when the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/19/steep-rise-criminalizing-pregnant-people-during-roe-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alabama law enforcement criminalized nearly 700 people}}}\uc2  for allegedly endangering \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn life\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a record number of cases. In 2018, Alabama became the first state in the country to enshrine a fetal personhood clause into its state constitution after voters supported a measure to recognize \u8220\'20\'1Cthe rights of the unborn child\u8221\'20\'1D, including the right to life.\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re in a red state, it really just feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s just a matter of time\par \par \uc2 Kristin Dillensnyder\par \par \uc2 Many within the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement oppose IVF, in part because embryos may be created that are not ultimately used during the complex process. But many IVF patients never realized a process to create families could land in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes\u8217\'20\'19 crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI never ever imagined that IVF would be questioned,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gabrielle Goidel, an Alabama woman who started using medications to prepare her body for egg retrieval on Friday, the same day as the supreme court decision.\par \par \uc2 Goidel and her husband, Spencer, have already confronted some of the consequences of overturning Roe. While the pair were living in Texas, which has banned almost all abortions, Goidel experienced three miscarriages. During her second miscarriage, she went to the hospital and confirmed that the pregnancy no longer had cardiac activity. Goidel wanted a D&C, a common procedure after a miscarriage. But a Texas doctor refused, Goidel said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe was very dismissive, said that was a complicated surgical procedure and he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t going to do it \u8211\'20\'13 and then asked if he could pray with us,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalled.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Goidel\u8217\'20\'19s provider said it would stop performing IVF. She and her husband, Spencer, rushed to hop on a plane to Texas, where they could continue the egg retrieval process.\par \par \uc2 When Roe was overturned, Spencer Goidel said: \u8220\'20\'1CThere was at least an ability to believe like: \u8216\'20\'18Okay, the side that is against Roe v Wade, the side that wanted Roe v Wade overturned, is just pro-family. That\u8217\'20\'19s what it is. They\u8217\'20\'19re just pro-family.\u8217\'20\'19 And then this happens and you go: \u8216\'20\'18Oh. Oh, so you\u8217\'20\'19re not pro-family at all, actually. This is religious fundamentalism.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A concurring opinion in the Alabama state supreme court case, written by the court\u8217\'20\'19s chief justice, repeatedly cited the Bible as justification for the court\u8217\'20\'19s reasoning. \uc2 Liberty\uc2  Counsel, a conservative Christian legal organization, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lc.org/newsroom/details/021924-alabama-supreme-court-rules-frozen-embryos-are-children-1?fbclid=IwAR2qqKvE_w5iVo7yBEPExKcrR0hfpNCWYS80tKkXiDG8L6LeOz3Ma0OPh8A"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has already used}}}\uc2  the Alabama supreme court ruling to argue in a Florida case that Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution, like Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s, protects \u8220\'20\'1Can unborn child\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But not all religious Christians agree with Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. Rodney Miller is the chairman of the board for Carrywell, a faith-based Alabama group that helps people navigate infertility, including through financial grants to people going through IVF. He says he and his wife relied on their faith to get them through their decade-long struggle with infertility, which culminated in their adoption of frozen embryos that his wife carried and that led to twin toddlers. Over the last week, amid the fallout from the ruling, the couple traveled to Tennessee to transfer embryos in the hopes of having more children.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s very disheartening to know that... Alabama seems to be going backwards\par \par \uc2 Rodney Miller\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere we are with the advances of medicine and science, discussing in-vitro fertilization and frozen embryos, and I think it is a beautiful thing that God has given us with the science and the medicine and the advancements,\u8221\'20\'1D Miller said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very disheartening to know that, despite all of that, Alabama seems to be going backwards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Both Democratic and Republican Alabama state legislators are now in the process of drafting legislation to protect IVF. Alabama governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2 , a Republican, has also said that she supports a solution that will shield the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But it is still unclear what will happen next or how soon change may come. Mallory Wear, the executive director of Carrywell, leads a support group for women dealing with infertility and who have had multiple miscarriages. The ruling has left them devastated, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been great to see people coming out and voicing their opinions and really bringing awareness to infertility and the IVF process,\u8221\'20\'1D Wear said. But, she added: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you are in the middle of it, every day counts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240224ek2o001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
