\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republican \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  arguments echoed in Alabama IVF case\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2024\par 22:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Arguments that led to Alabama supreme court ruling that embryos are \u8216\'20\'18extrauterine children\u8217\'20\'19 similar to Missouri senator\u8217\'20\'19s in 2013 case\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  arguments made in the recent controversial {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/23/fertility-doctors-reaction-alabama-embryo-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alabama supreme court decision}}}\uc2 , which led to the shut down of in vitro fertilization (IVF) in nearly half of the state\u8217\'20\'19s clinics, echo those made by the Republican US senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Missouri lawmaker made similar arguments in 2013 and when he worked on the legal team arguing the \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D case on contraception before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Alabama supreme court ruled embryos, which are created in the IVF process, are \u8220\'20\'1Cextrauterine children\u8221\'20\'1D in the eyes of the law. The decision thrust fertility treatments in the state into chaos, as clinics and attorneys said the practice of modern reproductive medicine in Alabama might no longer be legal.\par \par \uc2 The IVF process typically relies on the creation of multiple embryos, fertilized eggs of several hundred cells called blastocysts. Some of those embryos may be discarded during the IVF because of genetic testing, or may not survive the freeze-thaw process of cryogenic storage (used to create embryos for future IVF rounds).\par \par \uc2 The state supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision cast into doubt whether multiple stages of the IVF process were legal, as it appeared doctors could be held liable for \u8220\'20\'1Cwrongful death\u8221\'20\'1D if an embryo failed or was destroyed during routine treatment.\par \par \uc2 The decision has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/23/fertility-doctors-reaction-alabama-embryo-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infuriated doctors across the country}}}\uc2  and led to confusion that has stopped IVF at three of the state\u8217\'20\'19s eight fertility clinics. In 2021, the state\u8217\'20\'19s two larger programs at the University of Alabama and Alabama Fertility Specialists together performed nearly 900 IVF cycles resulting in 273 pregnancies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nccd.cdc.gov/drh_art/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=DRH_ART.ClinicInfo&ClinicId=325&ShowNational=0&islCycleTypes=T002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to data}}}\uc2  submitted to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  (CDC).\par \par \uc2 Hawley took office in 2019, unseating the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2024/02/23/erin-hawley-abortion-pill-supreme-court-00142493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrat Claire McCaskill}}}\uc2 . He is known as a very conservative Christian lawmaker, who notably {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2024/01/06/jan-6-josh-hawley-photo-q-a-00134017"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raised his fist}}}\uc2  in support of a pro-Trump mob during the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Erin Hawley, the senator\u8217\'20\'19s wife, has become a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2024/02/23/erin-hawley-abortion-pill-supreme-court-00142493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative legal force}}}\uc2 , and was on the team of attorneys who helped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the nearly 50-year-old precedent that once protected the right to terminate a pregnancy, and which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jul/25/states-using-fetal-personhood-laws-to-criminalize-mothers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paved the way}}}\uc2  for Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s recent court decision.\par \par \uc2 In 2013, Hawley authored an opinion article in Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s Springfield News-Leader that criticized a federal decision requiring employers to provide access to contraception through health insurance. In the article, Hawley said the morning-after pill, \u8220\'20\'1Cmay induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by preventing implantation of a fertilized egg\u8221\'20\'1D, in effect the same logic accepted by justices at the Alabama supreme court that led to shutdowns of IVF.\par \par \uc2 Doctors do not consider a person pregnant until both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/11585-conception"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fertilization and implantation}}}\uc2  occur. For that reason, contraception is not considered \u8220\'20\'1Cabortifacient\u8221\'20\'1D, because there is no pregnancy. In fact, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/12/23/health/morning-after-pills-abortion-fda.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the morning-after pill works}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cbefore release of an egg from the ovary\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Separately, Hawley served as a member of the legal team that argued the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2014/jun/30/supreme-court-employers-religious-objections-contraception"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hobby Lobby case}}}\uc2  before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  from 2011 to 2015. In that case, Hawley and others on the team argued that private companies should not be required to cover birth control methods that were \u8220\'20\'1Cmore like abortifacients\u8221\'20\'1D because, they alleged, the contraception prevented an embryo from implanting in the uterus wall.\par \par \uc2 Religious-affiliated, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attorneys are already attempting to use the Alabama supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to advance a similar argument in Florida. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lc.org/newsroom/details/021924-alabama-supreme-court-rules-frozen-embryos-are-children-1?fbclid=IwAR2qqKvE_w5iVo7yBEPExKcrR0hfpNCWYS80tKkXiDG8L6LeOz3Ma0OPh8A"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Liberty Counsel}}}\uc2  said in a statement that it used Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s decision as a precedent to argue against a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/florida-abortion-election-2024-17540f14ed2880f03a2bb14274b03ed3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed ballot initiative}}}\uc2  in the state. The ballot initiative asks voters to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240223ek2n002uz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
