\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  centers raked in $1.4bn in year Roe fell, including federal money\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2024\par 12:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: memo shows anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers received at least $344m in government money in 2022\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities raked in at least $1.4bn in revenue in the 2022 fiscal year, the year Roe v Wade fell \u8211\'20\'13 a staggering haul that includes at least $344m in government money, according to a memo analyzing the centers\u8217\'20\'19 tax documents that was compiled by a pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group and shared exclusively with the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 These facilities, frequently known as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers or crisis pregnancy centers, aim to convince people to keep their pregnancies. But in the aftermath of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has framed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers as a key source of aid for desperate women who have lost the legal right to end their pregnancies and been left with little choice but to give birth.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say, the centers need an influx of government cash.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose are the centers that states rely on to assist expecting moms and dads,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike Johnson, the speaker of the \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2 , told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/jan/19/march-for-life-anti-abortion-washington-dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  protesters at the March for Life}}}\uc2  in January. The Louisiana Republican praised the centers for providing \u8220\'20\'1Cthe important material support that expecting and first-time mothers get from these centers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, under Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s leadership, the House passed a bill that would block the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  from restricting funding for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers. State governments are also in the midst of sending vast sums of taxpayer dollars to programs that support anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers. Since the demolition of Roe, at least 16 states have agreed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/28/anti-abortion-pregnancy-crisis-centers-taxpayer-money-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 send more than $250m}}}\uc2  towards \u8220\'20\'1Calternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D programs in 2023 through 2025. Those programs funnel money towards anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers, maternity homes and assorted other initiatives meant to dissuade people from abortions.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say, much of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy center industry remains shrouded in mystery \u8211\'20\'13 including their finances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStewards of both taxpayer and charitable funds should insist on a real impact analysis of the industry, whether investments that are being made are achieving their desired outcomes and are cost-effective,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jenifer McKenna, the crisis pregnancy center program director at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductivehealthfreedom.us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reproductive Health and Freedom Watch}}}\uc2 , the group behind the analysis of tax documents. \u8220\'20\'1CTaxpayers deserve performance standards and hard metrics for use of their dollars on these centers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The analysis by Reproductive Health and \uc2 Freedom Watch\uc2  examined 990 tax documents, which most US tax-exempt organizations must file annually, from 1,719 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers in fiscal year 2019 and from 1,469 in fiscal year 2022. The analysis confirms that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy center industry is growing: while the centers\u8217\'20\'19 revenue in 2022 exceeds $1.4bn, it was closer to $1.03bn in 2019, even though more centers were included in the earlier analysis.\par \par \uc2 Centers reported receiving hundreds of millions of dollars in donations from private funders between 2018 and 2022. While only a relatively small fraction of the centers reported receiving grants from state and federal governments in both 2022 and 2019, that number is on the rise, according to the Reproductive Health and \uc2 Freedom Watch\uc2  analysis memo. In 2022, the centers said they received $344m in such grants, but they received less than $97m in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Just 21 centers identified the federal grants that they received in 2022, the analysis found. Those grants included the Fema-funded Emergency Food and Shelter Program, which is primarily meant for organizations that alleviate hunger and homelessness, and the Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, a program for low-income families.\par \par \uc2 This accounting does not represent the full financial picture of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy center industry. More than 2,500 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers are believed to dot the United States \u8211\'20\'13 a number that far outstrips the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18What did they do with all that money?\par \par \uc2 \u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Much of the modern, publicly available information on anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers comes from one of their biggest cheerleaders: the Charlotte Lozier Institute, which assembles reports on the industry and operates as an arm of Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America, one of the top anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations in the United States.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the Charlotte Lozier Institute said that 2,700 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers provided consulting services to 967,251 new clients on-site. In 2022, the Institute said 2,750 centers provided consulting services for 974,965 new clients \u8211\'20\'13 an increase of 0.08%.\par \par \uc2 Even though the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe at the halfway point of 2022, it did not appear to result in a crush of new clients \u8211\'20\'13 despite anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates\u8217\'20\'19 argument that the pregnancy centers need an infusion of funding to handle post-Roe clients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe new client numbers alone don\u8217\'20\'19t fully tell the story,\u8221\'20\'1D a bevy of Charlotte Lozier Institute scholars \u8211\'20\'13 Moira Gaul, Jeanneane Maxon and Michael J New \u8211\'20\'13 said in an email to the Guardian, adding that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  centers and groups have seen an increase in violence following the fall of Roe. (The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that remain post-Roe have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prochoice.org/violence-against-abortion-providers-continues-to-rise-following-roe-reversal-new-report-finds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced rising violence}}}\uc2 . That has not stoppered the demand for their services, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/24/us-abortion-rates-post-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rates of abortions have risen}}}\uc2  since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise.)\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers are seeing a dramatic rise in calls for certain kinds of help. Data from the Charlotte Lozier Institute reports show that centers handed out 64% more diapers, 52% more baby clothing and 43% more wipes in 2022, compared to 2019. Demand for new car seats and strollers also increased by about a third.\par \par \uc2 All of these items would presumably go to new parents. The fall of Roe led to an estimated 32,000 more births, particularly among young women and women of color, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.iza.org/dp16608.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 2023 analysis found}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The total dollar value of these goods and services was about $358m, according to the Charlotte Lozier Institute report. Reproductive Health and \uc2 Freedom Watch\uc2  found that the roughly 1,500 centers included in the group\u8217\'20\'19s 2022 analysis reported expenses of more than $1.2bn on their 990 tax documents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey took in \u8211\'20\'13 according to the 990s \u8211\'20\'13 $1.4bn, and they spent $1.2bn on expenditures,\u8221\'20\'1D McKenna said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat did they do with all that money? There\u8217\'20\'19s so many questions begged by their own reporting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Charlotte Lozier scholars said there were other expenses not listed in the report, such as maternity clothing, property-related payments, fundraising, marketing and staff salaries. Data from their report indicates that, between 2019 and 2022, the number of volunteers who work at the centers fell while the number of paid staffers rose. (Volunteers still make up the overwhelming bulk of the workforce.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost non-profits prefer to use staff when possible. Centers are attracting more professionals that desire to help women,\u8221\'20\'1D the scholars said. \u8220\'20\'1CMany centers are now in a place where they can pay them so they are less reliant on volunteers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The institute\u8217\'20\'19s report on anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers in 2022 is a very different document to the reports that it released to cover the centers in 2019 and 2017. The earlier reports span dozens of pages; the 2022 report is only four. A longer report is now in the works, the Charlotte Lozier scholars said, which will include information about government funding of centers.\par \par \uc2 A lack of regulation\par \par \uc2 Although anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers may appear to be local mom-and-pop organizations, in reality many are affiliated with national organizations like NIFLA, Care Net and Heartbeat International. These centers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/02/kentucky-crisis-pregnancy-center-anti-abortion-malpractices"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thrive in a kind of regulatory dead zone}}}\uc2 , providing medical services like ultrasounds. But many are not licensed as medical facilities, leaving them unencumbered by the rules or oversight imposed on typical medical providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are changing their names a lot and changing their names in ways like including \u8216\'20\'18clinic\u8217\'20\'19 or \u8216\'20\'18medical\u8217\'20\'19 or \u8216\'20\'18healthcare\u8217\'20\'19 into their names and dropping things like \u8216\'20\'18Care Net\u8217\'20\'19 and other types of wording that might instantly identify them as a CPC,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andrea Swartzendruber, an associate professor at the University of Georgia College of Public Health who tracks anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 These centers, she said, are \u8220\'20\'1Cchanging their names in ways that make them seem more like medical clinics\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Charlotte Lozier Institute scholars said \u8220\'20\'1Ccalls for governmental regulation are nothing new\u8221\'20\'1D post-Roe and that \u8220\'20\'1Csuch efforts have been ongoing for decades\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have been found to be politically motivated and have been largely unsuccessful,\u8221\'20\'1D the scholars said. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities are in need of far greater government regulation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers\u8217\'20\'19 taxes can also be deeply intricate. The analysis by Reproductive Health and \uc2 Freedom Watch\uc2  found that the centers used a variety of tax codes to describe themselves, frequently describing themselves as organizations that provide \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily services\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive healthcare\u8221\'20\'1D. They were sometimes listed as organizations that work to outlaw abortions, or as explicitly Christian, religious organizations.\par \par \uc2 The National Committee for Responsive Philanthropy, a charity watchdog group, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://archive.ncrp.org/initiatives/movement-investment-project/our-active-movement-areas/reproductive-access-gendered-violence-movement/abortion-roadmap-intro/deceptive-cpc-track"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has previously found that many centers}}}\uc2  share tax identification numbers with much larger organizations that do multiple kinds of charity work, such as non-profits run by Catholic dioceses. By sharing numbers, these organizations are effectively collapsed into one legal and tax entity, the committee said.\par \par \uc2 The Charlotte Lozier Institute scholars told the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis is not our understanding at all\u8221\'20\'1D. NIFLA, Care Net and Heartbeat International do not share tax identification numbers with affiliated centers, they said.\par \par \uc2 Just because these particular groups do not share tax identification numbers does not preclude centers from sharing them with other organizations. For example, Care Net is affiliated with a string of Florida pregnancy centers \u8211\'20\'13 which, rather than sharing Care Net\u8217\'20\'19s tax ID, are instead listed on tax documents for a wide-ranging charity run by a local Catholic diocese.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers tend to be faith-based. Given the industry\u8217\'20\'19s religious bent, courts have proven reluctant to restrict centers in order to avoid treading on their free speech rights.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled to toss a California law that would have forced centers to disclose whether they were a licensed medical provider. Then, last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/22/abortion-pill-reversal-ban-progesterone-judge-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a federal judge in Colorado paused a law}}}\uc2  that would have banned \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal\u8221\'20\'1D, an unproven drug protocol that aims to halt abortions and is often offered by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers. (The first randomized, controlled clinical study to try to study the \u8220\'20\'1Creversal\u8221\'20\'1D protocol\u8217\'20\'19s effectiveness suddenly stopped in 2019, after three of its participants went to the hospital hemorrhaging blood.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMore regulation could lead to better reporting, which would also then help with reducing all of these risks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Teneille Brown, a University of Utah College of Law professor who studies anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers. \u8220\'20\'1CThen the consumers could get some sense of like, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, this clinic has had a bunch of violations,\u8217\'20\'19 and if there were regulation, they could actually even shut them down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240214ek2e001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
