\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The threat isn\u8217\'20\'19t over\u8217\'20\'19: the expert arguing to the supreme court Trump is an insurrectionist\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,801 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2024\par 19:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jill Habig has filed an amicus brief saying the 14th amendment applies to Trump \u8211\'20\'13 and the brief gives the court\u8217\'20\'19s originalists a taste of their own medicine\par \par \uc2 When {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.berkeley.edu/our-faculty/faculty-profiles/jill-habig/#tab_profile"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jill Habig}}}\uc2  had an office down the hall from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in California, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was US president, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a constitutional right and January 6 was just another date on the calendar. A lot has happened since then.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday Habig, now president of the non-profit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chatham-strategies-dot-yamm-track.appspot.com/2qkyB1mtlY0nX0rzKbZBYCCvJPRa5YF485TJzD0vHD5eQcBRWjQGDyv8TZAxuB4Onmh5hjbKbhmyo8PRMCFKI2qbSHpalhx25uXM3NZlEG_BFIpA7jsrr7gnLgjGRpFx4zFrPqe_hNysrDWa9uGx-4lohDbRxaDiv3DNpWnS2cB6NCzxLC8gv0k03dJsacRR9c6E"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public Rights Project}}}\uc2  (PRP), hopes her arguments will persuade the supreme court that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is an insurrectionist who should be disqualified from the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Habig has filed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chatham-strategies-dot-yamm-track.appspot.com/23KzENcoEVnnPsFC78_aKU-2cAJycc2lM0we36hfQJ_-ScBRWjQFBnCcG2IMDx_gt94BQCW5zKd2YfGbU2dD5Buh75AUj-_6hHDqL9w_k8sEAHDImocTLJ0IpWQplvvRMbmbbDBqm6Txd-qK9aARTC9WXRA006ro7X1x0argialTKtSKy97NVunYmDf3RPRKnwgrul9SyBCvn8naPTr9eLluCjuBPn17YNxaQQjyUMMx8fhW7-SaeFjeSZFlL0t9vYyLVHvLlKIlp1em9PCcFGUxDrViaaNPox6H_"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amicus brief}}}\uc2  on behalf of historians contending that section 3 of the 14th amendment to the constitution, which bars people who \u8220\'20\'1Cengaged in insurrection\u8221\'20\'1D from holding public office, applies to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s role in the 6 January 2021 attack on the US Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The brief gives the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s originalists, who believe the constitution should be interpreted as it would have been in the era it was written, a taste of their own medicine. Conservative justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  are self-declared originalists while \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  has described himself as a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thomasaquinas.edu/news/justice-alito-discusses-faith-originalism-tac-town-hall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 practical originalist}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur goal was to bring an originalist historical perspective to the supreme court as it considered the meaning of section 3 of the 14th amendment,\u8221\'20\'1D Habig, a former special counsel to then California attorney general Harris, says by phone from Oakland. \u8220\'20\'1CThe point we make with our historian colleagues is that the history of section 3 is actually very clear. It demonstrates that section 3 was intended to automatically disqualify insurrectionists.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The amicus brief, led by historians {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/01/31/1228067671/does-history-support-removing-donald-trump-from-the-presidential-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jill Lepore of Harvard and David Blight of Yale}}}\uc2 , cites debates from the time in which senators made clear that their view that the provision that would not only apply for former Confederates but to the leaders of rebellions yet to come.\par \par \uc2 Habig adds: \u8220\'20\'1CIt was intended to apply not only to the civil war but also to future insurrections and it bars anyone who has betrayed an oath to uphold the constitution from becoming president of the United States.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will hear arguments on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/19/trump-colorado-presidential-ballot-disqualified-14th-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Colorado case}}}\uc2  in which Trump was stricken from the ballot; a decision in Maine is on hold. Other states have ruled in favor of keeping Trump on the ballot. The flurry of decisions have prompted debate over whether Trump can be fairly considered to have committed insurrection even though he has not been found guilty in a court of law \u8211\'20\'13 at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 Habig, who founded the PRP in 2017, says yes. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear historically that there was no requirement of a conviction or even of charges, that the framers intended section 3 to be self-executing. The brief goes through a number of examples of people who had taken part in the secession and been on the Confederate side actually petitioning Congress for exceptions. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of evidence that it was self-executing. There was no need for a particular conviction.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She adds: \u8220\'20\'1CThe evidence that we have seen and heard and watched with our own eyes over the last few years has made it quite clear that President Trump lost an election in 2020 and has spent the months and years since then trying to overturn the results of that election in a variety of ways, including people marching to the Capitol and invading the Capitol.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to argue with a straight face that these activities don\u8217\'20\'19t qualify for section 3\par \par \uc2 Jill Habig\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Blight has pointed out that the US Capitol was never breached during the civil war but was on January 6. Habig comments: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to argue with a straight face that these activities don\u8217\'20\'19t qualify for section 3 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, there are plenty of Republicans, Democrats and neutrals who warn that the 14th amendment drive is politically counterproductive, fueling a Trumpian narrative that state institutions are out to stop him and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the true threat to democracy. Let the people decide at the ballot box in November, they say.\par \par \uc2 Habig counters: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important to note that the American people did decide in 2020. We had a political process and then we had a president of the United States who attempted to overturn that political process. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Spectacular as it was, the January 6 riot did not occur in a vacuum. Habig and her work at the PRP place it in a wider context of a growing movement to harass and threaten election officials and to interfere with the administration of elections. She perceives a direct line between Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D and threats to democracy across the country today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRegardless of this particular case, the threat isn\u8217\'20\'19t over. It\u8217\'20\'19s actually intensifying. We\u8217\'20\'19re just seeing an array of efforts to rig the rules of the game against our democracy and it\u8217\'20\'19s part of why we\u8217\'20\'19re investing a lot of resources into protecting election officials this cycle, and to litigating and advancing voting rights and free and fair elections this year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 How did America get here? A turning point was the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s 5-4 decision in 2013 to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jun/25/shelby-county-anniversary-voting-rights-act-consequences"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike down a formula}}}\uc2  at the heart of the Voting Rights Act, so that voters who are discriminated against now bear the burden of proving they are disenfranchised. Since then states have engaged in a barrage of gerrymandering \u8211\'20\'13 manipulating district boundaries so as to favor one party \u8211\'20\'13 and voter suppression.\par \par \uc2 Habig reflects: \u8220\'20\'1CThe gutting of the Voting Rights Act by the supreme court left states to themselves to rewrite the rules of the game in a variety of ways that disenfranchised voters and continued to rig maps against their systems and fair representation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen the supreme court take itself out of the game of protecting other fundamental rights like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and throw that back into the states. What that\u8217\'20\'19s creating is a lot of volatility at the state and local level as officials try to rewrite the rules or pick up the pieces and protect their constituents\u8217\'20\'19 rights. What we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to do is help state and local officials across the country use the power that they have to fight back and advance civil rights in all the ways that they can. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The PRP is building a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.publicrightsproject.org/election-protection-litigation-hub-overview?utm_source=Email&utm_medium=link&utm_campaign=Presidents_Update_2023_Nov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rapid response hub}}}\uc2  to provide legal support for 200 election officials to combat harassment and intimidation and targeting election deniers. It is pursuing litigation against gerrymandering, the disqualification of legitimate ballots and state officials who try to prevent voters weighing in on ballot measures to advance \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an all-out effort to make sure that we don\u8217\'20\'19t have death by a thousand cuts for our democracy this year,\u8221\'20\'1D Habig says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are potentially less likely to see one central threat like we did on January 6 or even in the 2020 election. We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen some of the larger counties like Maricopa county, Arizona, Philadelphia, Detroit et cetera, who have been targets in the past, have more resources to fight back.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re most concerned about is the soft underbelly of our democracy, which is the smaller, less-resourced jurisdictions that just don\u8217\'20\'19t have all of the capacity they need to push back against this harassment and intimidation. Because of our decentralised system, election deniers who are intent on disrupting our elections and disrupting the outcome of our election don\u8217\'20\'19t have to mount a huge effort in one place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey can pick apart jurisdiction by jurisdiction, invalidate 250 ballots here, and a thousand ballots there and 500 there, challenge absentee ballots, disrupt targeted polling places and that in the aggregate can actually change election results, sow disillusionment and distrust in our system and have the same or even worse aggregate outcome in terms of undermining the integrity of our election. That\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re mobilising to prevent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There was no greater measure of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/jun/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-ruling-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s ailing democracy}}}\uc2  than the 2022 decision to overturn Roe v Wade, the ruling that in effect made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal nationwide, by supreme court justices appointed by presidents who lost the national popular vote. But since then, in a series of ballot measures in individual states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have prevailed.\par \par \uc2 Habig reflects: \u8220\'20\'1CEvery single time that has been put to voters, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have won. As a result, we\u8217\'20\'19re actually starting to see a lot of overlap between the reproductive rights fight and the democracy fight because this battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fuelling additional efforts to break the rules and prevent voters from having a meaningful say in their rights. We\u8217\'20\'19re mobilising on both fronts because the future of both is interconnected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 PRP says it has worked {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/opinion/judiciary/3787043-the-abortion-battle-has-moved-to-your-local-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with local elected officials}}}\uc2  to provide legal guidance and filed dozens of amicus briefs in key reproductive rights cases, secured legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 6.5 million people. Habig explains: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re working with state and local officials to overturn criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans at the state level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re working to poke holes in existing criminal bans when there\u8217\'20\'19s not a path to overturn them right away. Then we\u8217\'20\'19re working to hold crisis pregnancy centers accountable for deception of women and patients; these are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  centers that masquerade as health clinics that provide comprehensive healthcare. We\u8217\'20\'19re looking at this multi-pronged approach state by state and across the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Habig, a political strategist who was deputy campaign manager for Harris\u8217\'20\'19s first Senate election campaign in 2016, has no doubt that democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will play a big part in the November election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI appreciate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2024/01/05/remarks-by-president-biden-on-the-third-anniversary-of-the-january-6th-attack-and-defending-the-sacred-cause-of-american-democracy-blue-bell-pa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s clarity}}}\uc2  on democracy and the constitution and his leadership on the issue. I do think it\u8217\'20\'19s important for people to understand what democracy means and for their real lives. It can sound abstract sometimes and like an academic debate but bringing it down to the level of, do you have autonomy over your future and your community, do you have autonomy over your own body, is important for people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She adds: \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in cases when we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about the fundamental right to vote, people get that. When we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about their autonomy, they get it. When they\u8217\'20\'19re talking about their dignity in the workplace, people get that and feel that on a visceral level. It\u8217\'20\'19s important that we work to build a democracy that actually delivers so that people can feel the value of it in their daily lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240205ek25001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
